      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</head>
        <p>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</p>
        <p>'There are more women than men in the world." Tom: "Yes; but there are enougk women who want to be men to balance matters.' Flltsr STATESMAN: "I can truly say that n man ever sought to bribe me." Second Ditto "Oh, well, I wouldn't be discouraged. Your luck may change some time or other." "DEAR father, we are all well and happy. The baby has grown ever so much and has a great deal more sense than he used to have. Hoping the same of von, I remain your daughter Molly.'</p>
        <p>LirrL .6E-rir (in the country) : " Grandpapa, yon must have to keep an awful lot of policemen out here." Grandpaps.: "Why, Beth ? " "Oh, there's such a lot of grass to keep off of !" FIRST LADY (off for a journey): "I hope we've got the right train." Second Lady : "I asked seventeen porters and ninety-three passengers if this train went to Manchester, and every one said Yes,' so I think we're all right." • A COUNTRY newspaper correspondent recently sent this cheerful bit of news to his paper : "Brooks is at last provided with a nice hearse, and or citizens can now be conveyed to thyir graves in comfort. This is something that has long been needed here."</p>
        <p>"BuT, Emma how can you prefer the plain and shabbily-dressed Julius to my elegant an hand- some brother?" "That is quite simple; your brother is in love with himself, and Julius with me." PLAUSIBLE. —Little Johnny : "Aunt Julia, what makes those funny spots on your face?" Aunt Julia (who is very freckled): " I believe it's because I have so much iron in my blood; it is only when I have been out in wet weather, though, that they are noticeable." Little Johnny: "Oh, yes; I know. You got out in the weather and the iron in your blood gets rusted." AN ELABORATE TEST. —Mother (suddenly com- ing upon her daughter as she is terminating a love-letter with these words: "I take you at your word; I love you, and will continue to love you while life shall last ! ") : " What does this mean, Amelia?" Emancipated School-girl: "Nothing, mamma. I was only trying a pen ; " "WE have a lovely whist club," she said, art- lessly. "We made a rule that any girl who spoke should pay a penny into the treasury for every word she uttered." "Quite ingenious." " Yes ; but I don't belong to it any more" "Why not?" "Pa says lie can't afford it." MEDICAL A D VICE. —" You see your wife ought to take a great deal more exercise." "But, doctor, what am Ito do? She never will go out." "Oh ! that is very easily remedied. Give her some money and tell her to go a- shopping." HEROIC REMEDY. —Distressed Young Mother (with crying baby in railway carriage) : "Dear, dear! I don't know whatever to do with this child I" Kind and Thoughtful Bachelor (on the opposite seat) : " Shall I open the window for you, madam?"</p>
        <p>Nor A GOOD BARGAIN. —The eyes of the suitor filled with tears. "Sir," he protested, "I would give my very life for your daughter." Her father started violently, "Young man," he thundered, "do I look as if I might be easily cheated in a bargain?" Before his gaze the youth averted his face, abashed. AT THE SODA FOUNTAIN. - Clerk: "What flavour?" Bridget (aside to Pat) : " Phat do he mean, Pat?" Pat: "Whist, Biddy, don't show yer igerance ; they has fruit flavours in the soda. I'll be havin' strawberry in mine." Bridget: "Oh, yis, Oi see, fruit flavours; Oi'll have onion in mine." - -</p>
        <p>"How did you come to hit the plaintiff ? " said the counsel io the prisoner. "Come to hit 'im, is it? 'Why, man, dear, your honour, I wasn't two fut from him, and I couldn't have missed hittin' him if I had been cross-eyed." CANDID.—Doctor : "Your wife is in a very critical state, and I should recommend you to call in some specialist to consult on the case." Husband: "There, you see doctor, I was right again ! I told my wife long ago she ought to get proper medical advice, but she always thought you might be offended 1" NEWWIFE "To-morrow is your birthday, darling, and I am going to stop at the jeweller's and buy you a present." Her Hubby: "Get something cheap, pet; I haven't paid him for my Christmas present yet." WATcHrut MOTHER : "Have you complied with my wishes and informed Mr. Busser that he can have but one kiss when he says good night!" Dutiful Daughter: "Yes, ma." "And how does it affect him?' "He says it much earlier and oftener than he used to." RIGGLE : "What did Miss Dawson say when you broke off your engagement with her ? " Twaggle "She didn't say anything; she was speechless." Riggle "Good gracious ! What a wife you've missed ! " INQIRING PERSON: "What time did the hotel catch fire?" Fireman: "Midnight." Inquiring Person: "Everybody got out safe!" Fireman: "All except the night watchmen. They couldn't wake him up in time." "You have a lively set of clerks," he said to the proprietor of the establishment. "It must be pleasant and profitable to have employes so full of energy and enthusiasm." "Yes,' responded the proprietor; "we close early to-day, and they are getting ready to go home." LITTLE EDITH had just been to church for the first time. "And what did you think of it?" asked her mother. "I didn't like the organ very well," she replied. "Why not?" "'Tense there wasn't any monkey with it 1" A THOROCH BUSINESS MAN.—Optician (to his new shopman) : "When you send out the price lists, write the addresses as small as possible so that the customers may see that it is time they began to wear glasses." "JOHN," called out Mrs. Billus, "are you ready to put up those new curtain fixtures ? ' "I am beginning to put them up now, Maria," was the response that came, in a metallic tone of voice, from the parlour. "Children," said Mrs. Billus, with nervous haste, "run out and play!" "I SUPPOSE you know," said the indulgent parent to the anxious suitor, "that my daughter -has been reared in the lap of luxury?" "Yes, sir, I know that. Still, lam glad you realise the importance of making us a liberal allowance to live upon." UNCLE: "When you find that you've made a mistake, start again right where you were before and try to do better." Nephew: "Thank you, sir. I've made a mistake of spending that five- pound note you gave me two weeks ago. Please give me another, so I can start where I was before." RECTOR'S WIFE : "You ought to avoid even the appearance of evil. Do you yourself think the girls who dance are right?' Belle of the Parish: 4' They must be. I know the girls who don't dance are always left." A CAREFUL FATHER.—"Look here," said the parent to the school teacher' "I see that one of the lines in my boy's copybook is 'Less haste, more speed.'" Yes." "And here's another that reads, The longest way iound is the shortest way home.'" "Yes." "Well, I want it stopped. I don't want those out-of-date proverbs festooned around his intellect. I'm educating him for business." LICKED INTO FITS.—" I can tell you something that you haven't got," said a German to an American. "We have built a church so long that it takes half an hour to walk from the porch to the pulpit." "That is nothing !" replied the Yankee. "We have a church where a little boy is carried to his baptism through the east porch, and at the west porch he walks out h.% i 4 minister with his second wife." UNDRESSING MADE EASY.—A gentleman who, near the close of the last century, accompanied Baron Von Swieton nu a visit to the old Duke of Sachsen-Hidburghausen relates the following in his memoirs: "The Duke regularly goes to bed every night at eight o'clock. When leaving the sitting. room to proceed to his rattier distant sleeping apartment, he has a number of his valets posted ali along the line of route. One of them takes possession of his wig, the second divests him of his coat, and so on, until, by the time he has reached the goal, he is ready bo slip into bed with- ul more ado."</p>
