      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>CAP " CHAT.</head>
        <p>CAP " CHAT.</p>
        <p>It is reported that Louise Michel is so seriously ill that her recovery is almost despaired of. It is probable that Louise Michel has never quite got over her terrible sufferings as a convict after the suppression of the Commune. It is certain that the experiences to which she was then subjected made a permanent, moral impression upon her, largely changing the current of her nature. Those who know her only as an Anarchist of the most violent type would be surprised to discover that she is personally a quiet, kindly woman, with a homely countenance and a soft voice. The milk of human kindness in her case was certainly turned sour by treatment which was, at the time, believed to be necessary by the French Government, but which sowed the seeds of not a little of contemporary French Anarchy.</p>
        <p>The determination of the Opposition to delay the progress of the Welsh Disesiablishment, Bill became evident on Monday long before Mr. Asquith rose. The discussion on the Glasgow Police Bill at the beginning of the sitting would have been confined within much narrower limits had it not been for the fact that the prolongation of the debate ren- dered it easier to carry over the discussion of the Welsh bill to another sitting of the Rouse. The same tactics are, I am told, to be steadily pursued throughout. Anyhow, there is very little doubt indeed that if the plans which are being laid for the Committee stage are carried out strong measures will have to be adopted to prevent the bill from dragging along during the greater part of the ses- sion. It must be said that there is more substance in the opposition to this measure than there was, say,in the obstructive policy adopted on the Budget last year. The Church party are bitterly opposed to the principle and details of the bill. and the spirit of the opposition which the measure will have to encounter from the members who are in -earnest in the matter is to be found much more in Sir hi. Hicks-Bea?,ll's angry speech on Monday night than in the line taken by Mr. Chamberlain or any of his 1 friends.</p>
        <p>--- Instead of hearing any news of arrests of dis- orderly and savage Turkish officials or soldiers, we learn of the arrest ot twenty-five Armenians at, Constantinople for some alleged acts which are not specified. We do not understand that this action on the part of the Turkish Government has produced a painful impression. The Porte seems to be en- tirely uninfluenced by the severe criticism of the outside world. Further allegations from Armenia represent the reign of outrage and violence as still continuing in the town of Tchernich-Ghazak. These alleged facts iurlicate still more clearly the absolute need of that thorough investigation which the Porto evidently foars,and which its officials are not likely to make.</p>
        <p>--- Mr. Clement Higgins has definitely taken his seat on the Opposition aide of the House, and regularly puts his card there. He has for all intents and pur- poseagone bodily over to the Conservative party. It is not unlikely nat a movement among Norfolk Liberals to drive him into resignation will be heard off before long. It is pointed out that his conduct i, quite unique. Even Sir William Marriott had the , outward decency to go through the form of contest- ' ing his seat us an independent before going over openly to the Tories.</p>
        <p>--- Liberals and municipal reformers will not be deceived by statements put forward on behalf of the Sporting League to the effect that it has nothing to do with politics or municipal affairs. In fact, it will be worked in the interests of Tories and re- actionaries, and will be a sort of male branch of the Primrose League. Called into activity, if not into actual being,iu order to protect ready-money book- makers and the 'Empire promenade, it has sine e I ostentatiously taken under its protection such sports as cricket and rowing, which need neither protection nor patronage. According to Mr. James Lowther, the Sporting League will also defend whippet racing. This is a low and unhealthy form of sport, which not only involves much gambling, but also a great deal of cheating and fraud. Accord- ing to the doctrines of the Sporting League, although you agree with a Parliamentary candidate on such vastly important questions as Home Rule. Welsh Disesiablishment,the local veto, the abolition of the House of Lords, and one man one vote, if he has happened to have uttered a few—perhaps in- considerate—words against horse-racing, you must vote against him and' use all your influence on behalf of the candidate with whom you disagree on all the weightier matters of politics. it is said that the object of the Sporting League is to resist the faddists, but its own objects seem to some people to be the very insanity ot faddism.</p>
        <p>The next best thing to the examination of Mr Keir Hardie that the Committee on the Unemployed could do would be to put Mr. Burns, who has by his cross-examination done his best to destroy Mr. ardie's position, also in the witness-box. Such a question as that put by Mr. Burns, why Battersea and Birmingham should be taxed to assist West liam, is directed against the imperial grant which Mr. Hardie urges the Government to make. If Mr. Burns's suggestion is that each district should Lear the total cost of its own unemployed population,i would be well to know that that is his view. But as all large towns—as London certainly is—are swarming with agricultural labourers who have been driven from the land, Mr. Burns's view would amount to this, that Liverpool, London, and such places should tax themselves to give work to the agricultural refugees. This is simply making the towns tax themselves and allowing the lancilor,lB to escape; and when such a good Tory as Mr. Jackson compliments Mr. Burns on his line of cross-examina- tion, the inference is that the latter either has no proposals worth listening to tl make, or that he has allowed his difference with Mr. Hardie to warp his views and lead him into a line of examination detrimental to the unemployed and to his own reputation. The question before the committee, when stripped of all verbiage, is simply this—Are the unemployed to be helped, or are they to starve ?</p>
        <p>--- At last a standing reproach is taken away from the Zoological Society, as a giraffe is in all proba- bility to arrive at the Gardens. The beast was safely delivered at Southampton a few days since, and has only to be packed up for transmission to London. Unfortunately when the London and South-Western Railway constructed their line they neglected to consider the convenience of giraffes, whose necks, it is hardly necessary to state, are above the regulation height for bridgPs,and are not as adaptable as the funnels of river steamers. What exactly will be done with this particular giraffe is a matter of speculation at present. The traffic superintendent will no doubt be equal to the occa- sion, and devise some plan to ensure the safe arrival of this interesting beast at the Zoological Gardens.</p>
        <p>The motion of which the Chancellor of the Ex- chequer gave notice on Tuesday afternoon will give the Government the whole of the time up to Easter, with the exception of Wednesdays and Friday evenings. The Cabinet have, I believe, decided to treat the motion as one of confidence, and should they be defeated they will at once resign. 'there is hardly any danger of this, however, notwithstand- ing that Mr. Robert Wallace and one or two others may opnose it. The demand of the Government will come on immediately after questions on Thursday, and the expectation is that the division will take place before the dinner hour. In this Case the first reading of the Welsh Dieestablishment Bill will be finished at the same sitting.</p>
        <p>Sir Francis Powell has again brought in his Rivers Pollution Prevention Bill. This subject is ripening for action. In the last two or three ses- sions there have been several serious collisions in Private Bill Committees between the conflicting in- terests of large towns and adjoining rural districts, arising from the various schemes for disposal of sewage. In the country there are constant conflicts ' and actions between county councils and other local authorities. The law ss clearly needs re- settling as the physical disposition of sewage needs more vigorous and decisive scientific treatment. I hear that it is not improbable that either a Depart- mental Committee of legal and scientific experts under the Local Government Board may be asked for, or else a Royal Commission to investigate and report on the various methods of treating end dis- posing of sewage, and also to report and advise on the conflicting interests of adjoining localities. It is felt that this is far too difficult a matter to settle off-hand across the floor of the 1101180 of Commons or in one of the Standing Committees,and yet it is a matter which must not be much longer postponed.</p>
        <p>Everybody wonders how the Pearson Pottery Company Hanley, Ciaa sell 100 pieCes of crockery for one guinea (£1 1s) Get a post-card at once, and send for list. A beauti- ful dinner services a china tea service, jugs, basins, dishes &amp;c., &amp;c. Ilverytods' pleasurably surprised. All for one el-tine:L. It yon v.isti to &lt;my. rro CV, write to the I'EARgoN rorreur Co., Hanley, for aruculara.—Advt, •</p>
        <p>•</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>11,1 EXAMINER, SATURDAY MARCH 2, 1895.</head>
        <p>11,1 EXAMINER, SATURDAY MARCH 2, 1895.</p>
        <p>LADIES' LETTER.</p>
        <p>Mr DEAREST NELLIE, I am going to tell you something about skating costumes, even though the frost may all have gone before you get this. I do hope it won't, for, do you know, I think—and I'm sure many men do— that with a becoming costume a girl is never Been to greater advantage than on the icq. As you know, fashion is much more elastic now than it used to be, and you can exercise your taste in choosing a suitable costume. A good wide skirt, well lined to prevent clinging round the feet, is, however, what I advise.</p>
        <p>A very smart and serviceable gown can be made of Press' in blue. The toque is admitted to be the most becoming and appropriate hat for a skating costume, or, if a very severe style is preferred, a felt boat-shaped hat may be worn. Above all, re- member that fluttering ends of ribbon or fur—as the long fur boas—must never appear on a skating gown. They are evidence of the very worst taste on the part of the wearer. A very pretty toque has a full crown of pink satin, sprick'ed with small jet pailettes ; a black valvet brim, with a smart upstanding bow of jet at the left side, secured with a cluster of violets and leaves. The black aigrette and osprey in front are secured by a rosette of satin and a crystal buckle; and a similar rosette is placed at the back, with a few drooping violets and leaves fail- ing from under it on to the hair.</p>
        <p>The inevitable coati and skirt seems to retain perennial favour among Englishwomen, and, although the style does not admit of any great variety, still various modifications are introduced every season; which give the costume the cachet of novelty. At one time it is the length or fulness of the basques that distinguishes one year's coat from another; then lb will be the sleeves, collars, revers, or absence of revere, and so on. In the near future, the large, double, turn-down collars will be the newest feature. These will be square, in feat, a large sailor. shape, or fashioned like an exaggerated coat-collar. This is an orceedingly smart design, and one that will look well right through be spring. The material is bright pansy face cloth, with velveteen of a darker hue. The under-collar is faced with velvet only, and the vest is of white satin.</p>
        <p>A pretty little home gown can be made of electric blue viouna trimmed with black lace. The gored skirt is trimmed with a row of tan- dyked laoe and two rows of insertion above. The round-waisted bodice is perfectly plain at the back. The full fronts are trimmed across with three rows of insertion before they are gathered over the lining at the neck and waist, the hod ice booking invisibly at the front. Ravers ot doubled material are in- terlined wich muslin and edged with lace. They outline the front from the waist to the shoulder, where they are set in a box pleat. The plain high collar is ornamented with a rosette in front, and the folded belt fastens at the batik. The wrists of the gigot sleeves are trimmed with two rows of in- sertion.</p>
        <p>-- - Some very beautiful effects can be obtained by trimming shot satins with tinsel embroidery and shot velvet. An evening gown of nacre or mother. oi.peari satin is edged with a splendid embroidery of pearls, mother•o%pearl spangles, gold thread and Imitation amethysts. This embroidery is edged on each side by two tucks or pleats of shot amethyst velvet. The full bodice is of satin, very low, with no shoulder, sewn into an 1830 band of embroidery —some six inches wide. The short puff sleeve, nearly on the elbow, is of amethyst velvet. What could be more tastef al and beautiful, more rich These embroideries are also used on oorties-de•bal and tealowns.</p>
        <p>MOXON'S NURSERY BOON (Unequalled for DESTROYING NITS, and PABAS In the HUMAN HAIR.) OF CHEMISTS and the PROPRIETOR, J. L. M 0 X 0 N, 262, PARK-ROAD. LIVERPOOL. 6d. and li. per box. Post Free 7 and la. Please mention this paper.</p>
        <p>This is a pretty frock for a girl of from six to ten years of age. The material is golden•brown diagonal serge, and the deep c, if , shoulder•pieces, shaped in three points, d neck-hands, are of Louis velveteen in a darker shade, finished with the inevitab!e rosettes of brown ribbon. The full bodice fastens at the back, and is stitched to the skirt, the join being concealed by the loose Wtkiet• belt. Yours lovingly, JENNIB.</p>
