      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <p>~.</p>
        <p>The Uses ot Salt.</p>
        <p>Salt and water will sometimes revive a person when unconscious from a hurt. Salt in tepid water is a good emetic. For poisoning with alcohol, an emetic of warm water and salt should be frequently given. A teaspoon- ful of salt in a glass of water is a cure in many stomach troubles, relieving colic and helping digestion. A bag , filled with salt and heated is a great comfort to anyone suffering from neuralgia. There is nothing more rest- ful to tired eyes than a bath of warm salt and water. If the head be washed occassionally with salt and water,it will lessen the falling out of the hair. salt i,dded to the bath will be found almost as invigorating as an oct-au dip. If the carpets be sprinkled with salt before sweep- ing it will be found that not much dust will arise, and that the carpets are wondertully brightened. Salt thrown on soot will extinguish the flames. If it be sprinkled on the stove when the kettle has boiled over, it will prevent all disagreeable odours. These are some of the very numerous ways in which salt is in aid to us. It is so very common that it is within the reach of everyone to keep it for emergencies, as well as for cooking.</p>
        <p>The Uses of Hot Water. The best methods of using hot water are, according to a writer in the "Ladies' Home Journal," as follows : For sprains of the ankle and wrist or any joint the part 11 should be thoroughly soaked for half an hour at a time, night and morning, in very hot water. Anyone stiffer- log from a severe sprain will not require more than the first soaking to convince them of the advantage of hot over cold water. A flannel bandage should be applied firmly alter each treatment. For bruises very much the same method should be followed, although the applica- tion need not be continued for so long a time. For wounds or sores the best method is to drip or pour for a few minutes. For styes and inflamed eyelides, and even for sore eyes, use water as hot as can be borne by sop- ping. To stoic bleeding, very hot water applied to the raw surface will be found efficacious. For many forms of dyspepsia and bilousness, particularly a catarrhal condition of stomach, a goblet of hot water, drunk after the night's fasting, will give relief. For continued application, in the form of a poultice, as in catarrh of the breast, pleurisy, pneumonia, &amp;c., a jacket of cotton batting wrung out in very hot water by means of a towel, and covered with oiled silk or waxed paper, should be used.</p>
        <p>To Cure a Cold. One of ths first effects of a cold is to drive the blood from the surface, thus causing pressure on some nerv- ous centre. This means a little congestion or stagna- tion of blood in the part. Now, this is why a cold is often observed to be ushered it by the act of sneezing as a means of restoring the imperfect circulation,and often quite effectually, too. Now, let us take a hint from nature and supplement her efforts by adopting some form of gymnastic exercise, and in a large number of instances the circulation will be so quickened and warmed that the congestion will be removed and not have time to ripen into inflammation. The early treat- ment of a cold resolves itself into efforts to get warm ' and promote the actioh of the akin which has ceased to properly perform its work. It is a time-honoured custom to promote warmth and skin action by hot drinks and !cot baths; but such measures are really only safe and effective when adopted with the very invasion of the cold. As to hot drinks, thin gruel is handy and suitable to many; whilst others like an orange sliced and put into a vessel with a little sugar, and boiling water poured over it. It i,s to be drunk as hot as hot as possible. The foot 4..iath should be used at the bed side and it may be made more stimulating by the addition of a few tablespoons of mustard. The invalid must be well wrapped up, and a short blanket drawn closely round the knees. This process should only occupy five or ten minutes, when the patient should be Ouiekly transferred to a warmed bed. • In a few minutes a general ,eeling of warmth will be accompanied by perspiration. The object is to get the skin to act, and if not successful, ten to twelve grains of Dover's Powder way be taken in a little hot gruel. This is a remedy only to be used by adults ; it may do harm to children. If our patient would rather have a bottle of medicine. he will find the following a useful and safe remedy : Mix together two ounces of liquid acetate of ammonia. half-an-ounce of spirit of nitrous ether, and four and a half ounces of camphor water ; of this two taolespoon- fuls to be taken every two or three hours. •</p>
        <p>Sir George Stewart White, the Commander-in-Chief in India, received the Victoria Cross in 1879. He not only personally led an attack on the enemy, who, out- numbering his own force by eight to one, held a very strong position on the summit of a fortified hill, but. when his men were exhausted by their climb, he seized a rifle, and, advancing by himself, shot the enemy's leader through the heart. This so intimidated the op- posing forces that they fled. Lord Acton, the new Professor of Modern History at Cambridge, is a profound scholar, says " Figaro," but not a very prolific writer. He was a baronet at three, an M.P. at twenty-four, a peer at five and-thirty, and is now a professor at sixty, besides holding the office— rather unusual in a great scholar—of Lord-in-Waiting to the Queen. He has also held for many years the un- official post of Lord-in-Waiting to Mr. Gladstone, whoa- attacks on the Vatican Deczeas he seconded in a pare ticularly vigorous manner.</p>
