      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>CHIEF CONSTABLE'S REPORT.</head>
        <p>CHIEF CONSTABLE'S REPORT.</p>
        <p>Mr. Hunt, the chief constable of Warrington, has presented his report for the year 1894 to the Watch Committee, and it has as usual been issued in a printed form. This is Mr. Hunt's 29th annual report.</p>
        <p>INDICTABLE OFFENCES. The number of indictable offences dealt with during the year were as follows :—Wounding, endangering life, 3 offences ; one person discharged, two committed for trial. Defilement of girl under 13 (C. L. A. Act, 1885, sec. 4), one person for trial. Burglarly, 10 offences no apprehensions. Breaking into shops, warehouses, &amp;c.,4 offences; no apprehensions. Larceny of horses, Sc.. 2 offences ; three persons committed for trial. Larceny from the person, 7 offences ; 14 persons appre- hended and tried summarily. Larceny to £5 or more house, 1 offence ; no apprehension. Larceny by a servant, 1 offence; one person apprehended and tried summarily. Simple larceny and other equivalent offences, 147 offences ; 141 apprehensions, 137 tried summarily and 4 committed for trial; 4 persons were summoned for like offences, and were discharged. Destroying (or attempting) railway trains, 3 persons summoned and convicted;uttering or poss2ssing counter- feit coin, 1 offence, 1 person discharged. Suicide (at- tempting to commit), 1 case ; charge withdrawn. Total eases, 179 ; total persons apprehended and disposed of, ' 172.</p>
        <p>NON-INDICTABLE OFFENCES.</p>
        <p>Adulteration'Wft;Cl;nd drugs. 4 persons convicted. Aggravated assaults, 11 offences; 1 discharged, 10 con- victed. Assaults on constables, 20 offences, all convicted. Common assaults, 86 offences; 42 disclaarged, 44 con- victed. Betting and gaming, 11 offences •, 8 discharged, a convicted. Brothel keeper, 1 discharged. Cruelty to animals, 105 offences; 25 discharged, 80 convicted. Cruelty to children, 31 offences; 18 discharged, 13 eon- Victed. Elementary Education Acts, 322 .4ffences ; 53 discharged, 269 convicted. Unlawful possession of game, 2 offences' 1 discharged, 1 convicted. Indecent exposure, 3 offences ; 1 discharged, 2 convictel. Drunk- enness, 364 cases; 72 discharged, 292 convicted. Offences against PAL closing regulations, 2, both convicted. Other P.n. offences, 5, all convicted. Factory Acts, 13, all convicted. Malicious damage to fences. &amp;c., I dis- charged ; to trees, shrubs, &amp;G., 4, all convicted. Other offences, 17; discharged 8, convicted 9 Army Act, 45 offences ; 6 discharged, 6 convictions, 33 otherwise dis- Posed of. Navy Aet, 1 otherwise disposed of. Unlawful pledging, 1 discharged. Town police clauses. 165 ; 36 discharged, 129 convicted. Borough -.Y.aws, 40 offences ; 22 discharged, 18 convicted. Neglecting to maintain family, 11 offences; 4 discharged, 7 convicted. Misbehaviour by paupers, 9 offences ; 1 discharged, 8 convicted. Offences of supervisees, 1 dis- charged. Prostitution, 17 offences; 6 discharged, 11 convicted. Railway offences, 37 cases; 2 discharged, 35 convicted. Revenue Laws, 25 offences • 7 discharged, 18 convicted. Public Health Acts, 17 offences; 1 dis- charged, 16 convicted. Stealing fruit, plants, &amp;c., 4 offences, all convicted. Sunday trading, 3 offences ; 1 gischarged, 2 convicted. Vagrancy, begging, 6 offences; discharged, 1 convicted. Sleeping out, 15 offences; 6 discharged, 9 convicted. Gaming, &amp;c., 60 offences; 5 discharged, 55 convicted. Found in enclosed premises, 6 offences, all convicted. Frequenting, 10 offences ; 2 dis- charged, 8 convicted. Other offences, 9 cases; 3 dis- charged, 6 convicted. Weights and Measures Acts, 5 r'ses, all convicted. Canal Boats Act, I convicted. _raodulently removing goods (aiding), 1 convicted. Total cases, 1,391, heard and disposed of. QUASI-CRIMINAL PROCEEDINGS AND ORDERS. Orders to find sureties to keep the peace,6B. Bastardy Sirders, 17. Separation orders, married women. 11. tlaintenance order in case of deaertion,3. Orders under InPleyers and Workmen Act, 1875, 7. Landlord and zenant, relating to fraudulent removal of goods, 4 Poor la'w order for niaintence of wife and family ,25. Schools, crendance order, 5. Committal to industrial,7. Order f! payment of maintenance industrial school, 6. Order (pr dealing of maintenance in reformatory school, 2. 3;de with nuisance, 2. For closing polluted 7ell, 1. The total offences disposed of summarily is, bdictable, 156; non-indictable, 1,491 ; and quasi-crimi- nal, 189 ;in all, 1,836. MISCELLANEOUS.</p>
        <p>".r.jhe number of licensed houses in the Borough is : "censed victuallers. 87 ; beerhouse keepers. 45 ; and b. off-licence holders 74 ; total, '206. The transfers have ioeell..te 18 licensed victuallers, 4 beerhoase keepers, and °LI license holders ; 37 music licences to public-houses "33(1 beerhouses, and 12 to public rooms (total 49) were granted during the year. The number of children detained in Reformatory and the ridustrial Schools is 39, for whose part maintenance of £62 18s. has been paid by their parents dur- rlg.the Year. tint„lg,htY-five pedlars' and nine chimney sweepers' cer- have been granted,the fees for which amounted £22 7s. 6d. a ',:lhoere are in the Borough plying for hire 11 cab owners 41" 06 cab and bus drivers; 9 marine store dealers and .Powder and fireworks dealers. licensed for those usinesses. re°f sudden, violent and unnatural deaths, 91 were bePorted to the coroner through the police. inquests ctenrgtilifipeld yd on 40 cases, 51 referred to the registrar, and tlerita doctors. The verdicts were-25 "acci- -3 "natural causes," 3 "open," and 8 "suicide sing temporary insanitary." on fires took place in the Borough, damage being e Probably to the amount of about £4,070, mostly Save by insurance. parnntY lost children were found and restored to their ver`nts, and 40 horses, cattle, and sheep found straying Penfolded ane afterwards owned. L wo hundr d deighty-five shops, warehouses, and throumouses an were found insecure by the police on night duty, Rh doors and windows being left unfastened. Value nouulation in 1891 (census) was 52,743, rateable 6ro4Z)-Y4,001, acreage 2,004, inhabited houses 9,631. the p 'total cost of police, £5,079 35. 61. • the value of with °II" Superannuation Fund being £9,290 17s. 6d., sum B:.,eharee of about 19s. 6d. a week upon it. The £2 7,1,,419 104. for services of police, including during from the Coroner for use of Parade Room the go ncluests held therein, was paid in due course to Slipera rr'ugh Treasur