      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>DR. TALIIAGE AUAIN.</head>
        <p>DR. TALIIAGE AUAIN.</p>
        <p>In one of Dr. Talmage's sermons he says, " If ten men were to come to you when ill with some appalling malady, and if they had the same illness and took a certain medicine and it cured them, you would prob- ably take it. Now, suppose ten other men were to come and say, •We don't believe that there is anything in that medicine." Well I say have you tried it ? ' No, no.' ' Then avaunt. Let me take the testimony of the millions of souls that have been converted and com- forted in trial, and solaced in the same hour, in prefer- ence to the advice of unbelievers who have no actrial experience. We will take the testimony of those who have; for they are witnesses.' " Thousands of the press,tte profession, and the masses iri all nations are witnesses to the value and efficacy of Warner's" Safe ' Cure in the cure of liver and kidney diseases, and if any symptoms such as furred tongue, flatulency and indigestion, pain in the shoulder-blades and in the back, yellowness or burning of the skin, nausea, sleeplessness, loss of flesh, puffy eyelids, short- ness of wreath, and dropsical swelling of ankles or body assail you, be wise and be guided by the experience of a cloud of witnesses.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>DISTRESS IN WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>DISTRESS IN WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>MORE FUNDS WANTED.</p>
        <p>• During tl.e past week, a large quantity 6f relief has been given at the soup kitchen at the Drill Hall. The weather has, however, been much less severe, and the distress in the town will con- sequently, it is expected. diminish considerably. The following ladies have been present at the Drill Hall at the distribution of the soup and bread : The Mayoress (Mrs. James Fairclough), Mrs. Carleton, Mrs. Slater, Mrs. Percival, Mrs. J. Yonge, Mrs. Clay, Mrs. H. Woods, Mrs. Drummond, Mrs. Watkins, Mrs. Hol ford, Mrs. Forshaw, Miss Ken- drick,Miss White (Sankey-st.),Miss White (Legh-st.) and Miss Litton. The funds at present are almost exhausted, and it is hoped that a further supply will be speedily forthcoming. An average of over 3,000 persons have been relieved daily. A meeting of the relief committee was held on Thursday last week in the Town Hall, at which the hon. sec. Mr. Stanley Wilson reported that the soup kitchen was opened on Friday last week in accordance with the instructions of the committee Owing to the very large number of applicants and the short time in which issue the tickets, he was afraid that a considerable number of people (which the fund was never intended for) was receiving relief. However, this difficulty was remedied on Saturday, when all the tickets expired. The total number of tickets issued UP to that morning was 710, representing 3,550 persons. He was pleased to state that the sum of £159 144. 3d had been received in resnonse to the appeal for funds and he estimated that £145 10s. would have been ex- pended by Saturday in connection with the kitchen, leaving a balance in hand of £l4 4s. 3d. The following showed the distribution of food each day : Friday 1,840 pints of soup, 1,050 lbs, of bread. Saturday 1,650 09 1,200 Sunday 1,930 „ I.boo Monday 2,400 „ 2000, „ Tuesday 2,560 „ 2,000 „ Wedne,day 2.680 „ 2.500 19 Thursday 2,69) „ 2.500 „ Friday 2,685 „ 2,500 „ He purposed keeping a separate account, showing how the balance from the coal strike had been dealt with. The total number of tickets issued during the past six weeks for food was 1,940, which represented £2lB ss. ; I,RBO coal tickets, £63; printing,.wages of officers, post- ages, &amp;c., ; total estimated expenditure, £290 ss. The balance in hand, it would be remembered, was £253 7s. 4d., and taking the expenditure as £290 ss, this account showed an excess over receipts of £36 17s. 82. 'I he following was a summary of the finance :—Balance from coal strike fund, £253 7s. 4d.; donations received in response to appeal for funds, £159 14s. 3d. ; total, £413 Is. 7d.; estimated amount expended by issue of tickets for coal and food, 2290 55.; estimated amount of expenditure up to Saturday next in connection with soup kitchen, £145 10s; over spent, £22 13s. 52. In consequence of the shortness of funds, the Mayor has issued an urgent appeal for more funds in which he says that it is believed that the soup kitchen will have to be continued for at least another fortnight, as it will be a full week after the frost has gone and the men have returned to work before they will receive any waees. The amount required, will, it is estimated be £3OO.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>CONCERT AT THE CO-OPERATIVE HALL.</head>
        <p>CONCERT AT THE CO-OPERATIVE HALL.</p>
        <p>To further argument the distress fund a concert was held in the Co-operative Hall, which had been lent free of charge for the occasion, on Thursday evening. last week. M. T. Morris F.G.S. took the chair, but being compelled toi leave before the termination of the programme he was succeeded by Mr. .1. C. Dalton. The programme.which was heartily enjoyed, included songs : Miss A. Sutton, "Angus McDonald "and "Enchantress, Signor Darew- skev ; Mr. A. Lcewenberg, "Old Memories ;" Miss M. Pemberton, "The Stars of Normandy" and "The promise of life ;" humorous songs, Mr. Maedermott " Peculiarities," and "Musical. Party ;" Mr. G. Parr " My Sweetheart when a boy.:" duet " Maritana" pupils of Miss Sutten ; selections by the Borough Band. Mr. H. M. Athton read " Lecture on the Mormon," ( Artemus Ward) and as usual,was a eminently successful and kept the hares audience in a state of continuous merriment. Mr. F. Hickman was the accompanist. A sum of between £5 and £6 will be handed ,over to the relief fund.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>WARNING AGAINST IMPOSITION.</head>
        <p>A WARNING AGAINST IMPOSITION.</p>
        <p>Sir,—l shall be glad if you will allow me to warn the charitably dil:iosed against giving any money te persons who represent themselves as collecting for the relief fund. Several cases have come to the knowledge of the Executive Committee of considerable sums having been given for the relief fund, but which have not been paid to the credit of the account. I must also call upon my fellow townsmen to consider the needs of the deserving poor at this time, and give all the assistance they can, as the funds at our disposal are entirely exhausted. Under any circumstances, we shall require from £3OO to £4OO, and if this frost centinues, considerably more, if • the relief is to be continued on the present scale. Every effort is being made to find out the most deserv- ing cases and to relieve them, but there is a good deal of imposition against which we warn the public to be aware. All gifts should be sent to the Town Hall, or paid into the banks, and these will be acknowledged in the papers weekly. On behalf of the Executive Com- mittee, for the relief of the distressed.</p>
        <p>F. MONKS, Caairmaa, Monksflll, and Co.,Lirnited, Warringtoh, February 22(id, 1895.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>MAGAZINE STATISTICS.</head>
        <p>MAGAZINE STATISTICS.</p>
        <p>From the Jubilee issue of The Newspaper Press Direc- tory for 1895 we find that there are now published in the United Kir.gdom 2.081 Magazines, of which more than 487 are of a decidedly religious character. Comparing 1895 with 1840 (the first year the Directory was pub- lished), it is estimated that in that year there were only 200 of such publications in existence. Almost every branch of science and thought is represented.—From " The Newspaper Press Directory," 1895.(C. Mitchell and Co., Red Lion Court, Fleet-street).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>ALLCOCK'S</head>
        <p>ALLCOCK'S</p>
        <p>WEAK BACKS are re- lieved and generally cured by the use of</p>
        <p>PLASTERS. They sustain the part where applied, and are warming, soothing, comforting in the highest degree. Use for Bron- chitis, Lumbago, Rheuma- tism, and wherever and whenever pain is felt.</p>
        <p>Of all Chemists from 111 i to 2216 each</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON INFIRMARY WORKING- MEN'S COMMITTEE. ANNUAL MEE LING. MAJOR PICKMERE'S REMINISCENCES. On Saturday afternoon the annual meeting of the Workingmen's Infirmary Committee was held in the Council Chamber of the Town Hall. There was a very large attendance of representatives of various works in the town and the chair was taken by Major Picktnere, who said, with reference to the committee of the working classes of Warrington, that a great meed of thanks was due to them for the manner in which it had been taken up. After the minutes of the last meeting hal been read and passed,Mr. J. ROBERTS (chairman of the committee), said he was pleibed to see the balance sheet show such a handsome sum as £449 123. 91. against £438 149. 21. in 1893, and considering the depression in trade and that so many people had been out of work during the greater part of the last 12 months it was very creditable to the workingmen of Warrington that so much had been sub- scribed to the Institution. They had done their shareof the work and he thought that few towns of its size could produce from the working classes such a balance sheet to its Infirmary as Warrington. Ie 1879 he could remem- ber that the sum of money collected towards the Institu- tion was only about £5O or £6O and when they put that amount against £450 there was a great difference which he attributed to the efforts of the Workingmed's Com- mittee. That committee was formed in 1880 and they kept gradually increasing the amount year after year until they reached the figures already mentioned. He drew attention to the fact that this year they had been unable to hold the concert which they had been in the habit of holding and which had been very success- ful in raising money for the Infirmary. They would shortly have a building in which to hold their annual concert. The speaker then referred to the kindness of the gentlemen who had formed them- selves into a committee and held a skating handicap oc the Twiggery Farm ice which had realised the sum of £lB as. and he proposed that the thanks of the meeting be tendered to them. He considered that every work- shop where the workpeople contributed five guineas to; the Infirmary should appoint a person to give recom- mendations. This was an important thing, and his reasons were that he thought that anyone wanting a re- commendation could then get it from the person ap- pointed' at his workplace. It would not increase the number of patients to the Institution, and the recom- mendations for the treatment would certainly come into the hands of deserving people, and he hoped the pro- position would be favourably considered. Mr. JOSEPH TAYLOR (assistant secretary) said the fol- lowing letter from Messrs. James Parker and Sons to Mr. E. M. Crnsfield (secretary) :—" Dear sir.—Our men state that they cannot see their way to 'give to the In firmary through the Workingmen's Committee, as they consider they should have the privilege of giving recom inendations and prefer,therefore,to contribute in another manner." _ _ . _ _ . .</p>
        <p>Mr. G.THOMPSON said that he had looked over the rule which said "that no person having a weekly income of over Iss. a week shall be entitled to the benefit of the Institution," and contended that this was too low. He thought that those earning 18s. a week shonld be entitled to those benefits. (Hear, hear.) In common fairness he thought that the rule should be altered and in refer- ence to Mr. Roberts' suggestion he also did not think it would increase the number of patients. The alteration in the rule he first referred to should be, he thought, that one person should be admitted from a family earning IBs., 2 for 21s. and 3 for ns. Re moved that this be re- commended to the Board of Management. This was seconded and carried. The recommendation moved by Mr. Roberts, Mr. Fanshaw seconded,and it was carried unanimously. Mr. THOMPSON moved that any firm contributing 10 guinoas shovld have a representative on the Board of Management. This was also seconded and carried.</p>
        <p>ELECTION OF GENERAL COMMITTEE. The general committee was re-elected for the ensuing year.</p>
        <p>EXECUTIVE COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>The Assistant Secretary read the names of 17 members of the present Executive C•immittee. and it was decided that they be allowed to stand and that the names of Mr. Walmsley (Messrs. Smith and Sons) and Mr. Emerson (Firth Mattress Co.) be added.--11r. TAYLOR then an- nounced that as Mr. Hartley of L4tchford, had not been able to attend regularly his name be struck off and thPt of Mr. Habberley be put on. This was seconded and carried unanimously, thus mtking three fresh names on the Executive Committee.</p>
        <p>44 . t. tt • ,hatekre, 0 t t _ , • ki... 0 4 ' 4 ‘1) 0 + i .7,11- Pr'f,iftlrgt'r 0 iegi ippiir r, 10, / , ,ovq't 4:11111,IW,,, ; 0 '' 0 0 0 0 4 4/4 S# #6 .4 II 44 / 4 t, 14 4 1 4. 4 i• 4. 4 44 4 - ' - - - - - 4 4, 4 #I</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY MARCH 2 1895.</head>
        <p>EXAMINER, SATURDAY MARCH 2 1895.</p>
        <p>THE CONTRIBUTION BOXES,</p>
        <p>Mr. TAYLOR said that the contribution boxes were called in every May and November and he thought they could not have more suitable dates. It was then carried that the dates for the collection of contribution boxes be allowed to remain as before.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>ELECTION OF CHAIRMAN.</head>
        <p>ELECTION OF CHAIRMAN.</p>
        <p>Mr. WILLIAM GLEAVE proposed that Mr. John Roberts be re-elected chairman of the commiitee for the ensuing year. The Chairman seconded, and remarked upon the consistency with which Mr. Roberts had always done his duty. The proposition was then put to the meeting and carried with acclamation, and Mr. JOHN RoßEirrs, in responding, said lie was first elected chairman in 1880 and had held the position ever since and they might rely upon him to do his best for their interests in the future.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>VOTES OF THAEKS.</head>
        <p>VOTES OF THAEKS.</p>
        <p>A vote of thanks was heartily accorded the medical officers of the Infirmary, Mr, FANSHAW being of the opinion that they had the goodwill of all the workmen of the town and he regretted to hear of the illness of Dr. Bowden.—The CHAIRMAN informed them that the doctor was improving and a vote of condolence was then passed to him.—The thanks of the meeting were also tendered to Mr. E. M. Crosfield (secretary), and to Mr. Joseph Tay- lor (the assistant secretary).—The same compliment was paid the Chairman of the Committee, Mr. John Roberts, and the Chairman of the meeting, Major Pick- mere, and also to the Mayor for allowing them the use of the room. MAJOR PICEMERE'S REMINISCENCES.</p>
        <p>In responding to the vote of thanks Major Pickmere said his heart was always with the working-classes of this country, as in the first place he was brought up as a workingman—as an apprentice. That was nearly 50 years ago. He told them an incident which he had never told to the Warrington people before, and that was that before he was out of his time he was made foreman of all 'he fitters and turners in his place of em- ployment, because he had had a good education, knew all the drawings, and was considered a tolerably good workman. The first year of his apprenticeship he re- ceived 53. a week,and in the last year 9s, a week. After being foreman at this place he was made manager of a large works in Glasgow—the Atlas Works. He suc- ceeded there, and a relative dying in 1851,he thouaht it time to he his own ma,ster,and he had since remained in Warrington, where he had been successively in all the offiees up to that of Mayor. He was one of the old t izens of the old town of Warrington, and he hopcd that himself and allthose present would live long enough to see each other again, and that the Infirmary would go on prospering. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>DRINK TRAFFTO IN WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>THE DRINK TRAFFTO IN WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>A sacred cantata, entitled "The Captive Maid of Israel," was given by the Sankey Bridges Wesleyan Chapel choir, on Saturday evening, in the Workingmen's Gospel Temperance Mission. Mr. W. Brough conducted and Mi. T. Brough presided at the piano. Sales were given in an excellent manner by Mrs. A. B. Willey', Miss Hannah Brough, Mr. D. Farishaw and Mr. Levi Richard- son. The ren4ering of the cantata was of a high order, and greatly delighted the large audience. The Rev. EDWARD T. REED, vicar of Emmanuel Church presided, and in the course of his remarks said that the subject which he supposed was uppermost in the minds of most people at the present time was• the distress caused by the prolonged and unusually severe frost. That morning he, along with others, had received from hiA Worship the Mayor a circular appealing for funds to the amount of £3OO, with which to keep open the soup kitchen another fortnight,so that the di,tress and deserv- ing poor might receive the help they needed. To that appeal, he (the speaker) hoped there would be a liberal response, for it was, unhappily, too true that great destitution esisted. At the same time there were certain facts which the inhabitantsof Warrington would do well to think over at a time like this. The popula- tion of the borough of Warrington was estimated to be about 57,000. Recently statistics respecting the con- sumption of alcoholic liquors for the past few years had appeared in the "Times," and how much did they think was the amount spent upon drink in Warrington? No ipss a sum than £203,893 15s. during the past year, or £3,921 a week. To put it into words instead of figures, over two hundred thousand pounds a year or nearly four thousand pounds a week were spent on drink in the borough of Warrington alone. Further, the amount spent on drink was about twice as much as the amount spent on bread, so that in Warrington the sum snent yearly on bread was only £101,946 17s. Bd., or £1,960 a week. It would easily he seen from these figures that what was needed in order to do away with the • distress was to transfer a portion of the expenditure on drink from the publie- house to the baker's shop. All reasonable and intelli- gent people would admit that such an expenditure on what was at bast a luxury was both wasteful and un- necessary, and if even one half the amount, or one hun- dred thousand pounds, were employed in legitimate manufactures the effect upon trade in. general would be most beneficial. Be hoped that the consideration of these facts would lead to more earnest efforts being made in the great cause of temperance, a cause which for many years had had his deepest sympathy.—Mr. R. Storey proposed, and Mr. J. Drury seconded, a hearty %. otos of thank.. to Mr. W. Broirsh and all the artistes, which was accorded with acclamation.—Mr. J. W. Tanner proposed, Mr. A. Horneks seconded, a hearty vote of thanks to the Chairman for his able address.— The motion was warmly agreed to,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>-MEXICAN CUR IC</head>
