      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>WARRINGTON BLUE COAT BRI)THERLY SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON BLUE COAT BRI)THERLY SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>ANNUAL DINNER.</p>
        <p>The fortieth annual dinner in connection with the above society was held at the Conservative Club, on Monday evening, when the Mayor (Councillor James Fairclough) presided. There were about 100 persons present, among whom being the Rev. E. C. E. Carleton, the Rev. H. R. Wild, Aldermen Sutton and Wright, Councillors Hepherd and P. Davies, and Mr. Nield, secre- tary of the Liverpool Blue Coat Brotherly Society. The two vice-chairs were filled by Mr. W. J. Forshaw, hon. secretary' and Mr. Rose, headmaster of the school. Mr. and Mrs. Watkins efficiently discharged the duties of catering, being assisted by a good staff of waiters and waitresses.</p>
        <p>After the usual loyal and patriotic toasts had been honoured, Alderman SUTTON proposed "The Church," coupled with "Toe Bishop and Clergy of the Diocese," and said that it was a toast which must commend itself to every true Blue. Every lad who entered the Blue Coat School was taught to respect the clergy; a religious training was and always had been a most important element in his education. He sincerely hoped that religion would always form a part of the education of the scholars at the school. Their worthy Rector (the Rev. F. W. Willis), whose name appeared upon the pro- gramme as a respondent, with the Rev. E. C. E. Carleton to this' toast, he was sorry to seo,was not present. He was sure they all admired th- Rector for his constant endeavours to promote the welfare of every inhabitant of Warrington, more especially for all his exertions in administering to the wants of the poor. In this work he was very much aided by his excellent wife, who had, he (Alderman Sutton) regretted to say, been temporarily incapacitated from artmding to those charitable duties on account of an accident which had befallen her. They would all join with him id hoping t.lat she would soon recover and be able to continue her duties. Then there was the name of the Rev. E. C. E. Carleton, who was with them that night. Alderman Button was sure everyone would agree with him in saying that Mr. Carleton worked hard for his parishioners with the assistance of his able wife. He had much pleasure in proposing this toast, and in coupling with it the name of the Rev. E. C. E Carlet ,n.</p>
        <p>The Rev. E. C. E CARLETON, in respense, thanked Alderman Sutton for the kind way in which he had spoken of the clergy and also thanked the company for having so cordially drunk the toast. The Blue Coat School was a Church institution, and if his hearers would tax their memories, they would, he thought, find out that when the present school was founded, in 1677, it was a time of great religious activity. Not only was that school founded, but also many others. The first was established in London, and within a period of about 20 years previous to 1710,about 500 were founded throughout the country. Mr. Carleton then proceeded to criticise Mr. Asquith's Bill for the Disestablishment of the Church in Wales. and said that all the talk they heard about the beneficial effects which the measure would produce upon the Church was so much cant and was much the same as if Mr: Jabez Balfour were to say that he had taken the money of the Liberator Building Society for the spiritual welfare of the unfortunate victims. He again thanked the company for their kind expressions,and hoped that they would not think he had unduly occupied their time, as he thought, the school being a Church institution, that this was an occasion upon which he might be excused. Mr. PERCY DAVIES Submitted The Army, Navy, and Reserve Forces," a toast which he regarded as most important. He referred, firstly, to the absence of his respected father from that assembly, and afterwards spoke of his own connection with the Army and Navy. In 18eI2 he was in the gallant cadet corps of Warring- ton and could therefore say, in a certain measure, that he had been in the Army. He had also been in the merchant service, and could consequently say he had been in the Navy. He had never, however, been in the Reserve Forces, and would therefore say little about them.</p>
        <p>_ Councillor HEPHIRD briefly responded, saying that he had not the advantage of having belonged to the army or navy, as Councillor Davies had, but he had had some- thing to do with the reserve forces, as his father had before him.</p>
        <p>Mr. T. LANE then proposed" The Trustees of the Blue Coat School," and in doing so said that they were a body of gentlemen of the highest character, and were men whom the " old blues " were ever ready to honour. They held high positions in the town, and were ever ready to help forward any good work both with their time and money. As one who had been connected with the school for about fourteen years. he could testify to the I interest they had always taken in the school. They had always been ready to entertain any suggestions for its improvement. It must have been highly gratifying to the trustees at the distribution of the prizes to the scho- lars, to hear of the high standard which the education of the school maintained, by Mr. Rose. They all owed the trustees a deep debt of gratitude, and he hoped everyone of them would do his best to repay it. He had pleasure in coupling the name of the Mayor with the toast.</p>
        <p>Mr. BURGESS said, that like Mr. Lane, his connection with the school had been a long one, and he would like to testify to the interest which the trustees had ever taken not only in the school, but in every boy and girl in it.</p>
        <p>_ ._. The Melton, in returning thanks, said it had been a pleasure to him to be appointed a trustee to the school, and he would always endeavour to the best of his ability to do his duty to the institution. He might tell the company that they had held a meeting of the House Committee that morning, and the want of a new orean was then brought under discussion, and they had in- structed Mr. Rose to write to one of the best organists in Manchester to see about having one. The Rev. H. R. WILD then proposed " The Mayor and Corporation of Warrington," and said he thought they would all agree with him in saying that they could not possibly have a more excellent man than Councillor James Fairclough to fill the position of Mayor of the borough. They would all remember how able and ener- getic Mrs. Greenall had been, and they also knew how active and energetic the present Mayoress was. Then as to the Corporation, so far as he could make out, they were doing their best for the town in every department. The Meyoe acknowledged the toast on behalf of the Corporation and himself, and said that when he was chosen Mayor af the borough he took the position very reluctantly,but several of his brother councillors pressed him so much, that he consented. He intended to carry out his duties as Mayor to the beet 3f his ability, and he assured them that the Corporation and the Town Council were very anxioussitm)vperoryvetioltihgdetilst,cthhwaarirtrtbhutehtye. iurhs.a.dforgTtruheenayi awteerl Fe .vtehrey ratesanxious w were rteo difficalty ined Bktl rhieki eedeypa to enwo. keepingthemae ,gv i,lo: ii ttdnhhogewirtranor,Stdaotrorateahetteosli irtwtthlipeder ee nsTee odn morewt, ifini andigutrihrae: this l Tdaihrirheeycotritwotnouuinnl gates, referred to by Mr. Wild, he might say that when the Cauncil effected the extension to Crosfield•st. and got the trees planted and the footpath widened, Ste., the inhabitants would begin to think they were in Paris. Further, when the small tables were placed round the trees, the band Was playing, and they sat beneath these trees sipping their coffee they would indeed not know where they were. He thanked them for so kindly speak- ' ing of his wife, and should tell her all about it when he reached home that night. (Applause.) Alderman WRIGHT proposed "Success to the Warring- ton Blue Coat Brotherly Society," which institution he understood had been established about forty years, and by no less men than Dr. Bowes and Mr. Joseph Davies, both of whom he respected and esteemed. He coupled with the toast Mr. Forshaw, the hon. secretary of the society, who had held that position 21 years, and who with himself, had been a day boy at the school. In Mr. Rose the school had a master deserving of the biehest possible praise and respect. (Applause.) Mr. Foasrmw thought that in response he could not do better than to read to them the report of the last year's proceedings. Before doing so, however, he remarked that letters of apology for non-attendance had been re- ceived from Mr. Greenall, Mr. J. C. Parr, and others all 1 of whom wished the society continued success. He then read the report, which ran as follows :—" The commit- tee in submitting the 40th annual report of the society 1 tender their best thanks to the subscribers and friends for their valuable assistance. The expenditure for the past year has been £23 18s. lid., and after satisfying all claims there is a balance of £3B Os. 4id. standing to the ssociety's credit at the Warrington Sayings' Bank. The committee believe that this is the first time that Mr. ' Joseph Davies, the president of the society, has been absent from the annual gatherings. They regret this I very much, the more so as it is caused through illness. They earnestly hope and pray that he may be speedily restored to health. It is with profound regret that the committee have to record the deaths of Sir Gilbert Greenall, Birt.—who was for many years a valuable trustee of the school and a subscriber to the society— of Mr. N. B. Percival—who was also for many years a valuable member of the society—and of Mr. Alfred Greenall, a trustee of .the school. In conclusion, the committee ask for the confidence of the subscribers, and hope that they will continue to help them." Proceeding, Mr. Forshaw said the committee always endeavoured to do their duty, and to carry out the wishes of the sub- scribers as well as the objects of the society, and it was a pleasure to him to think that he was of some little use to hie own school. Me Ross ha virg thanked Mr. Forshaw and the re- maining guests for their kindness, remarked that he would ever try to do his duty t 3 the schools. Be also proposed " Success to kindred Societies," begging to mention especially the Liverpool Brotherly Society, and to couple the name of Mr.Nield—the Liverpool represen- tative—with the toast.—Due honour having been done to the propene], Mr. NIELD in reply spoke of the financial position of the Liverpool society, and the work it was carrying on, and concluded by wishing the Warrington society every prosperity. Mr. NDER ', expre TON, in submitting "The Sisterly Blues, Mr. ' he opinion that the teaching of the schoe had ha effect upon the girls of making them model mothef wives. The p 'tion havtc*:'been heartily received, the preceeclin urinated. . -%.... • 4.. •</p>
        <p>The addresses were interspersed with songs by Coun- cillor Percy Davies and Mr. Forshaw ; a trombone solo by Mr. Andemon ; and glees by a male voice glee party under the direction of Mr. Thomas Lane, a member of the Incorporated Society of Musicians, and formerly usher and organist of the school. This gentleman also accompanied ties vocalists. Mr. Whittaker likewise gave selections on the concertina in a manner which earned far him hearty applause.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>DEATH OF MRS. WILLIAM WOODS.</head>
        <p>DEATH OF MRS. WILLIAM WOODS.</p>
        <p>It is with regret that we are compelled to mention the death of a lady whose connection with the town has been long and faithful, namely,Mrs.William Woods, who succumbed to a long illness on Saturday night, at her residence,l, Fennel-st. Mrs. Woods was born at Chester on November 18th,1822,and she had, therefore, exceeded in age man's allotted portion of three score years and ten. She was the widow of the late Councillor William Woods, and like him, her aim was ever the social and moral advancement of those by whom she was sur- rounded and loved. Much might be said of her kind and thoughtful conduct towards the poor on many ozicasions. and her loss will be keenly felt by those in the parish where she lived who have benefited by the consideration of others, which was always the guiding star of her existence. The deceased's example might with justice be regarded as the pattern by which the women of the town should shape their lives. Among the many things in which she took an interest, was the support of the parochial institutions in the towe, and by the clergy , who are thus indebted to her, her demise will be looked upon as a loss both personal and general. The last time on which she appeared in public was at the sale of work held at the Rectory on Thursday,the 19th of July,in aid of the funds of St. George's Mission. Since that time she has been ill almost without cessation, and at the time of her death left three daughters and four sons, who will receive the full sympathy of the public in their bereavement.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>TILE FUNERAL.</head>
        <p>TILE FUNERAL.</p>
        <p>The interment of the deceased lady took place at noon I on Tuesday, at the Warrington Cemetery and in addi- tion to being largely attended was throughout of an impressive character. The funeral cortege left the late residence of the deceas€ d at twelve o'clock in the follow- ing order :—First carriage,containing Mr. Henry Woods, R.A„(Venice),Mr. W. S. Wood, Councillor Alfred Woods Dr. Frank Woods, Timsbury, Bath (sons) ; second car- riage, Mr. Luke Fildes, Et. A., London, Mr. J. J. Webster M.lnst.C.E. (sons-in-law), Mr. Joseph Musgrave. (nephew) ; third carriage, Mr. Edwin Woods, of War- , rington, Mr. Peter Woods Liverpool, and Mr. Thomas Woods, (nephews) ; fourth carriage, Dr. John Healey, Mossley, Manchester (nephew), Dr. Spinks (Warrington) deceased's medical attendant; fifth carriage, contain- ing the wreaths and crosses ; sixth carriage, containing the Revs. F. W. Willis (renter of Warrington), G. B. Thurston' A. A. Markham, and H. M. R. Hutt; seventh carriage, Mr. F. Fearnley ; eighth carriage, Mr. Henry Houghton ; ninth carriage, Mr. Joseph Birchall and Mr. William Bibby, the latter representing the tenants. Amongst those at the church or at the cemetery were Councillor R. T. Fairclough, the Rev. A. A. Bater, and Messrs. Samuel Davies, John Dewhurst, William Hough- ton (representing the Board of Guardians,of which bod' the late Mr. William Wood was at one time a member), and Mr. F. Taylor,the whole of the members of the Parish Church choir,a deputation from the Bank-street Mission, ' and Mr. William Brookes. On reaching the Parish Church the procession was met by the church officers, clergy and choir, and Beethoven's funeral marcn was played on the organ by the Rev. A. A. Markham. Then the hymm commencing "Sun of my Soul," and the 90th Psalm, were sung, the Rev. G. B. Thurston reading the lesson. Then another hymn, "Abide with me," was sung,both hymns having been great favourites with the deceased lady. The service was brought to a ' conclusion by the singing of the Nunn Dimitis and the , Rev. A. A. Markham playing on the organ" 0 rest in the Lord." The coffin was again placed in the hearse, and the procession to the cemetery was continued, the remainder of the service being read by the Rector. The interment took place in the family vault, which is on the north side of the cemetery. The following is a list of the ladies and gentlemen who had forwarded wreaths From the sons and daughters of the deceased a massive cross, which almost hid the coffin from view; the Rector and Mrs. Willis. Mr. and Mrs. Edwin Woods, the Misses Woods (Bewsey-street), Mr. Peter Woods (Liverpool), Mrs. Joseph Woods (Rylands-street), Mrs. Joseph Lee; Mothers' Mission, Howley, per Mrs. R. T. Fairclough; the Rector. wardens and sioesmen of the Parish Church ; Councillor R. T. Fairclough, Mrs. W. Brookes, Dr. and Mrs. Spinks, adult members of the Parish Church choir, the choir boys, Mr. and Mrs. Isaac Houghton, Mr. Joseph Birchall, Mr. William Bibby. The funeral arrangements were carried out by Mr. Joseph Mounfield, Messrs. J. and W. Dutton, and Mr. Edward Connor. The coffin, which was of pollard oak with an inner lining of zinc, had perfectly plain brass mouatings, with the simple inscription on la shield :—" Fanny woods. died Febru- ary 23, 1895; born November 18, 1822."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>SHIP CANAL.</head>
        <p>SHIP CANAL.</p>
        <p>HALF-YEARLY REPORT OF DIRECTORS. THE PROGRESS OF TRAFFIC.</p>
        <p>The report of the Directors and statements of account for the half-year ending 31.,t December, 1891, to be sub- mitted to the nineteenth ordinary meeting of share- holders was issued on Monday. The report contains the following items : . . .</p>
        <p>- ACGOUNTS.—The accoants for the half-year, duly audited and certified, are represented herewith. The expenditure on capital account for the half-year, after deducting proceeds of sales of plant and land, was £183,967, making a total expenditure out of capital of £14.833,377, and leaving a balance on that account of £573,622. The receipts on Ship Canal revenue account amounted to £53,593, and the expenditure to £42,595, showing a balance of £10,998. The profits of the Bridgewater undertaking amounted to £25,872. giving a total balance of £36,870. The following are the de- tails of the net revenue account (No. 10) :—At the com- mencement of the half-year the credit balance carried forward amounted to £271,090, to which are added the profits for the half-year £36,870. and the balance of the general interest account £6,123, making a total of £314,083. After deducting interest on mortgage deben- tures for the half-year, viz. £158,395. there remains a credit balance of £155,688 to be carried forward. SHIP CANAL THAFFIC.—The receipts from the Ship Canal traffic amounted to £49,578, an increase com- pared with the previous half year of £9,455, although there was a decrease in passenger tolls of £1,780. The revenue from goods traffic showed therefore an increase of £11,235. The Directors consider this in- crease satisfactory in view of all the difficulties that have been encountered. Constant vigorous efforts to secure augmented traffic are being put forth in every direction.</p>
        <p>BRIDGEWATER UNDERTAKING.—The net revenue from this undertaking was £28;872, an increase of £5,848 compared with the 'half year ending June 30th last. Although the Ship Canal has naturally diverted some busines from the Bridgewater Canal, the traffic was well maintained considering the generally depressed state of trade. Special efforts are being made with some measure of success, to develop new traffic princi- pally in conjunction with the Ship Canal, in substitu- tion for the diverted traffic. Reduction of expenses will also be continued. SIR LEADEn WILLIAMS AND TEE EOMPANY.—The fol lowing directors retire by rotation, and will be proposed for re-election, namely :—Sir Edward G. Jenkinson, K.C.B.,and Mr. Charles J. Galloway. Mr. George Ricks, one of the auditors, also retires by rotation, and offers himself for re-election, The construction work tieing so nearly completed, a new arrangement was made on the let January last, on the expiracy of his previous en- gagement, with Sir Leader Williams, under which,while retaining the title of the chief engineer, he relinquished the active control of the works on the canal, but will continue to advise the Company on all matters relating to engineering, and will also take el arge of the settle- ment of all pending disputes in which engineering ques- tions are involved. Mr. W. H. Hunter has been ap- pointed the engineer of the Company, and has taken charge of the executive work. ENGINEER'S REPORT.—The following is the engineer's report as to the progress of the works Dredging : Good progress has been made with the dredging of the silt which accumulated in the section of the canal between Eastham and the river Weaver during the con- struction of the works between that point and the Old Quay D.cks, Runcorn. It will be remembered that this part of the work could not becommenced until the lower portion of the canal was open for the Weaver traffic. The winter floods and high tides ha :e somewhat increased the deposit in the estuary section,but a central channel has been dredged, and the dredgers are now actively employed in completing the work. In the upper portion of the canal dredgers have been at work widen- ing portions of the canal and removing deposit. The , Docks: Two pairs of the large three-storey sheds are practically completed, and the third pair will be ready soon after the frost goes and the work can be resumed. The twenty additional hydraulic cranes have been fixed at the docks during the past half-year. Canal Slopes : The work of facing the canal slopes with stone is nearly completed. Walton Lock: The Walton lock is now open for traffic. Hydraulic Machinery: The hydraulic ma- chinery at the docks, swing aqueduct,bridges, and locks, continues to work in a satisfactory manner." CARRYING SALFORD SEWAGE TO THE SEA.</p>
        <p>A sewage sludge steamer for the Corporation of—Sal- ford is expected to arrive at Salford on Monday next, having been launched on the Bth inst. from the yard of Messrs. W. Shnons and Co., Renfrew. The steamer, which has been named the Salford, is 170 ft. long, 321 t. beam, 13ft. 9in, depth moulded. Her lad draught will be about 10ft., and her speed ten knots an hour. She has four sludge tanks, together holding 600 tons. The river Thames has been so much improved by the use of these steamers that the fish which had almost deserted its lower reaches, and even its estuary, are now caught in large numbers many miles up the river, and it is an- ticipated that by carrying out to some ten miles beyond the Mersey tar the refuse which in former years fouled the Irwell and the Mersey a similar improvement will be effected, at least so far as Salford's responsibility is concerned.</p>
      </div>
