        <p>Instances of humorous and unexpected answers often turn up in the Asslze cases when a witness is under ex- amination. Two or three ouod examples were provided at Friday's ji of the Newcastle Assizes. A solemn- looking in e..:.isi —an Irishman—was prosecuting in a. C"s" a 1.-ge,i that three men had throttled and atteint.teo t r,o Inn. " How were sou going home that nigh( ? asked of the defending counsel,meaning of course, which way he was going. "I was going on my feet," came the answer. " You sac he held you till yoll• were black in the face ? " was annther question,and the reply was, " Well,l felt like it, but, of course,l couldn't see my faee." " I suppose when you were thrown down sou soon said counsel, and the reply,given with a expression,was," If I hadn't recovered I sh,,old be dead." De last reply was perhaps the best nf all. "" Do you happen to know a man named Martin O'Hara ? " asked counsel, and Wu retort was, " I don't by name, but I do by sight."</p>
        <p>You must rememb: r that consumption has its beginning in trifling affections. A loss of flesh may be the warning note, or a chronic cough. If the first signs of weakness are ove.come by Scott's Itimulsion, consumption will positively be averted, and the patient will be strong and free from tkis tendency toward th e disease. (9,)</p>
        <p>Mr. Labouchere, when is the diplomatic service married more couples than a great runny country parsons do in their lives, says " Pearson's,"</p>
        <p>' •</p>
