      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <p>- - -</p>
        <p>STRANGE NEWS FROM DIT, GEN ESS.</p>
        <p>A WOMAN'S REMARKABLE STORY.</p>
        <p>TEIE LOCAL PRESS ON THE CASE.</p>
        <p>MIDWAY on the Coast line between New Romney and the Point of Dungeness there is a fishermen's hamlet of Ave houses and a coastguards' station, the whole having a population of fourteen men, the majority married and with families. In the largest house in the little hamlet, lately the centre of so much interest, dwells Mrs. Brig- nail. The Kentish Express, an Important local news- paper, sent a reporter to question Mrs. Brignall, who is described as a woman of seventy-one, but hale, vivacious, and agile at the present moment, though she is known to have been on the verge of death from sick- ness and old age a few months ago. The explanation of this marvel was what the reporter sought to ascertain. " I suffered severely from pains in the side and chest," said Mrs. Brignall in answer to a question ; " I was continually vomiting owing to severe wind spasms; my head was always giddy I got no rest night or day, nor had I the least appetite. This state of things had been coming on for years." "Yes, and it became at last very serious," interposed her daughter, " for my mother began to fall down in fits of unconsciousness due to heart failure. One day my father picked her up senseless in the living-room and the nest day in the same way in the wash-house. Of course we had a doctor for her. But her case seemed beyond the reach of ordinary medicine. There seemed no help for her in it."</p>
        <p>"My restoration to health from decay," said Mrs. Brignall, " was effected by the use of Dr. Williams Pink Pills for Pale People." " She had hardly finished taking the first box of Dr. Williams Pills," interposed Mr. Brignall, " when she managed to get out and take a walk on the shore, a thing she never expected to be able to do again. After the second box she get a ride to Lydd, and she actually walked home, a distance of three miles over the beach." "It was wbat I could uot have done for years past," said Mrs. Brignall, " nor yet thought of doing." "And it used to be most discouraging to us," said the daughter," to find that she would not eat, get what we would for her. Now she seems always hungry, and she can eat anything. She never used to be much of a meat- eater, but it does one good to see the way in which she attacks her breakfast. We can scarcely realize now that she was for so long a time a misery to herself and to us, now that she is in such health and spirits. No one , can doubt the immense good Dr. Williams' Pink Pills I have done her, and friends of ours in Lydd who have been ill, and who have tried Dr. Williams' Pills on our recommendation, have found the same benefit from them."</p>
        <p>Dr. Williams' Pink rills for Pale People are not like other medicines : they cure by enriching the blood and purifying it from disease, and thus strengthen the body and give tone to the nerves. They cure in this way all such diseases as rheumatism,neuralgia,partial paralysis, locomotor ataxy, St. Vitus dance and nervous headache, nervous prostration, and the tired feeling resulting therefrom, diseases depending upon humours in the blood, such as scrofula, chronic erysipelas, &amp;c. They restoro Pale and sallow complexions to thn glow of health, and are a specific for all the troubles peculiar to the female sex, while in the case af men they effect a radical cure in all cases arising from mental worry, l overwork or excesses of whatever nature.</p>
        <p>These Pills are not a purgative, and are absolutely safe, even for the most delicate. This applies, however, only to Dr.Williams' genuine Pink Pills ; substitutes and imitations may be dangerous. Dr. Williams' Pink Pills 3re only genuine in round wooden tubes, about two inches long labelled with a circular containing Dr. Will- iams directions for use, and secured in a wrapper of pink paper, printed in red ink. Purchasers should look for thefull name,Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People. Sold by chemists, or sent direct from the Dr. Williams' Medicine Company, of 46, Dollsorn-viaduct, London, at 2s. 9d. a box, or six boxes for 13s. 9d.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>HUMOROUS EVENING AT THE GYMNASIUM.</head>
        <p>HUMOROUS EVENING AT THE GYMNASIUM.</p>
        <p>Monday evening was the date fixed for Mr. Lester Barran to appear at the Gymnasium in his popular even- ing's entertainment "Hilarity." At the time announced for the commencement of the programme a large audi- ence was assembled in the room, and when it was an- nounced that Mr. Barratt was suffering from a severe cold which would prevent him singing, some very deep though unexpressed disappointment was felt. Mr. Tint:don, who made the unwelcome announcement, in- formed them that Mr. Barratt had procured some excel- lent substitutes in the persons of Mr. Richard Evans, Manchester; Miss Marie Smith, soprano, Manchester ; Mrs. Batchelder, Manchester; and Mr. Clarence Ibbot- son, popular American humorist, from the same place. This relieved the minds of those present from the fore- bodings of a return home, and in the end a fair degree of complacency was attained. Mr. Evans gave great satisfaction in selections from his excellent evening's fun "Happy Hours," where the characters were of a sur- prising variety and the entertainer a complete master of them. Much of the enjoyment of his interpretations was derived from his "get up" without which the sing- ing would have fallen fiat. The chief and most amus- ing characters he represented were the elderly gentleman in " Boys and girls," the" Dutch Girl," the " Country Yokel," and the servant in "Ssreb, scrub, scrub." Mr. Evans' vocal attainments were made full use of, especially his ventriloquial powers which were of the highest order and a pleasing contrast to the usual dell- and-impudence exhibitions, keeping the audience in continual merriment. Miss Marie Smith's talents were not devoted to the humoroas, but were none the less ap- preciated. She possesses a soprano voide of peculiar adaptibility and power and her singing of such fav- ourites as "Killarney," and "The Holy City," were much more highly appreciated than is usual. Her share of the progran-.me was quite a Success and though the audience had come prepared to hear nothing but fun they were willing to be beguiled by good sineing as a set-off. The next person to appear on the platform was Mr. Clarence Ibbotson, who was chiefly engaged in pourtraying the attributes of childhood in a very realistic fashion. Mr. James Batchelder accompanied, on the piano all the ' singers and gave one or two pianoforte solos which sufficed to show that the player WAS a man with taste and skill highly developed as a pianist. Midway through the evening Mr. Lester Barratt made his appearance and was greeted with loud cheers, and after apologising for his very apparent hoarseness he said lie would endea- vour to sing "if it liust him." "By the sad sea waves" was the title of his song which he got thfough very creditably considering the ineonvenience Prom which be suffered. Unfortunately this WAS his first and last ap- pearance, a fact which somewhat added to the dis- appointment of his hearers, who, on his appearance, thought they were going to get their long promised " Hilarity " at last.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>HOW THE QUEEN LEARNED COURT</head>
        <p>HOW THE QUEEN LEARNED COURT</p>
        <p>CEREMONIAL.</p>
        <p>In the course of an article on "the Queen's Tutors' in the "Windsor Magazine" for February, Mr. Alfred T. Story explains that it was the Baroness Lehzen, the Queen's first governess, who "originated the happy idta of instructing her royal pupil in the forms and cere- monies of the Court by means of the dolls which the Queen as a child loved so well. Thus a juvenile pastime was made use of to attain an important end in educa- tion. Indeed, right up to the Queen's tenth year, no in- considerable portion of her play hours was given to rehearsing court receptions and presentations by means of her numerous retinue of do!ls,, which were put into .Court costume, with plumes and lappets for the occasion. A long board full of pegs, which fitted into holca in the feet of the little mannikins. served as the stage upon which the mimic ceremonials took place. In this way, the future Queen and Empress, while holding her make- believe drawing-rsoms and levees, with all their attend- ant dramatis persona of state, learned unconsciously to perform her part in the funrtfrms of her high position with that ease and grace which has since characterised her every act."</p>
        <p>11 Sir Frank Lockwood, who wields a clever pencil, sent the Treasury Bench into a titter the other night by pass- ing along a quickly executed sketch that represented Mr. Chamberlain under most undignified conditions. This work of art was secured by the Chancellor of the Exchequer, who will doubtless keep it for the edification of small and select parties.</p>
