      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>NEGLECT OF CIEIL,DREN AT</head>
        <p>NEGLECT OF CIEIL,DREN AT</p>
        <p>Welsh Ix the House of Commons on Disestablisnment. Monday, Mr. ASQUITH, the</p>
        <p>Home Secretary, introduced a Bill to terminate "the Establishment of the Church of Wales and Monmouthshire, and to make provision in respect of the temporalities thereof"; in ether words, he again submitted to Parliament the Bill for the disestablishment and disendownent of the Welsh Church. The Home Secretary's speech was a model of conciseness and lucidity. It was, as he said, a "first reading" speech and was therefore explanatory rather than argumentative. The proposals of the Government with regard to the Welsh Church were fully and clearly set forth, but the question of policy was scarcely touched upon. That remains, or rather should remain, to be dealt with on the second reading. But, unfortunately for the efficiency of Parliament, the object of the present Opposition appears to be the wasting of time, and in pursuing this object they set all previous usages at defiance. When Mr. GLADSTONE introduced the Irish Church Bill—a Bill which fairly bristled with controversial points—it was allowed, on the suggestion of Mr. DISRAELI, the then leader of the Opposition, to receive its first reading the same night. But the Welsh Church Bill has received no such considera- tion. All the forces of obstruction have been called into operation from the first, and in the end the Government will almost certainly be compelled to resort to the closure to get it through the House of Commons at all. This is the pass to which the self-styled "defenders of the Constitution" have brought thl3 " Mother of Parliament." ' It is quite evident that the Tories have absolutely declined, for the present at all events, to accept Mr. CHAMBRBLAIN'S advice. It will be remem- bered that Mr. CHAMBERLAIN practically advised his allies to abandon the fight so far as disestab- lishinent was concerned, and to concentrate their efforts upon making the best terms with regard to the temporalities, in other words to look after the "loaves and fishes." It was cynical advice, and certainly seemed to show that in Mr. Citaxt- neatane's opinion the opposition to disestablish- ment is not inspired by the highest motives. This advice, however, could not be decently acted upon at the very outset, but we venture to think that in the end the fight will be, for the most part, waged on the subject of the endowments. The proposals contained in the Bill have apparently been carefully thought out. The gross annual revenue of the Welsh Church with which the Bill deals amounts to £279,000, which will be dealt with in various ways. The burial grounds and glebes will be.transferred in rural parishes to thei Parish Councils, in urban parishes to the District Councils, and in county boroughs to the County Councils or to the Town Councils. As to the cathedrals, about which some anxiety has been expressed, they will be vested in the body of com- missioners to be created by the Act who will have to maintain them and keep them in re- pair subject to the right of the disestab- lished church to continue to use them for their present purpose. The present income of the Church which will be diverted from ecclesiastical to secular purposes, and which mainly:consists of tithe rent-charge, will be employed according to schemes prepared by the County Councils, in erect- ing and supporting such institutions as hospitals, providing trained nurses for the sick poor, main- taining public halls and libraries, erecting labourers cottages, and in helping forward schemes of technical and advanced education. The m•anneration of the objects to which the funds might be put evoked a good deal of hilarity from the Opposition but, most people will agree with Mr. ASQUITH when he argued that ,funds which are utilised to bring relief to suffering, to minister to the sick, or to provide refining and humanising influences, are not turned to any irreligious pur- pose. Thoughts of this sort will not, however, lessee the opposition which the Welsh Bill will</p>
        <p>meet w;th in Paliament. The Bill will will undoubtedly be bitterly opposed in the Com- mons; in the Lords it will be summarily rejected, thus affording one more object lesson of the com- plete bar to progressive legislation which is inter- posed by the veto of the hereditary Chamber.</p>
        <p>THE Liberals of the Newton The Newton division are not discouraged by Division. their defeats at previous elec- tions and intend to challenge Mr. LEGH'S return at the next opportunity. On Saturday they gave a hearty invitation to contest the seat to Mr. Jemsa MOON, of Liverpool, and doubtless he will accede be the request. Judging from his address and from the excellent political credentials which are given him by these who know him best, Mr. MooN Will make an excellent representative of all that le progressive in the Newton division. The address which he delivered on Saturday showed not only a thorough grip of what may be called the political questions of the day, but also a deep sympathy with what he aptly called the newer spirit which is now beginning to move in politics. He laid stress, and rightly so we think, upon the necessity of the electoral reform, summarised in the phrase "one man one vote: If that extension of electoral justice were once made,it would be an irresistible lever in the hands- of reformers, and especially in removing the block upon legislation which is caused by the veto of the House of Lords. Should Mr. Momv come forward as the Liberal champion in Newton Division be will not by any means be lighting a hopeless contest. The Liberals of the Division have, as Mr. RAWLINS pointed out, suffered in the past from a too frequent change in their cdndidates- What they have wanted is a candidate who would fight more than one contest, if need be, and who - would make himself well known in all parts of the constituency. The strength of the Liberals lies, of course, in the manufacturing and mining partS of the division, but the rural parts are not alto- gether hopeless, and after the achievements of the present Government, the claim of a Liberal candidate upon the agricultural labourers is ex- ceptionally strong.</p>
        <p>Jr is becoming evident that the Unemployed. report of the Select Committee of the House of Commons on The" tress from Want of Employment will not afford much guidance for local authorities and private charitable organisations for this winter. When the Committee was appointed it was instructed to make an interim report at as early a date a 5 possible, but it has not yet reported. In fact, the more evidence it takes the larger and more com- plicated becomes the problem with which it has to grapple,and even an interim report will need more consideration than was at one time expected. SO' far, the only definite proposal for immediate relief has been made by Mr. E'ER HARDIg, M.P., and it is that the Government should divide a sum of £lOO,OOO amongst the most distressed localities IU the country. A suggestion of this sort could onlf be adopted as a temporary expedient, for it ce Willy has no finality about it. Imperial alms!' giving is no cure for the evils of unemployment;„ and would, indeed, if persisted in, do a great deal,. more harm than good. Getting the people back " to the land in one way or another seems to be the only permanent remedy with any hopefulness In it, and the Committee will not have spent its time uselessly if it succeeds in evolving some workable plan for effecting this object.</p>
        <p>t tilli i , , # , , , , ~ „:, -10 , , 1 , , , , ; ~., , .„ ~.., . , # , , F. , _ .. .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>RUNCOEN.</head>
        <p>RUNCOEN.</p>
        <p>FOUR PARENTS SENT TO PRISON. At Runcorn Petty Sessions, on • Monday, Thomaa Wood, fisherman, and Mary Albiston, living together at 19, Cooper-street, Runcorn, were charged with neglect- ing five children—William Albiston (10), Elizabeth Albiston (7), Agnes Albiston (6), Joseph Albiston (4), and Charles Wood (2), in such a manner likely to cause them unnecessary suffering or injury Co their health On, the 13th February.—Mr. Kuowles,of Widnes, prosecute on behalf of the National Society for the Prevention of Cruelty to Children. Mr. Knowles said the two defendants were in charge of five children, and the complaint of the society he re- presented was that the children were neglected. The Inspector found that the child William was insufficiently clad ; the child Elizabeth's eyes were mattered together, and she was in a dirty, filthy condition ; Agnes, aged six, did not seem to have been washed for Some con- sid e r able time, and her head was alive with vermin. One child appeared so sickly that the Inspector thought she was in some danger of dying. He told dr, female defendant to apply to the parish doctor to atteou. to the child but she did not do so until some weeks after- wards. The defendants were not married. The whole family slept in one bedroom. Tnere was one iron bed: stead and two mattresses which were rotten tnrougu with filth. It was really one of those cases of immoralitY which were the degredation of the country. Inspector MnAteer gave evidence as to the filthy Cep- dition and insufficient clothing of the children at Me time of his visit on the 13th lust. The bedroom wheal the family siept ws in a most deplorable condition ao' tlw smell was most ..ffensive. He asked the remain de-t tend int how she accounted for it, and she told him 01, " her husband bad only worked a few days, was bard to get a few things which were required ia tiqe house. He told her to take one of the children parish doctor,but when he again visited the house severe days afterwards, he found she had not done so. The was no improvement in the condition of the children."' Sergeant Brewster and P.C. Wright also gave evidence: corroborating the Inspector's evidence.—The delendeni" Wood said lie was a fisherman, and it was the 31d ° November when he last had any work. The furnitur! they had in the house had been sold and pawned tO something to eat. He had been living with the fenita defendant about three years. She waa not a druns'_ woman. The reason he did not go to the workboaby •vi was that he was expecting things to brighten up.— ,in: Albiston said they had no money to live on. She for another trial.—The magistrates considered that ta_r, children had been kept in a most disgraceful rnatme!„! and sent the two defendants to gaol for OW maw' each.</p>
        <p>-- Edward Randle and Mary Randle, 20, Canal-at.' 97 charged with wilfully neglecting their two children. - Mr. Knowles said th..te was a family of two child 617'' in this case, one 14 years of age, and another 10. 41 did not complain of them being dirty, but they u.". 1" ' . been almost starved to death from want of sufficie...a— , clothing and food. They had been kept in bed beCa"rimill they had no clothes to put on, and such a practise vi,. bound to cripple their health. The husband was 11°,1' 8,1 much to blame as his wife, who drank, lay good part of the day, and pawned the children's c!u"°„,„,. ing. The man had been out of work for Borne c0115t0,0 , able time, and he had tried to persuade his wife , ti 32,0 to the workhouse. So far as they could gather he . a respectable man but his wife was master of him ~y,.. would ask the magistrates to simply fine the raalegrs. fendant the costs, but he pressed the case against led , Randle.—lnspector McAteer stated that when he Viscijaut , the defendant's house he found the female defer) oae lying in bed with a few old rags as a covering. but ' child wes sitting almost naked near the fireplace, wai. there was no fire. The other girl Elizabeth (14,'ordSs ' lying in bed. He called again about a week afterW and and found the mother again in bed, the two children almost naked. There ;0 nolfood, or fire in the house, and no bedding. the husband who told him be could do nothing at a'o,' Org. his wife, lie had tried to induce her to go t° °keg as to house, but she would not. —P.C. Gregory 51a,3.,. ;0 the visiting the house several times and findin,,, 1,.,:',, tbe condition stated by the Inspector. He artribuir .rt the neglect to the woman's drunken habits. He then ,igeu said male defendant was a decent fellow. Mrs Ilf, ~,r d . for she had had no money for twelve months fro t.n.__Tbe magistratesF(iurooymeamrisrtesdinic3oer toseglaniodi f oar rghoroeed mh°oll4`bsins7A hard labour, and the mak/defendant seven daYO b band. sonment. re 5 Wazi"ra 1 A telegram from Capetown says that tbe 0 . preparing to offer an armed resistance t . L'"countrY• when the latter assume the government of the„,.4 a.ci nt On the ()trier hand TrilLielaat reports ististo quiet in Swaziland.</p>
