      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>TEA MEETING IN CAMDEN WESLEYAN . e ScifooLuoom. . Mrs. R. Morris provided the members of the taothers' meeting class connected with Camden Wesleyan Chapel with a substantial tea, at the llutual•gathering. After tea an address on the words ' • "Behold how good and joyful it is for brethren it n 0 dwell together in unity," was delivered by Mrs. Morris. A conversation afterwards took place. ErVeral songs were sung during the evening. A SOCIAL GATHERING AT RUNCORN. . ' , The third social gathering in connection with the 4IIRCOrri Lawn Tennis and Social club was held in Bethesda Lecture Hall. on Friday evening. The ' r,ootj had been tastefully decorated for the ()ec:- ii.. `ans and over 100 persons were present from te ',own and district. A lengthy programme of dances w4B gonse through, and a most esijoyable time was Vent. Mr. Clement Handley satisfactorily fulfilled the duties of M. C. AN OLD OFFENDER AT RUNCORN. beAt Runcorn Police Court on Tuesday morning, Inliore Mr. T. llandley (chairman) and Mr. E. a young woman of no settled abode named L'44lzabeth Barker, who has repeatedly appeared aefore the Bench, was charged with being drunk , ati,,,d disorderly in Bridgewater-street, Runcorn, the ' ,Trevious night. She was found in an entry, in a unken condition, and only partly dressed. The I,l3,lMistrates committed her to prison for one month Ithout the option of a fine. ST. On Tuesd JOHN'S BAND OF HOPE. of ay evening at the meeting of this Band Mope a magic lantern exhibition was given by kier. Rulley, illustrating " Buy your own Cherries." veral views of London were also shown. A short ;. jell,te.rtainnient was afterwards given, at which Mr. .• 14-11•khasa irrt. Tens Presided. Songs were contributed by i perley, and Miss S. P. Hayes, and Mrs,. - •silterley aw., F. Mr. M. Thomas gave a duet. Mr. . ittler also recited. The Rev. J. P. Williams ( pas- !' gave a short address during the evening. LOCAL SuccEssiss. roost' I„entlan, certificated assistant at Greenway- h - uoard Schools, Runcorn, has gained the elttoumble distinction of being classed in the first tisysiej°n at the recent matriculation examination ....oss°4 13 • . Mr Molyneux, formerly an ass 4, in niversity.— . so 'ant in the Wesleyan School, Runcorn, and ta-Wnwhe,ad master of the Wesleyan School, Pember- wit'h " `Fan, has distinguished himself in connection tio•- the Science and Art Department examine- ho„na. The list shows that the number who took th(;,ours in practical organic chemistry was 278,and Wsrileine of the gentleman in question, of the ilsat-Ington Technical Institute, was bracketed citu'il,,n the first class. Only one other Lancashire the-o.,'"ate, and no Cheshire one, appeared among `0 who were thus distinguished. 44 BrziEnsgns LITERARY AND SOCIAL CLASS. in connection with Bethesda Literary the lq dal Class was held on Tuesday evening,when tore-"ev• .1. Simpson delivered an address on Penny- till 8," Idylls of the King." hext to "In Memo- he:lt3',.: this poem, he considered to be the poet's for seethiction, and was the outcome of that love had "e legendary lore of the medieval times,which of _ BC) often seemed to cast a glamour over many znodieittr ftobreernost poets. In these " Idylls,, Tenny- ty faithfullyilights of King Arthur's Round Table, pourtrayed various characteristic ne $4,-8 of men with all their faults and virtues. In kang Arthur was seen the poet's ideal of a true In gn'•Yi, The Pathos of the Idylls lay in the fact that, lisairt,e of all his noble efforts and the force of a hopeehul example, the king was destined to see his bed h respect to the conduct of his knights. tecij? to she ground, for all, excepting perhaps,Sir Stand ere. failed ignominiously to reach the king's Altb ard up 1 of true manhood. The "Passing of fhiess-n; es_nd " Guinevere," might be considered the Which"' t'n Idylls, though all possessed a charm amply repaid their study. -tale '''UN rims LIBRARY COMMITTEE. eVeDin"alßlittee met in the board room on Tuesday Tfandi 