      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>DELINJERNNESS.</head>
        <p>DELINJERNNESS.</p>
        <p>At Runcorn Police Court on Thursday morning, heti:ire Mr. F. R. Norman, William Bellett,a stranger in the town, who stated that he came front Winder- mere, was charged with being drunk in Church- street, the previous night. P.C. Butler proved the case. Prisoner was fined ss. or in default of payment seven days imprisonment. PRESENTATION AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>A meetini-w—a-eVeici-ai the Railway Hotel, Run- corn, on Monday evening, to make a presentation to Mr. Peter Darlington. Mr. W. M. Williams occu- pied the chair. The presentation took the form of an illuminated address, together with a silver- mounted pipe and pouch. The address was signed by Messrs. W. M. Williams,J. Bancroft,Robert Leek, E. Newton E. Woodland, H. Hill, W. Howard, 0. Thomas, W. E. Darlington, W. Parker, T. Bancroft, T. Smith, H. A. Crawford, W. Cooper, and A. Shaw. Mr. Darlington thanked the donors of the gift. Several songs were sung during the evening. Sr. EDWARD'S SCHOOLS, RUNCORN.: _ .</p>
        <p>A most successful entertainment in aid of St. Edward's day schools was given in the Public Hall, Runcorn, on Tuesday evening. There was a crowded attendance. The first part of the pro- gramme consisted of the perforinance of a minia- ture play entitled " Jackand Jill; or the pantomimic romance of the "Middy of the Mersey.' This was taken part in by a number of the school children, who had been most carefully trained by the school- mistress and assistants. The chief characters were as follows: Jill, Miss Kate Reilly; Jack, Master Thady Derr; Mother GOOBB, Miss Rachel Caskey ; Fairy Queen, Miss May Roberts; Demon King, Miss M. J. Reddington; Lord Nosoo, Miss Mary Coogan; Tommy and Billy, Masters W. Hughes Hughes and Willie Clynch ; Policeman, Master Tom Costello. There were also fairies, villagers, and sailors. The piece was most creditably given and greatly pleased the audience. Mr. Caddick kindly lent the scenery, and the lantern effects were in charge of Messrs. B. Crosby and Robinson. The second part of the pro- gramme was of a miscellaneous character. It in- cluded songs by Miss A. Goodwin, Mr. A. J. McCor- mack and Mr. James Goodwin. Two 'cello solos (piano accompaniment) were given by Mr. E. 13almford and Mr. W. Cooke. The Misses Crosby and Comerford and Messrs. Niekson and Lancaster formed a quartette party and eaug "El Dorado" and "Sweet and Low." Humorous sketches were also contributed by Mr. A. G. Davies. THE MISSING ENGISEEII POUND IN THE SHIP CANAL. Frederick John Le Couteur, engineer of the steam tug Liberator, sou of Captain Le Couteur, of Trent- ham-street, Runcorn, was found floating in the Manchester Ship Canal at Runcorn on Thursday morning about 10.30. lie had been missing from I his home since Decemb.ir 2tith and was last seen 'alive at an early hour on that day when he ex- pressed his determination to go to his boat which was lying in the Ship Canal at the Pier Head, Weston Point. He was 28 years of age, and was highly respected in the town. The body seems to have been disturbed by the Anchor Line steamer " Australia " in passing down the canal at Run- corn, for the body began to float soon after the steamer had passed. It was seen by three men named John Brown, Weston Point, Samuel Brown, Surrey-street, and Owen Roberts, Surrey-street,who immediately obtained a boat tuad secured the body by a rope. It was at once identified as the body of Mr. F. Le Couteur, and it was conveyed on the ambulance to the mortuary where it now awaits an inquest. His watch had stopped at 4.59. The place where the body was found seems to indicate that the deeeasea LOPL tom) fallen into the canal at Bottom Locks.</p>
        <p>---; Sr. JOHN AMBULANCE WORlit.-INTBRESTING LECTURB.</p>
        <p>At the meeting of the St. Michael's Young Men's Society and Improvement class, on Tuesday Under the presidency of the Rev. A. J. Jameson, an ad- dress on ambulance work was delivered by Dr. McDougall. After a few introductory remarks he referred to some historical facts connected with the order of St. John. He stated that the first hospital was opened in the year 1050 in Jerusalem by a num- ber of Italian merchants on account of the terrible cruelties perpetrated upon religious pilgrims who visited from time to time the Holy City. Shortly afterwards it was dedicated to St. John the Baptist and from that day to the present bore hi; name. The late Duke of Manchester introduced the ambu- lance department into this country in the year 1877 and at the present moment more than 300,000 members had been trained in first aid., Many people were impressed with the dangers arising from in- experienced persons moving cases of accident, and the ignorance displayed in restoring the apparently drowned. Since the lectures had been introduced into Runcorn about 300 candidates had been ex- amined and received the certificate of the central association, and only very reeently thirty candi- dates had been examined and he had every reason to believe that they had passed with great success. A vote of thanks was accorded Dr. McDongall on the motion of the chairman,seconded by Mr. Austin. There was a good attendance. SEAMEN'S INSTITUTE, RUNCORN. At the weekly entertainment in connection with this institute, a musical competition was held on Tuesday evening. Three prizes were s.wartled, the first being a melodeon and case, given by Mr. Rush- worth, musical manufacturer,Liverpool, to the best melodeon player, 2nd prize, a melodeon presented by Mr. W. Shaw jun., musical agent, Top Locks, for the best rtiouth-organ player, and 3rd, a fife, given by Mr. A. Abel,to the best flautist. Mr. Shaw, missionary, occupied the chair, and spoke of the pleasure it gave him to see so many sailors and boatpeople present. Six competitors entered into the melodeon contest, and Mr. Owen Garvey was adjudged by the audience as the successful competi- tor. Mr. W. Shaw jun., on behalf of Mr. Rushworth presented the prize,stating that that gentleman was ever ready to eueourage musical talent. Mr. Garvey then played a selection on the prize melodeon, and it may be stated that he has also won a gold, and , silver medals for his performances. The Chairman alluded to the unemployed boat people owing to the icki in the canal, and suggested that a collection should then be taken towards the expense of the dinner to be provided the following day. This was done, atsd the result was satisfactory.—Two persons entered for the mouth organ contest, and the prize was won by Mr. George Finney who played in grand style. In the fife contest there were four competitors, the successful one being Master Paul Green. Mr. W. Shaw jun., presented the fife on be- half of Mr. A. Abel. Mr. Hulse gave a recitation entitled " A Bridgekeeper's Story " for which he received an encore. About 400 persons were pre- sent. A GENTLEMEN'S EVENING. There was a very large attendance in Brunswick Wesleyan Schoolroom On Wednesday evening,when the gentlemen connected with the Brunswick Mutual Improvement Class provided a most enjoy- able entertainment. The hall had been decorated for the occasion,there being a profuse display of art muslins around the room,and surrounding the plat- form a number of Union Jacks were exhibited. In addition, certain electioneering literature had been introduced, a number of cards being hung up, containing several requests, including "Plump for Wagstaffe and down with the Commons," and " Up with the Lords," and also" Tote for Slap dash, the workingman's friend," which mystified the on- looker until the second part of the programme was reached. Mr. T.Simister, president of the class, oc- cupied the chair. The first item was a glee en- titled "Foresters sound the cheerful horn." Songs ' and readings were afterwards given. The soloists were Nlessrs..E. Worrall.R,Morris,Evans,and C.Leath wood. Mr. F. Ilosker recited "A Perfect Cure," and two humorous readings "Au Irish Wooing," by Mr. F. Stubbs, and " Bringing down tile house," by Mr. J. D. Jones greatly delighted the audience. The first part of the programme concluded with a trio, " A little farm well tilled," by Messrs. Burrows, Leathwood, and E. Worrall. Next followed a most laughable representation of" Nomination Day" in the "Good Old Times." The uniarsortant spot of Rook Savage, near Runcorn, 'had been selected as the borough for Parliamentary representajon. The Mayor (Mr. J. D. Jones) wearing his chain of office, who was accompanied on the platform by the borough clerk (Mr. F. Ellison), and the Hon. 'fin:10111y Warzstaffe, Conservative candidate. (Mr. W. Millinvtnii), and Paul Slapdash, Esq., Liberal candidate (Mr, J. H. Darlington), proceeded to call upon the electors to select a candidate, and a mock ction then took place which kept the audience in roars of laughter. The grote,q le speeches of the t wo candidates containing many local ail usions,and the heckling they underwent was most original, aud was carried through successfully by all who took part. These, in addition to those already mentioned, included Bolus Sapwehl, Mr. G. Hayes; Peter Blunt, Mr. T. Saunders; Overdose Gamou, Mr. E. v:den ; Abraham Puggius, Mr. J. B. Pick- thall ; (1.1) Usher, Mr. Nancollis ; and electors, nOO electors, hecklers, rowdies, Messrs. Smith, Millin- ger, Stubbs, Percival, Hosker, Dodd, J. J. Pickthall, and J. D. Bisbrown. A quartette "On the Bank of Allan Water" having been given, a pleasant even- ing's entertainment concluded. Mr. Yr. Cooke was the accompanist.</p>
        <p>. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>TIIE EXAMINER. SATURDAY, MARCH 2, 1895.</head>
        <p>TIIE EXAMINER. SATURDAY, MARCH 2, 1895.</p>
        <p>ELLESMERE-STREET MUTUAL IMPROVEMENT SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>An address on" Mer-rie England " was given at the meeting of this society on Friday evening, by Mr. Thomas Leach, in which be dealt with the varieus points enunciated in Nunquam's book. A discussion afterwards followed in which the Rev. John Stafford, Messrs. E. Jones, E. Gandy, T. Parry, Bentley, and W. C. Cooke, took part. The chair was occupied by Mr. F. A. Clarke.</p>
        <p>THE DISTRICT NURSE'S WORK AT RUNCORN. The District Nurse reports that there have been 21 cases visited (seven of these being fresh ones) from January 25th to February, 25th, viz. : 5 S chronic, 9 medical, 2 typhoid, and surgical. Of these two had died,one was well, and one had gone away for change. This left 17 cases on the books. Twenty-seven patients had received occasional visits.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>DEATH 07 MRS. CHADWICK, OF RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>DEATH 07 MRS. CHADWICK, OF RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>It is with regret we announce the death of Mrs. Chadwick, wife of Mr. W. H. Chadwick, 28, Leinster-gardens, Runcorn, which occurred on Mon- day evening. The deceased lady was 64 years of age, and for a long period had been a sufferer from bronchial asthma. She was knurled seventeen years ago, and was the daughter of Mr. Holme, farmer, Taddington, Derbyshire. She extended her sympathies to numerous religious movements, and had won the esteem and respect of a large circle of friends. She identified herself with Bethesda Con- gregational Church. The funeral will take place to-day (Friday) in the burial ground attached to Bethesda Church. RUNCORN LIBERAL CLUB.—" CHARTISM."</p>
        <p>At the Liberal Club, on Thursday evening, the Rev. J. Stafford introduced a discussion on " The six points of the People's Cbarter ; were they right or wrong." The rev. gentleman, who was identi- fied with the Chartists. and was acquainted with the leading men of the movement, took the affirma- tive.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>SOCIAL GATHERING AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>SOCIAL GATHERING AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>In Bethesda Lecture Hall, on Wednesday even- ing, a social gathering was held, when about 100 persons were present. Dancing was heartily in- dulged in until early next morning. Several songs were interspersed. Mr. Ravenscroft was in charge of the musical arrangements, and the duties of M.C. were satisfactorily carried out by Mr. F. Rearsley. Miss Spearing provided the refreshments. TUB DISTRESS AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>Considerable distress still prevails in the town, and relief is being most generously distributed. At the meeting of the general committee of the unemployed fund on Thursday night the recom- mendations of the several ward committees were submitted, and tickets for relief to the amount of £lOO were ordered. The subscriptions received include ; Messrs. Greenall Whitley and Co., £10; Mr, C. E. Parker, Penketb, £5; Mr. G. Heatherington, £lO 10s. • Messrs. R. Teige and Co., .£5 55.; Mr. R. Garnett. Warrington, £5 ;Alder- man McKechnie, .£5 ; Mr. W. E. Walker, £5; Local Order of Rechabites,•.£l is. ; Mr. R. Collinge, £1; Messrs. Huntley and Palmer,Xl; Bridgewater Foun- dry Works,Xl Bs. 3d. The amount found in the boxes was lie. 6d. The free breakfasts to school children are being continued at the Board schools,Wesleyan schools, and National Schools. Mr. A. R, Norman (on behalf of Alderman McKechnie) has distributed £5 between these three schools. Several tradesmen are also distributing soup and bread. These include Mr. Hedley, butcher, high-at.; and Mr. Thomas Pendlebury, of the Royal Oak, St. John-st. The employes of the Co-operative Society gave a free dinner of soup and bread, on Sunday, in the Co- operative Hall. About 900 persons were supplied. This was continued oa Wednesday afternoon when about 1,000 persons were provided with dinner. Mrs. Lawson kindly gave he services as cook. The soup kitchen has also been continued at the Sea- men's Institute. A SUCCESSFUL EXHIBITOR.</p>
        <p>Mr. S. B. Chadwick, J.P., of Runcorn, secured the first prize at the Shire Horse Show at London, this week from 14 competitors in th 3 class for geldings by means of his gelding Leo. He also obtained a fourth prize in the class for stallions four years old with his horse Apostle. There were 33 entries in this class. He was also the breeder ot an animal that secured the fourth prize in the class stallions three years old. THE STOPPAGE OF THE CHEMICAL WORKS AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>A special meeting of the Council was held on Wednesday morning when resolutions were adopted which have since been forwarded to the directors of the United Alkali Company. They were in effect that the Council heartily thanked the directors for their generous support to the relief funds, and then begged to direct their attention to the distress in the town as rapidly assuming an alarming charac- ter. The propositions also drew attention to the fact that a large section of the population was en- tirely dependent upon the chemical industry for a livelihood, and consequently the re-opening of the local works was anxiously awaited in the interests of the town. The Council respectfully urged the directors to re-open the works at the earliest possi- ble moment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>THINGS HEARD HEARD AND SEEN AT</head>
        <p>THINGS HEARD HEARD AND SEEN AT</p>
        <p>RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>BY OUEL 'RUNCORN CORRESPONDRNT.</p>
        <p>The milder weather which has prevailed during the past week has been most welcome. It has tended to mitigate the distress which has for so long a time been recorded. But work for the large numbers of unemployed is not yet forthcoming. The chemical works in the town are almost at a standstill, and the Bridgewater canal is at present unworkable. Should the thaw continue,traffic will be commenced in a few days,and this will of course find employment for a large number of boatpeople. With regard to the chemical works,l was pleased to learn that the Urban Council have passed several resolutions at a special meeting called on Wednes- day last,in which they respectfully urged upon the directors of the United Alkali Company the advisa- bility of re-opening the works at Runcorn at the earliest possible moment, owing to the distress in , the town rapidly assuming an alarming character. Recently the stocks of chemicals at the works have been considerably reduce, and it is to be I hoped that the company will act upon the sugges- tion of the Ideal authority, so that are long we shall ! have the works in full swing again. There is one redeeming feature in connection with the trade of I the town, and that is that the works at the docks ' has been brisker during the past week than has been the case lor a long time previously.</p>
        <p>At the Police Court on Monday a number of parents were committed to prison for neglect of their children. Runcorn has been rather tree from such cases, hut the evidence given on Monday dis- sipates any hopes which might have been held out that Runcorn is any better in this respect than her neighbours. There appear to be dens in our midst where children are brought up—or rather dragged up—in a disgraceful manner. The neglect lay chiefly in uncleanliness, and in this matter there surely cannot be much ground for excuse. The punishment inflicted, although it may seem rather severe, should have a salutary effect.</p>
        <p>The Halton Parish Council took some very neces- sary steps at their meeting on Tuesday evening, in directing that the attention of those responsible for the roads in the district should be called to their present bad condition. It was stated at the meet- ing that the roads were. never in worse order than at present. This being so, it would be wise policy to engage a number or the unemployed in repair- ing them. Such work has been recommended by the Local Government Board in their circular to local authorities urging upon them the necessity of commencing relief works for the unemployed. A difficulty however arises owing to the roads being in different hands, some being private roads, and others under the control of the Daresbury Highway Board. The Parish Council's power merely con- sists in suggesting that the roads shouid be at- tended to, and it may so happen that by the time aetion is taken in the matter the unemployed may have found other work.</p>
        <p>The County Council election at Runcorn has ended as was expected. There will be no contest, and perhaps this is the wisest course considering the present state of trade in the town. Mr. S. Taylor, J.l'. is therefore r3presentative for the North division of the town, taking Major Hazie- hurst's position from which he retired. Mr. Philip Speakman, J.P., goes back to the Council chamber for a further period of three years as representative of the South dil isiou. Both gentlemen are well fitted to occupy the honourable position, in fact Mr. Speakman took a most prominent part in the transaction of the business of the council during his last term of office. With regard to Mr. Taylor, it may be stated that he has occupied the most prominent positions in the towns, and has shown marked ability in the fulfillment of them.</p>
        <p>Mill</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>HILTON PARISH COUNCIL</head>
        <p>HILTON PARISH COUNCIL</p>
        <p>THE REPAIR OF THE ROADS AND OTHER BUSINESS.</p>
        <p>. A meeting of this council was held in the girls' school- room, Halton, on Tuesday night. The Rev. G. D. Wray , occupied the chair, and there were also present :—Messrs. , Astbury, Birch, Hughes, McCulloch, Stokes, Barrow, Jeacock, Looker and Woodier (clerk). It was reported by the Clerk that in accordAnce with instructions received at the last meeting of the Council, he wrote to the Charity Commissioners applying for information respect- ing the Haltom charities as to which were purely eccles- iastical and which would coma under the control of the Parish Council.—The Commissioners acknowledged the letter, stating that a further communication would be made en the subject.—The Chairman said he thought it simply meant that the facts were not ready.—With regard to the communication sent to Mr. Evans regard- ing the footpath through the fields from Runcorn to Halton, that gentlemen wrote stating that he was under the impression, the public had a right of way through the fields mentioned, but its being so, did not in his opinion oblige Sir Richard Brooke to keep the path in order. If, however, he was wrong in his contention,he was quite willing to do what was necessary in the matter.—The Chairman considered it was a matter to be dealt with by thecomn ittee which had been appointed.- -Mr. Stokes wished 1.0 know whether inquiries had been made as to who should do the repairs. The tenants usually did that.—The Chairman thought the Committee should be called together so that they might go fully into the mattele,;4-Mr. Astbury said that when he was tenant he lied Eh keep the footpaths in repair.— Mr. Jeacock said the evide f the last speaker was sufficient, he haVing tione t . pairs for many years. If il they could prose to:Mr. E that it was usual for the g tenants to keen them in,o" rhe would see that the matter was at ded to.—lt was ultimately decided that r a letter be sent a r. Evans asking him to get. the tenants to put tit.; 10 • is below the first stile in repair, and faili , '.4 as the wish of the council, as it was a matter 5 , CA that he should do it. With regard to the u . , portion of the footpath. Messrs. Birch and Hugh.. were teptiointed a deputation to wait on the tenants on either side, asking them to repair the footpath.—lt was ag,—ed to, ott the motion of Mr. Looker, seconded by Mr. Barrow, that the Clerk write to the Daresbury Highway Board pointing out the -bad con- dition of Spark, Bradawell, Sea, and Punip lanes, and asking for their immediate attention to the 'eame.—Mr. Jeacock proposed, and it was agreed to, that a letter be written to the Steward of the Duchy of Lancashire calling attention to the condition of Hanging Rock-road at Halton.—Mr. Stokes called attention to the dangerous stats.,df the fences on the approach way to the girls' schoolroom. It was decided that the owner should be written to.—The Coun- cil then considered the question of the transfer of the Burial Board to the Parish Council.—The Chairman said they had no option in the matter. As a matter of fact they were the Burial Board. The best plan would be, that they should defer the transfer until after the close of the Burial Board's financial year. The Act contem- plated that there should be compensation to any clerk who ceased to act in consequence of the transfer. That would be so in their case. The questiou arose, What was to be the compensatiou ? It was decided that the matter should be held over in order that inquiries might be made as to what was being done in other parishes in the matter. The annual meeting was fixed for Tuesday,the 16th April. This concluded the business.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>BOARD OF TRADE INQUIRY AT RUNCORN. THE ACCIDENT AT THE SPRINCH WORKS.</head>
        <p>BOARD OF TRADE INQUIRY AT RUNCORN. THE ACCIDENT AT THE SPRINCH WORKS.</p>
        <p>Mr. H. F. Stephens, Board of Trade inspector, con- AlCted an inquiry at the Town Hall, Runcorn, on Friday, telative to the death of Thomas Weston, who died from injuries received at the Sollch WerkS Of the Mane!Weer Ship Canal Company at Ildneedt, nedetiiber Ith, 1894. Mr. Brandon, of Messrs. Grundy, Kershaw, and Co., Manchester, represented the Ship Canal Company. The Inspector said that the notice of Accidents Act, 1894, required them to inquire into certain accidents which happened in different classes of works, and formal nctice having been given of oae that took place in the works of the Manchester Ship Canal Company at Runcorn, whereby Thomas Weston was killed, the Board of Trade thought it was of such importance that an in- vestigation ought to be held. They had therefore ap - pointed him to inquire into the circumstances. James Lloyd, 18, Norton-street, said that he was employed by the Manchester Ship Canal Company as " holder up "at the Spriuch works. He remembered the 7th December, the date of the accident. There was a girder 34 feet long, and a little over two tons in weight in course of construction. The girder was fixed on two blocks and was kept in position by three wooden shores on one side, and four on the other. He was going to work on the top of the girder in order to rivet a convex- bar, when on going along-side the boiler he s.w the girder moving. On of the shores uphold- ing the girder rested against the boiler. When he saw it moving he shsuted to Weston, but Weston was unable to get out of the way in time and it fell upon him. This hap2ened when they were re-commencing work after breakfast time. By the Inspector: Was any skylarking or anything of that kind going on during the breakfast time ? No, I never s low anyone touch the girder during that time. By Mr. Brandon : Haven't you some idea that innocently you were the cause of the accident? Well, when I was pass- ing along between the girder and tho boiler taking up my "holding up" hammer and raising it to my shoulder, I think it must bays caught one of the shores, for lin- mediately after lfelt the shock, the girder began to totter. Samuel Blundell, 30 Albert-street, said he was work- ing on the girder that fell before breakfast. There were four shores on either side of the girder to hold it erect. To his knowledge no one went round to examine the shores. He had never known a girder to fall before like that. Owing to the illness of the foreman, Mos ton witness was practically in charge, He was unable to account for the girder falling when only one of the shores had been knocked out. The other shores should have been sufficient to hold the girder. He was confi- dent that the other shores were all of the right length. Thomas Weston, Union-st., said he was not in the shop at the time of the accident when his son received the injuries from which he died. The previous day to the accident witness propped the girder. It was strutted in the usual way. He could not account for the other props giving way when one was touched. John Oakes, 42, Sewell-st., also gave evidence, stating that the girder was properly propped up. Mr. Wiswall, engineer for the Bridgewater Canals Un- dertakings, said he went to the place of the ascident soon after he heard of it. As an engineer he considered that the girder was properlf propped up. He could not account for one shore letting the girder down, but his opinion was that another must have been touched in the same way. It was the first fatal accident they had ever had in that shop. It was poste up that any com- plains should be made to witness. Mr. Lloyd recalled, said that the deceased was behind him at Mi.: time of the accident. Be did not see anyone else near the props. The Inspector said the inquiry was now closed, and he would make his report to the Board of Trade In the usual manner. He thanked Mr. Brandon for the assistance be had given him.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>FRODSIIAM.</head>
        <p>FRODSIIAM.</p>
        <p>MASONIC BALL.—Onleo night, under the auspices of the Worshipful Master and Officers of the Citolmon- deley Lodge of Ancient Free and Accepted Masons (No. 1,908), Frodsham, a very enecessful hall, took place in the Masonic-rooms, Main-street. The orchestral bind of Mr. W. P. Bayley, also of Liverpool, was ie4ttendanee. The ball was opened shortly after eight o'cTelc by Mrs. Davies, the wife of the worshipful master, and Bro. Hey- wood, the oldest past master and provincial grand officer present, and dancing was continued, with but short intervals until about 2.30. The refreshments, table decorations, and accessories were placed in the hands of Miss Bibby, of Frodsham. The duties of M.C. were ably performed lw Bros. T. D. Timmins and H. E. Heywood. The Ball Committee comprised—Rms. S. Davies, wor- shipful master ; T. D. Timmins, junior warden ; J. B. Holland, treasurer ; F. A. Smith, secretary ; J. J. W. Potter. senior deacon ; J. G. Kydd, inner guard ; and H. E. Heywood. P.M. THP. PnrrY Suss:corm—On Wednesday these sessions were held before Mr. C. Reynolds (chairman), and Mr. Thomas.—George Jackson and Ellen Jackson,of Lostock Gralam, were fined fis. each, including costs for being drunk and disorderly in Chester Main-road, Frodshim, on the 2nd February. P.C. Price proved the charge. TIZINITY WESLEYAN Cnuncm—At this church on Wed- nesday evening, a sacred cantata, entitled" Esther, the beaut:ful Queen," was rendered by a band and chsrus numbering fifty. Mr. T. Sant was the conductor, and the leader of the hand was Mr. Halstead Clough. The persons represented were as follows : Esther, the queen, Miss Mary Bennett ; Ahasuerus,the lsing,Mr. James Ben- nett ; Ha man ,oyerseer of the realm,Mr.H.Fogg ; Zeresh, Haman's wife, Miss A. Linaker ; prophetess, Miss Kent ; Mordecai, Mr. Joseph G. Critchley : maids of honour, Miss Isinalt,r and Mks Jeynes. Mr. Joh+, Holland bsals the role of high priest, Hegai and Flegai. A number of ehoruses,solms.airs,and resulings Jr-ni Esther were given. Mire A. Wrench a einanpanied on the piano, and Mr. W. U. Pickering on (Jos harmonium. The proceeds were in aid of the church restoration fund.</p>
        <p>• E ST ON. THE New Cattacn.-:-Preparations Rre now being made for the erection of the new church at Weston. Messrs. Douglas and Fordham have been appointed architects. The Church Building Committee consists of the following gentlemen :—Rev. Canon Maitland Wood (chairman), Messrs. E. Aaron, W. Bankes. T. Ford, 5n., J. Ford, Jun.. A. Guest, G. Robinson, T. T. Sill (treasurer), and I the Rev. W. H. Stables (secretary).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>WARRINGTON AND THE SHIP CANAL</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND THE SHIP CANAL</p>
        <p>THE DREDGING ov THE RIVER.</p>
        <p>• We understand that We negotations which have been carried on for some time pest between the Manchester Ship Canal Co. and the Corporation of Warrington, are likely to terminate very shortly either in agreement or a reference for settlement, partly to the arbitrator provided under the Act and partly in the Law Courts. It cannot be urged against the Corporation that they have shown the slightest want of consideration for the difficulties with which the Ship Canal Co. have been sur- rounded ; and they have a right to expect that the spirit in which they have conducted their part of the negotiations should be fully reciprocated by I the Ship Canal Co. There is an impression, how- ever, that some of the directors of the Ship Canal Co., are disposed to pay as little attention as pos- sible to the obligations imposed upon the Ship Canal Co. by Act of Parliament and te yield rather to pres- sure than to any feeling of hyper-sensitiveness. We believe that the Rivers' Committee had under con- , sideration at their meeting on Monday last the re- ply of the Manchester Ship Canal Co's. directors to the proposals made to them by the Corporation as a result of the conference held at the Town Hall a few weeks ago. The directors expressed their wil- lingness to meet the Corporation to discuss all the terms proposed. which, with the exception of the first two, they aonsider with some modifications, right be agreed to. They desired to eliminate from further discussion the two first proposals of the Coxporation entirely. The proposals in question are, that until the whole of the obligations of the the Ship Canal Co. to Warrington are car- ried out, all Warrington traders shall have the free use of the Ship Canal ; and secondly, that the penalties which have accumulated under the agreement of the 12th December, 1893, from the non-completion of the dredging of the river ; and which now amount to between £(1,000 and £7,000 shall be handed over to the Corporation to be used by them as they may feel disposed. The Rivers' Committee naturally did not feel disposed to meet the directors in conference upon the proposals which they have submitted, unless the whole of such proposals were discussed, and a reply to that effect has been sent by the Town Clerk. Notice has also been given to the Corporation by the Ship Canal directors that the dredg- ing of the river was completed some weeks ago and that the agreement under which certain Warrington traders had the right of use of the Ship Canal, pending the completion of the dredging, must now terminate. The statemeut alleged that the dredging of the river has beeu completed is as absurd as incorrect, and the sound- ings which have been taken regularly by the borough Surveyor and his assistants, and which are being con- tinued, show distinctly that not only has the dredging not been completed but that it is still far from comple- tion. As an evidence of this, we are informed that two, flats, each of which was drawing not more than eft. to aft. 3in. grounded in the channel somewhere near Walton Arches, on Wednesday. Whatever the result of the next conference may be, if it is held at all, we are quite certain that the manufacturers and ratepayers generally, will strengthen the hands of the Rivers' Com- mittee in every possible way, in the deter- mination, which we are informed they have unanimously decided upon, to insist upon the due observance by the Ship Canal directors of the whole of their responsibilities to Warrington •, and now that a reasonable time has been given for the fulfilment of the more important of these responsibilities, definite action will probably be taken before very long.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>OUR LOCAL WiT.TROHEB-WARItINUTON.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL WiT.TROHEB-WARItINUTON.</p>
        <p>SERVICES FOR THE PEOPLE.</p>
        <p>There was an exceptionally largo attendance at the „People's Service in the lI9M-st. Wesleyan Church, on Sunday afternoon. The meeting was conducted by the Rev. J. "Galan. After a selection had been played by the orchestra, and the service had been opened in the , usual manner, Miss Florence G. Hillier (of Manchester), sang "The Promise of Life" with excellent effect, and Mr. Podmore rendered a flute solo in a very accom- plished manner. The Rev. J. Kirtlan then called upon the Rev. Davies, of Didsbury College, to address the meeting. The speaker said the subject of his address was "Moral strength." Strength, he said, in every form was admirable, but the strength most worthy of a man was moral strength. After speaking of the importance of the subject in hand, and of the stress which had been laid upon it in the teaching of Jesus Christ,he proceeded to name the conditions which must be observed for the prepFr development of the moral character. Firstly, there must he a supreme purpose in life. He referred to the purposeless man whom he likened to a chip thrown into a stream. There was much truth in what had been so often said about man being the creature of circum- stances, and although he believed that no set of circum- stances however favourable would make a bad man good,and no set of circumstances however unfavourable need make a good man bad, still it was the duty of the church to influence the surroundings of men in as to make it easy to do right,and difficult to do wrong. He re- ferred to the fact that money and pleasure were the chief obkcts of pursuit in the lives of many, and reminded them that these things were insufficient to gratify the instincts of the immortal soul. Let them obey the command" Thou shalt worship the Lord thy God and Him only shalt thou serve." Secondly, it was essential that they should have a clear conviction and firm belief. There were those who thought it was a sign of intel- lectual strength to doubt about matters of religion. It was a mistake, for doubt was ever an element of weak- ness. The men who had worked for God and for I humanity had been men profoundly convinced of the eternal verities of the gospel. Thirdly, there must be a smareme love for the work they had chosen. Hers- minded them of the words" If ye love me, keep my commandments" ; this implied that without the love of God it was useless to endeavour to keep His com- mandments. It was imnossible to live Christian lives unless they were Christians. Miss F. G. Hiller then de- liehted the congregation with her rendering of" Come unto MP," As had been announced, a collection in aid of the distress was made, and realized the very satis- factory sum of £5 2s. 63. A further selection by the orchestral band brought to a close a most successful service, and one which doubtleqt added another link to the already long chain of popularity which the People's Services have gained for themselves. P.S.A. AT WYCLIFFE.</p>
        <p>Last Sunday T17.-ii-e-d thø c;i7n-PietiO-1; of the second year of a movement which has been steadily gaining 'ground in Warrington,and which has now reached such proportions that some effect upon the character of the town's morals may be looked for at no very distant date. It was reported at the meeting that during the year of the commencement ef the Wycliffe P.S. A. 1,150 members were enrolled, and on the registers which were compiled for the past year there are the names of 1,203 members. The chair on Sunday afternoon was taken by Mr. A. Birch, and the speaker was Mr. G. B. Birdsall, Whalley Range, Manchester, who delivered an address to which the audience listened with groat attention. Mr. W. Waterfield was the soloist and Mr. George ‘Vallington read the lesson, while Mr. Irish engaged in prayer. The attendance was one of the largest recorded during the year. GREAT BARGAINS in FURi ; the largest and beat assorted Stock in Warrington.—Evatts', 17 Sankey-street.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>lIA.LTON.</head>
        <p>lIA.LTON.</p>
        <p>INDEPENDENT ORDER OF ODDFELLOWS.—The balance sheet for the Loyal Earl of Chester Lodge in connecti&lt; n with the above order has just been issued. It appears that the year commenced with a balance in hand of , £459 18s. 6d., and with contributiows amounting to £B9 6s. 4d. ; interest £lB 41. 41., and funeral money from district £2O ; the total receipts were £585 9s. 2d. The expenditure included 119 week's sick pay at 10s., £59 10g., two weeks at 55.. 103. ; member's wife £lO, riistrict funeral fund £24 103. 4d., making a total of £O4 10s. 4d., and leaving a balance to be carried for- ward of £4BO 18s. 103. The management fund expenses amounted to £3O 83. Bid., which left a balance in hand of £3 10s. 31. The lodge comprises 91 members, the average age is 31 years, and £5 8.1. is the average worth per member. The aueitors of the accounts were Messrs. R. K. Jeacock and F. Fryer. The trustees are Mes,rs. John Barrow, and G. Leatliwood, Mr. Henry Hale is secretary, and Mr. Henry Povev, treasurer.</p>
        <p>DEATH OF A WELL-KNOWN HALTONTAN.—Mra. Jane Kingston, 68 years of age, who resided at 37 Percival- lane,Runcorn, died some days age,and her remains were interred in the cemetery on Saturday. For thirty years, Mr. and Mrs. Kingston were very prominent residents in Halton, and during that time they occupied the Norton Arms and the Castle Hotel. On her husband's death about fourteen years ago,she removed to Runcorn where she lived in retirement. She was well-known for her charitable disposition and greatly respected.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>ST. LUKE'S SUNDAY SCHOOL.</head>
        <p>ST. LUKE'S SUNDAY SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>The annual tea meeting and entertainment in connection with St. Luke's Sunday School was held on Wednesday evening. There was a large attend- ance. The tables were presided over by Mrs. Lowe, Mrs. Lacy, MN. Clarke, Mrs. Dutton, Miss Slade and Miss Owen. At the entertaiumeat which followed, the Rev. J. Simpson presided. A number of recita- tions were given by G. Lloyd, F. Dutton, J. Brad- bury, Nellie Jones, B. Brown, J. Wilding, S. For- shaw. M. E. Williamson, Lena Brown; H. Dutton, and P. Birch. Solos were sung by Miss L. Ellis, Miss M. Moore, Miss Lacy and Mr. G. Lacy. Mr. A. Humphreys contributed a clarionet solo, and a solo on the mandoline was given by Mr. J. Rollin- son. A pleasant evening was spent.</p>
