        <p>THE LADIES' YEAR BOOK. A most Useful - Compendium for the Household, containing Diary, Household Accounts and Washing Books, besides a - mass of Useful luformation for the Home. Post Free ls. 6d. UMBRELLAS, GLOVES, GENTS' TIES, and SCARVES. SEALSKIN JACKETS, FUR MUFFS AND BOAS. DRESS MATERIALS, Ac., For Presents.</p>
        <p>GEORGE lIENRY LEE &amp; CO., BASNETT STREET,</p>
        <p>LIVERPOOL.</p>
        <p>IMPERIAL PA RLIMENT.</p>
        <p>Continued from page 6: THURSDAY. In the House of Commons on Thursday evening Mr. ASQUITH informed Mr. J. Ems that lie hoped to intro- duce at an early date a bill dealing with fiery, and dusty coalmines.</p>
        <p>In answer to Sir W. WEDDERBMIN, Sir W. Fleaconar said that, with the SECRETARY for INDIA, he had come to the conclusion that the costs of the Opium Commis- sion ouakt to be borne by the English and not by the Indian Government.</p>
        <p>Sir W. Mai/comwr moved that until Easter, Govern.— meat business should have priority on Tuesdays and at morning sittings to be held on Fridays. Sir Molest._ HICKS-BEACH said he did not feel inclined to take away private members' time in order that the Government might introduce bills which were never intended to be -. passed, but were merely introduced as baits to- attrack certain members into the lobby in sup- port of bills for which a majority could be ob- tained in no other way. Mr. J. REDMOND asked when the Irish Land Bill will be brought on, and said that in the absence of information on this point he should ab- stain from voting. Mr. Latorcnkku said he was not prepared to vote for the motion. On a division Sfe W. ELtaconer's motion was carried by 236 to 221. The debate on the motion for leave to bring in the Welsh Disestablishment Bill was resumed by Lord Woutbn, who said that everything which had been given 'th the Church since the Reformation was given to it as it existed now, and to take away such property was nothing but sp,.ilation. After speeches from seve- ral members of the Opposition, Sir R. WEBSTER com- plained o. ha "conspiracy of silence" on the part of the Governnielit. Mr. BRYCE explained that the Govern- ment thought that fuller argument should IV deferred to the secend reading of the measo-• ind said it was not true that the Bill was levelle: .s.; the Church of England, which would remain ed and untouched in every respect. The motion t-, ,ave to, bring in the measure was passed without a di'. .sinn, aryl the Bill was then read a first time. Mr. AsQUITH sa'l the seoond reading would not be taken for a fortnigl,-. Mr. MORLEY' stated that on Monday he would ask leave to bring in the Irish Land Bill.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>SHIP CANAL,</head>
        <p>SHIP CANAL,</p>
        <p>HALF-YEARLY MEETING OF SHAREHOLDERS. The half-yearly meeting of the Manchester Ship Canal Company was held on Thursday in the Gentlemen's Concert Hall, under the presidency of Mr. J. B. Brthell. The revenue from the Canal, the Chairman said, during the half year was £49,578, an increase of £9,455 on the previous half-year. Although, of course, it was to be admitted that this was a very small amount of revenue for so great an undertaking—£9o,ooo for the year,--they must bear in mind the circumstances. He could with confidence say that although the canal did not yet show any large increased traffic returns, distinct progress was being made In many directions. The *ld routes naturally did not yield up traffic without a struggle, and there were byways in con- nection with business, and sometimes private arrangements which, though secret, were sometimes very powerful and difficult to get over. He was glad to- report that of late they had seen a very considerable dis- position on the part of Manchester merchants to help the canal. If Manchester traders and others would remem- ber the benefits they had received from the canal eater- prise,and entnbine to let it have a steady and continuous flow of traffic, its success would be all that had been anticipated. After explainiag the financial position, the Chairman said they seemed to be driven to one conclu-• sion—if they did net spend their remaining, capital now on increased equipment they would be quite unable to- cope with a greatly increased volume of traffic when it cam. Eveni if they had the money at their disposal whenncrrss theaccommodation, thoefy twhoeuldtralodstdraemffiac and revenue during the period of the construction of such increased accommodation, and traffic turned away for i emergencies the want of the necessary facilities for dealing with it might not be easily brought back to the port when the facilities were provided. All this pointed to the neces- sity for some arrangements being made in regard to the payment of interest on some of the Company's debenture loans. lie had not, however, any information to give at present on this part of the question, because no negotia- tions had yet been discussed by the Board, but he con- sidered it to he his duty to draw attention to the fact that the questiqn would hve to he dealt with immediately. The report was unanimously adopted</p>
      </div>
