      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>EARLESTOWN WESLEYAN CHAPEL.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN WESLEYAN CHAPEL.</p>
        <p>LIFE IN MASHONALAND. In connection with the above place of worship, special sermons were preached in the chapel on Sunday morning and evenino,by the Rev.lsaac Shimmin,missionary from Mashonalai7d. There were large congregations at each service, at which the choir rendered special selections of music, in the evening, Miss May Clare singing the sacred solo, "Rejoice greatly," from the Messiah. The dis- courses of the r . gentleman delivered in a capable and interesting m nor,. were intently listened to and thoroughly en• • ad: On Monday iening the Rev. Isaac Simmin gave an interesting addVess in the chapel to a verylarge audience, on " Varied life and startling experiences in Mashona- land." Mr. Thomas Ormand presided, he was accom- panied on the platform by the Revs. J. Barley and Isaac Sitimmin. The Rev. J. Bartley said the collection which would be made at the close was on behalf of their local contingent, which was indefinite for anything. Nobody in that chapel knew what their local contingent fund was, unless it was the stewards and himself, but there were certain current expenses in connection with a church, which could not be charged to anything con- nected with that church. The contingent fund had been in debt to the society stewards for about two years, to the tune of about £l2, and it was too much to expect the society stewards to do their duty to the chore), and still be out of pocket for two years to the extent of Zl2.</p>
        <p>The Chairman, after expressing the pleasure it gave him to be in that position, called upon the Rev. Isaac Shimmin who said his object was to convey as much information to their minds, about Christ's work in Africa, as he could in the address he was about to give. Mashonaland had been very much to the front during the last year. People's geographical ideas about Africa were very vague and indistinct. When they spoke of South Africa, they spoke of a Continent, and when they spoke of Mashonaland, they spoke of an area as large as Germany, France, and Austria put together. He thought Mashonaland, was the coming country in the next few years. America had been filled up, Australia was filling up, Africa was left.—Did they get the fever out there? Of course they did,along with the gold, and if there was gold there, men would risk the fever in the hopes of going home in a short time and building a villa in the suburbs of London, and living the rest of their lives in peace. Had they ever come across Bing Solomon's mines? Of course they had in in Rider Haggard's book ; not outside.—All over the centre of Africa, they had copper working, and gold working, and old ruins, tell- ing them that at one time a semi-civilised people lived in Africa. When he got to Salisbury in September 1891, he found there was one man who wore collars, and he explained how expensive provisions and other things were, which was owing to the cost of carriage. Speak- ing of the opening up of the country, he said they now had a telegraph wire going right up from Cape Town to Salisbury. They had a weekly newspaper,and anything special was posted outside the newspaper office. They had a railway working in from the East coast from the Portuguese territory, and 17,17 soon that line would be extended up to Salisbury, and it would shortly be no difficult matter to step on to a train going right down to the East Coast, and embark straight on the steamer for home, and they would soon have a service from Bulawayo to Cape Town. They were becoming civil- ised. Bulawayo was not more than 15 months old, and there was now about 2,000 people there, and about 200 ladies, and some Americans had got a concession to lay down an electric light, and water works. They had actually put up a gallows and hung two men. They had hospitals, reading-reoms, masonic lodge, small hotels, and many other advan- tages of civilised life. If Dr. Livingstone was alive to- day, he would be delighted at the opening up of Central Africa. In Mr. Shimmiu'a opinion the telegraph would do more to kill slavery, and to open up that portion of Central Africa than the British gunboats which sailed up and down the Coast of Africa. He then proceeded to give interesting accounts of conversations with the Mashonas who until the white man appeared on the scene were the favourite victims of the Matabele,and the Mashonas were forced to seek refuge in the hills. All this had now been altered,and the Mashonas could work peaceably in the valleys, and he recounted how expert the natives were in the making of farming implements with the rude means at their disposal. Alluding to his missionary work, he said he happened to be in the Transvaal in November 1890, when Mr. Rhodes was passing through the town where he was staying. He , was the great man of the Chartered Company, and was now the uncrowned king of Africa. The speaker went to see him, and asked him to open the door for the. Wesleyan Church, and to do what he could to further the interests of Methodism. Mr. Rhodes said in reply, " I grant your request wil- lingly, I give you money,so much a year ; I promise you land in the town for your churches and schools ; I will do all I can to help You, and I hope you will go as a pioneer to the North." Continuing, he said Mr. Rhodes had done more for all churches up there than any Government or company or official had done in any other part of the world, and when a man had done that, however they might differ as regards their politics, at any rate they cculd say. " Thank you." He went ois to give some of his experiences in travelling in Africa, and said that on one of his journeys he met Lord Randolph Churchill,who was even then failing in health, but he could only say that when they did meet him he could not have been more genial. In Septerriber, 1891, they got to Salisbury, and built a church there for about £5OO or £6OO and without a penny in debt and opened it just when the Matabele war was breaking out. There were now hundreds of native preachers willing to go into the interior of Africa to preach the Gospel. After detailing some terrible experiences he and others had undergone just twelve months ago when Maki'. Forbes Wilson lost his life by being drowned in a swiftly running river, he concluded by telling a lion story. in which he was personally concerned, for the benefit ot the children who were present. The address was gives in a quaint, homely fashion,and often caused much merriment.</p>
        <p>During the evening a couple of sacred solos were sung by Miss E. CoHinge and Mr. F. P. Clare. A vote of thanks to the speaker and chairman terminated the proceedings.</p>
        <p>CONGREGATIONAL CHURCH, NEWTON.</p>
        <p>The annual church meeting was held in the Congrega- tional schoolroom on Thursday evening. The proceed- ings were initiated with a tea, to which about 180 persons were present, the tables being presided over by the lady members of the congregation. After tea the meeting was presided over by the Rev. James Allatt, who said h. was glad to see so large a number, able and willing to be present at another annual meeting. If those meetings were named according to the general incidents associated with them, he feared that one would be known in future as the frozen out annual, seeing that every drop of water required that night had to be carried on to the premises. It was to him, after a period of so many of those annual meet- ings, a subject of thankfulness that he was able to look upon so many of the faces that had met him 32 years ago. It was a matter of gratitude also that he should think of the many more who had passed away from them, the memory of whom was blessed, and whose lives they were reminded of again and again were operative for good in some who were still living in various places. The chief event in 1894 was that of the renovation of the church, the needful altera- tions that were made, and an entirely new system of lighting, which had become quite necessary in ortrer to save the place from going more and more into a ruinous condition. It was in one sense a work of faith, inasmuch as the work had to be done first, and the money to pay for it to be obtained afterwards. The idea came to some of their friends to put themselves in communication with old scholars, to do as much as they possitily could, and the responses had been most gratifying, showing that the memory of that place still lived with those scattered in different parts of the world. A sale of work had been arranged for tube held at the beginning of April, and it was one which justified their faith in the works just commenced. The old infants' schoolroom had been in the hands of the decorators, the walls had been carefully prepared, and the ceiling decorated,and now it formed a kind of church parlour,and asindicating the good young life about them he might say that the whole of the expense connected with it had been entirely met by the young people I themselves. (Hear, hear.) Referring to the binding of the magazines in connection with the Sunday School, he had to announce that £5 had this year been paid by the scholars in order that they might have an especially attractive binding, showing that they had grown to ap- preciate the work done for them, and were becoming more and more artistic in their taste. Passing from matters of that kind, some might ask whether, after all, they were adding to the forces that make for righteous- ness, and he could give to any questien of that kind a very emphatic yes. Some of the papers that were read from time to time by their young friends had awakened in him at first a feeling of surprise, arid for some time past now the papers had been so excellent, that if they were not so he should have a feeling of disappointment. There were many who were beginning to feel that they had something to do in life to make it pure and fresh, and strong and healthy. (Applause.) Mr. M. Battershy in his school report, said the attend- ance for the year was 160 scholars and 16 teachers, but durieg the past year they had the misfortune to lose I heir bate and esteemed Superintendent Mr. Holmes, from circumstances whiah they all regretted, especially as it was brought about by ill-health. On the other hand there was a pleasant circumstance,—they had se- cured the services of two teachers.and on the whole the condition of the school was satisfactory. (Applause.) Miss Allhtt. in her report of the Young People's Society of Christian Endeavour, said 1894 was the best and most successful year they had had since they began in 1891. They had eight honorary members, 28 active members, seven associated members. In the juniors they had 14 active members and six trial members, the total mem- bership of the society being 63,,and average attendance 45 at the meetings. Mr. John Owen, treasurer, in submitting the financial statement, said the amount of money raised by the Church during the year was £587 us. 91 and expendi- ture £752 12s. 9d., the excess in expenditure over receipts being due to the extra amount spent in the s decoration of the church.</p>
