      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>BEFORE T.HE MA_GISTRATES.</head>
        <p>BEFORE T.HE MA_GISTRATES.</p>
        <p>STREET RTJIPTIANISH. At the Borough Police Court, on Friday, before Alder- man Harrison (chairman), Alderman Wright, Councillor Roberts end D.- 13 nall, John Ball, 13, Philip-street, John Green, 32, Philip-street, William Glover, Silver- street, were all charged with assaulting, on the 16th Feb. in Warrington, John Woodward, Watkin-street.—John Woodward stated that he was going down Watkin-street on Saturday night, when he noticed a crowd round some wen lighting. Be went to see who they were when Green rushed to him and took off his coat, offering to fight witness. Green then struck witness and was followed isy the other two who attacked him on the ground, Glover saying that he had been waiting for " this chance" a good while.—Lizzie Pemberton said that on the Saturday night she was coming from her brother's house about 11.45 when she saw the defendants striking Green. When one finished another commenced and complainant never struck back.—Defendants were ordered to keep the peace for 6 months in their own recognizances.</p>
        <p>THE BOARD OF GUARDIANS AND RELIEF QUESTION IN COURT. On Fi iday morning at the Borough Police Court, before Alderman Barrisoo and other magistrates, Walter Clarke, 26 Ashton-st., was charged with unlawfully deserting his wife Mary Clarke, 19 Clare-st., en and since the 9th of August, 1894.—Defendant pleaded not guilty. —Mary Clarke, the complainant, said that she and her husband had been married nearly three years and defendant had deserted her on the 9th of August. He went to live with his mother and complainant not hav- ing any money to pay the rent lett the house they were living in and took lodgings. Defendant only lived a few streets away and she had been to him and asked him for money but he never gave her any saying that he had none. Complainant however knew he was working.— The Chairman of the Bench advised complainant to go to the relieviog officer and tell him that her husband con- tributed nothing to her support and the Boa rd of Guar- dians would take steps to make him pay.—Complainant said she had been once to the Guardians and was told to come to the Court.—lt was stated that the Board of Guardians were unwilling CO give support outside the workhouse to married women.—Dr. Gornall : If I was a respectable woman I would not go to tne workhouse, and if that is the law of England the sooner it is altered the better.—Complaint said she was averse to going to the workhouse but ultimately consented to see the relieving officer again and state that she had been re- commended to do so by the Bench.—The case against defendant was thereupon dismissed.</p>
        <p>STRANGE{ CONDUCT IN • PUBLIC-HOUSE. At the Warrington Borough Court on Monday, Edward Massey, 28 Horsemarket-st., was charged with unlaw- fully being on the licensed premises of the White Bull, during the time that it was by law required to be closed, on February 21.—Inspector Cos said that at ten past one, on the morning February 21, he was in Horse- , market-st., in company with sergeant Tailor, when they heard scuffling inside the White Bull. They went , to the door, and heard some one tell a man to go home. The door was opened, and defendant was seen inside in a state of drunkenness. Witness haked the landlady bow she accounted for the man being there, wild she replied [has be had forced his way through the front door shortly after eleven o'clock, and they had been try- ing to get him out since that time. He had not been served with any drink.—The Chief Constable said that the defendant bad been married some time ago and set ' up business as a butcher not far from the White Bull, in which public-house he sometimes went. The landlady had told him (the Chief Constable) that the r:ason she did not call iu the police was because they did not wish to get the defendant into trouble. The defendant had also said that he aas not aware that he was committing any offence as he had not got any drink from the house. He however promised that it should never occur again. —The magistrates fined defendant £1 and costs, the Chairman saying that such conduct as that of defendant placed publicans in a very awkward position.</p>
        <p>A PUBLICAN FINED. John Shaw, the publican at the Harp and Crown, was 11 charged at the Warrington Borough Court on Monday, with being drunk in his licensed premises on February 14.—Inspector Bowen said that on the above date, he was in the police office, and heard a disturbance going on in Irlam-st. He went out tc see what was the matter, and saw Mrs. Shaw running screaming out of the house into Mr. Horrock's shop. Witness went into the Harp and Crown along with P.S. Smith, and in the taproom, they found the defendant drunk. He was leaning ' against the counter, and when he saw the officers, he swore at them asking them what business they had there. They told him that he must behave himself, but on going out,he followed them to the door using abusive language.—P S. Smith gave corroborative evidence.— The Chief Constable said that the defendant was a glass blower by trade, and the fact of being out of work seemed to have troubled him a lot. Ile howev, r pr., mised not to offend again.—Defendant was fined 10s. and costs. SERIOUS CHARGE AGAINST A SCHOOLGIRL. Sarah Allcock, 11, River-road, was charged at the Warrington Borough Court on Tuesday, with stealing from the cloak room of the People's College, on Feb. 12, a girl's sable fur cape, valued £3, the proerty of Alfred Perris, 24, Arpley-at. Mr. Hula said that the cloak belonged t 3 May Perris, aged 13 years. who at- tended the People's College school. The defendant. who was only 11 years of age, also went to the same school. On the above date, May Perils went to the school wear- ing the cape. She hung it in the cloak room and put her jacket over it. Alter school time, she went for her cloak and cape, but found the cape missing. She at once informed Miss Brown and Mr. Twiss, but they could not find it anywhere. The next morning, Detec- tive Monaghan was informed. Every possible enquiry was made but nothing was discovered until Saturday afternoon, when May Perris saw Maggie Allcock, sister to the defendant, wearing a cape similar to hers. She ran after her, and told her that the cape was hers. Maggie Allcock said that she had borrowed the cape from next door and th,ri ran away. May Perris told her mother that Maggie Allcock had got her cape. De- tective Monaghan was then informed. Monaghan went to Allcock's house in River-road, and there saw the father, who said that he recollected some days ago hear- ing of a cape that had been found, but he had taken no notice. He left such things to his wife. Mrs. Ancock came in soon afterwards, and said that her daughter Sarah, had found a cape on the Gymnasium steps as she was coming home from school. Upon defendant being questioned about the matter, she said that some girls saw her pick up the cape,but could not give any of their names.—May Perris gave corroborative evidence to the Chief Constable's statement.—Monaghan also gave evi- dence to finding the cape. and said that the defendant admitted hearing Mr.Twiss,the sehoolmaster, informing the scholars that a cape had been lost, and asking them whether any of them knew about it.—The, mother of defendant said that when her daughter came home with the cape, she was positive that she had found it on the Gynmasium steps. She had intended advertising the cape in the local papers, to try to find the owner.—Coun- eillor Roberts, who was the presiding magistrate, said that the bench considered that Mr. and Mrs. Allcock were very much to blame in the matter, as they ought • to have reported the case to the police at once, espe- cially as a police officer lived close by. There was no excuse whatever Of their conduct. 'fbe Bench did not wish to record a conviction against the little girl, and would not do so if the mother would pay the costs, 12c. Mrs. Allcock agreed to this course.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="4" unit="words">
