      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>EXAMINER, SAT URDAY, MARCH 2, 1895.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SAT URDAY, MARCH 2, 1895.</p>
        <p>CWILMIN AGAINST • ralf•LS PICIPOCILKT. At the Borough Police Court on Thursday morning be- ' fore the Mayor and Councillor H. Roberts, Sarah Dig- man was brought up on remand and charged with steal- ing from the person of Louisa Elizabeth Hughes in the Warrington Market, on Wednesday the 20th inst.,a purse containing 12s. Id. in money and 3 co-operative checks. —Mr. Hunt (Chief Constable), stated that the charge was pretty much the same as before, and he had found that the prisoner was very well known and had been often dealt with. He had written to Leeds and found that there, was no such person known as the prisoner at the address given by her. Mr. Hunt had also written te Liverpool and received a letter from the Chief Constable there enclosing a " photo" of prisoner and her charac- ter. which said that there she was known as Ann Maria, alias " Cocks," and had been living in Circus-st. there. She was known at several other places, and Mr. Hunt asked that prisoner might be committed te the Liver- pool Quarter Sessions.—Louisa Elizabeth Hughes, 86, Long,ford-st., complainant, identified the purse produced as hers, and repeated the evidence given in ourcolumns last week, with the addition that on losing told that her purse was stolen by prisoner, complainant went to the latter and asked her if she had got the purse, and prisoner said " No, I ha,vn't." She held prisoner till Detec- tive Sergeant Monaghan arrived and took her in charge, afterwards finding the purse on the floor.—Agnes Mas- kall, 7, Edgerten-st., said she was in the market at 330 on the day in question,and saw the prisoner deliber- ately go behind complainant, lift up her jacket, and take out the purse. Witness then seized prisoner, and with assistance searched her, and afterwards felt the purse drop on her foot.—Elizabeth Atherton, 40, Hopwood-st., gave similar evidence to last witness.—Detective Monaghan deposed to receiving the purse from the eorn- plamant on the 20th, and brought prisoner to the police , station and charged her, she replying "I never saw any purse."—After hearieg the evidence prisoner said she 5 had nothing to say, but afterwards pleaded guilty. She was committed to the Liverpool Quarter Sessions.</p>
        <p>DARESBURY PETTY SESSIONS. TUESDAY.—Before Mr. J. C. Parr (chairman), Messrs.W.</p>
        <p>, . C. Jones, H. Nield, and J. Mclntyre. UNLICENSED Dos.—The following persons were dealt with for keeping dogs without licenses :—Walter Curzon, 2nd Feb., fined the costs ; James Smith, 4th Feb., Preston Brook, fined 7s. 6d. ; John Squires, Preston Brook, 7s. Bd. ; Edward Boswell, 4th Feb., Preston Brook, 7s. 6d.; Samuel Lunt,same date and place,7s. 6d.; James Williams. same date and place, 7s. 6d. ; James Johnson, 11th Feb., Grappenhall, 3.,. 6d. ALLEGED ASSAULT: PALTRY CASE.—Frarat Meld ,of Warrington, was charged with assaulting Edward Brockleburst, at Stretton, on the 2nd of Feb. Mr. W. H. J. Jenkins,solicitor oppeared for Nield who had taken out a cross-summons against. Brocklehurst for assaulting him. From the evidence adduced it appeared that on the day in question there W3S a pigeon shooting match at Stretton, and from which a number of men, Nield among them, had arrived at the " Cat and Lim," and noticed a milk cart at the door of. 8 shippon belonging to Brocklehurst. Nield said he would have a drink, and putting the can to his lips he did so, some of it, however, running down his clothes. At this juncture Brockle- hurst came out and high vverds ensued. Brocklehurst was given 6d., and Niel 4 turning round, it is stated, struck him in the eye. A youth bore out this statement. —The del ence went to show that Brocklehurst was the first to strike a blow, and Charles Parker, Warrington, deposed to seeing Brocklehurst strike Nield at the back of the neck- This was confirmed by three other wit- nesses. The magistrates after some ;consideration dis- missed the case, saying that it was a paltry one and ought never to have come into court.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>MINO It CASES.</head>
        <p>MINO It CASES.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOROUGH COURT. FRlDLY.—Before Alderman Harrison (chairman), Alder- man Wright, Councillor Roberts and Dr. Gornall. NEGLECT OF CRILDREN.—SamueI and Annie Beckett, 18, Haydock-st., not appearing after an adjournment of one month,in order to allow of imprqvement in the con- dition of their family. Inspector McAteer, N.S.P.C.C., said that he had visited defendant's house several times since the first adjournment and there had been no im- provement whatever, in fact the case had worsened. On his application the case was adjourned for another month. ALLEGED CRUELTY TO a FlonsE.—John Bolan, Dallarn- lane, was charged with illtre ating on the B.h February,a horse by working tl'e ame while in an unfit state.—De- fendant wanted the We adjourned for a week so that Mr. Shaw-cross who was one of his witnesses could appear. This was granted on the condition that he pay Mr. J. Dixon,veterinary surg,eon,los. 6d.costs,as he had appeared as a witness for the prosecution. After some demur defendant agreed to this.</p>
        <p>- - MONDA.Y.—Before the Mayor (Councillor J Fair- dough), Alderman Harrison, and Mr. J. W. Hayward. DRITNIIINNESS.—For this offence, Patrick For, 3, Robinson's-yard,was fined 10s. and costs; Patrick Burns, 21, Erwood-street, ss. including costs ; Jane Johnson, 9, Silver-square, adjourned bra month. A VIOLENT PRISONER.—Thomas Kelly, 17, Shipiard, was charged with being drunk and disorderly in Golden Grove-street on Saturday night, also with assaulting Charles Jones and P.S. Taylor at the same time and place. He was further charged with being drunk and disorderly on the previous Monday in Ship-yard, P.C. Butler said that owing to the violent conduct of the prisoner he was obliged to take him in custody and call the assistance et P.S, Taylor, and two young men, of whom Charles Jones was one. The prisoner continued his violent behaviour and kicked P.S. Taylor on the legs, and Jones on the chest. The Chief Constable said that he considered that the assault upon Jones was the worst feature of the case, as such conduct prevented people from helping the police. P.C. Evans gave evidence to the drunkenness of the prisoner on Monday. There were a large number of convictions against the prisoner pre- viously, and the prisoner was sent. to gaol for three months. CHIMNEYS ON FIRE.—For having their chimneys on fire, Joseph Horlock, Rope and Anchor Inn, and Thorns. Henry Poole, Sankey-street, were fined Is. and costs, 75. in all. A NEGLIGENT DRlVER.—Patrick O'Donnell, Foundry- street,was charged with being at too great a distance from his horse and cart to have sufficient control thereof, in Bridge-street, on February 16. P.C. Bell stated that the defendant's horse and cart were going over Warrington Bridge, and defendant was walking ten or twelve yards away. When passing the end of Mersey- street, witness had to turn the horse at one side, or a collision would have taken place between it and another conveyance. Defendant was quite sober. The magis- trates inflicted a fine of 2s. fid. PIGEON FLYERS LT VARIANCE.--William Allen, 21, Crosley-street,and Thomas Moran, 18, Back Crosley-st., were charged with fighting in School-Brow on Feb. 17th. P.C. Bell in proving the case, said that the prisoners were flying pigeons, and they had a quarrel about them. The prisoners were bound over for six months, them- selves in £5, and also one surety of £5. TIIESDAY.—Before Councillor H. Roberts (chairman),and</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>Dr. T._Starkey Smith.</head>
        <p>Dr. T._Starkey Smith.</p>
        <p>•__ . _ DESERTEH.—Frederick Calligan was remanded to Strangeways Gaol to await escort for deserting from H.M. Bth King's Liverpool Regiment since 20th Dec., 1893.—P.C. Jones. who preyed the case, was recom- mended for the usual reward. DRUNK IN THE PARK.—William Stephenson was charged was charged with being drunk in the park on the previous afternoon. Mr. Ashton, the park keeper, said that the prisoner, who was very drunk, came int. the park the previous afternoon and shut the gate, and acted otherwise strangely. He refused to give any 11E• count of himself, and witness was obliged to tike him into custody, When passing the White Hart Inn, Sankey-street, he persisted in going in, and the assist- ance of a policeman had to be obtained to take him to the police station.—Prisoner was fined 10s. and costs.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN PETTY SESSIONS. MONDAY.—Before Sir Richard M. Brooke and Mr. Thomas Handley. WITHOUT DOG LICENSIIS. —For keeping dogs without licenses the following were fined Is. and costs :—Charles Rice, Weston Point ; James Robinson, Weston Point ; George Goodall, Clifton ; Henry Peacock, 28, Lord-st., Runcorn ; William Carroll, 40, York-st. ; Harry Price, 6, Peel-pt. ; Newton, 1, Robert-st. ; and Thomas Jones, 51, Cooper-st. Dammt AND DISORDERLY.—George Dennis was fined 54. and costs,or in default seven days' imprisonment,for being drunk and disorderly in 4;reenwsy-road, Runcorn, on the 23rd inst.</p>
        <p>NEWTON PETTY SESSIONS. SATURDAY —BOOM Mr. T. J. Gillespie (chairman), CalonelJ. L. Wood, Mr. C. B. F. Borron, and Mr. S.S.</p>
        <p>Brown. DRUNK AND DISORDERLY. —For this offence, Patrick Halton, Newton, was fined ss. and costs; Henry Sutton, Haydock. 2s. 6d.; William Taylor, Golborne, ss. and coats. Doo OWNERS FINED.—John Swift, Newton, was fined 3s. 6d., for allowing his dog to be at large unmuzzled, on February 10th. John Phosnix, Golborne, was fined a similar amount for the same offence committed, on February 19th. No NAME ON CART.—Alexander Richard Taylor, News- ton, was fined Is. and costs for allowing a cart to be used upon the highway without his name being painted thereon. P.S. Longworth proved the case. The same officer proved a charge against James Dagnall, Newton, for not having sufficient control over his horse and cart, on February 170. Dagnall was fined 2s. ed. and costs. INFRINGEMENT OF THE SWINE FEVER ORDER. —Richard 1 Mayor, Wigan, was charged with infringing the Swine Fever Order, by removing four pies from Wigan to New- ton-le-Willows, on February 13th, without having ..btained license authorising him to do so. Inspector Morgan gave evidence for the charge,and defendant was fined 10s. including costs. PaosactiTioNs UNDER THE WEIGHTS AN) MEASURES ACT. —Joseph Nuttall, Newton, was charged with taking out his cart containing sacks of coal, without having metal labels affixed thereto, on February 11th, contrary to the Weights and Measures Act. Inspector Spencer proved the case, and defendant was ordered to pay costs.—John Cunliffe, Newton, was similarly dealt with for commit- ting the same offence on the same date.--Lot Hughes was charged with having a pair of scales in his posses- sion, on February 9th, at Golborne, which were unjust. Inspector Brian said that the scales were two ounces out of balance. The defendant said that he was not aware that his scales were wrong at all and produced certifica, dated February sth, that the scales were all right. The magistrates dismissed the case on payment ot Costs, 2s. 6,1. IRREGULAR ATTENDANCE AT SCHOOL.—Peter Jones was charged with not sending kis child regularly to school at Golborne. Mr. Henry Pegg, the school attendance onhier, said that the child should have attended school 260 times, but had only attended five., Defendant was tint d ss. William Roberts was also fined ss. for not sending his two children regularly to school at Golborne.</p>
        <p>SKINS ON FIRE! SKIN 3 ON FIRE! With tortur- ing, disfiguring eczemas, and every species of distresS- log skin and scalp diseases relieved by a single applica- tion, and speedily cured by the CUTICURA REMEDIES when the best physicians, hospitals, and all other remedies fail. To those who have suffered long and ' hopelessly,and who have lost faith in dmtors,medicities, and all things human, CIITICIIILA, REMBDIES appeal with a force never before realized in the history of medicine. Every hope, every expectation awakened by them, has been more than fulfilled. Their I.ICCNIS has excited the wonder and approval of physicians familiar with the marvellous cures daily effected by them. They have friends in every quarter of the civilised world. People in every walk of life believe in them, use them, and recommend them. They are, in truth, the greatest skin cure, blood purifier,and humour remedy of modern times.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="3" unit="words">
