      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>THINGS HEARD AND SEEN</head>
        <p>THINGS HEARD AND SEEN</p>
        <p>AT WAR RINGTO N.</p>
        <p>Not long ago I commented upon the gratifying slackness of business at the Police Court, but after a month's inactivity has come a rush of cases en- tirely out of the ordinary. Every day has had its quota of crimes big and little, while the County Petty Sessions and Occasional Court have also 'come out in a manner which is almost alarming. Evi- dently it is no use our congratulating ourselves on the decrease of criminal offences in Warrington for long, for almost immediately the Court is full again, and everything as lively as ever. Still it would not be just, to judge Warrington by the number of people brought up at the Police Court, for quite half the prisoners are strangers and only use " the good old town " as a place to steal enough to carry them on t he next stopping place. Mr. Hunt (Chief Constable) truly observed the other day that Warrington was used as a half-way house by the thieves and other bad characters of at least 10 towns larger than this within a 50 miles radius. In many cases it seems to be the method of proce- dure of the light figured gentry to make a round of all the neighbouring towns, stealing and picking pockets, just to " keep their hand in."</p>
        <p>Although the annual report of the Chief Constable which is issued last week has a dry official appear- ance,t here are some interesting pieces of information to be extracted from it. I notice for instance that that the Chief Constable has now given over thirty years services, and that of the four inspectors not one has been in the force for a less period than twenty-two years. The sergeants have also all put in a fair term of service, but there is only one of the forty-three constables who has given so much as twenty-one years' service. The force has been exceptionally well-conducted during the year as there have been fewer reports against the men than in any previous year.</p>
        <p>A rather promnent member of the Board of Guar- dians passed away last week on Saturday evening. I allude to Mr. George Ratcliffe. This gentleman has occupied the chairmanship of the Board and was probably the most regular attendant at its meetings. He was very pertinacious in his manner, and when be had a grievance at all, he refused to let it alone until he had seen it righted, or had some sufficient explanation given. It was this pertinacity which gave him his prominence on the Board. Ile was not an orator in any sense of the word, but still he had good business capacity, and it was in recognition of this, that he was elected chairman some time ago. Ile was always to the fore when- ever Earlestown and Newton questions came up for discussion, and he was always a bold champion for Earlestown. Fitting notice of Mr. Ratcliffe's death was taken at the meeting of the Guardians on Wednesday, and a unanimous vote of condolence with the widow and her family was passed. Mr. Ratcliffe will be much missed on the Board, for, in addition to being so regular in his attendance, he had something to say on almost every subject which came up for discussion.</p>
        <p>A welcome contribution was made to the relief fund on Thursday last week by the proceeds of the comic:football match, which was played on the Rug- by Football ground. The match itself was provoca- tive of great amusement. It is usually expected that a footballer has a lithe and active body, but it was an eye-opener to see one or two players who were almost as tall when they lay on the floor as when they were standing up. The market men were no match for the shopkeepers, however, al- though the score of three to two does not look very crushing, but Mr. Pritchard, the shopkeepers' goal- keeper, deserted his side and went to Mr. Place's assistance, so that there were practically twelve market men to ten shopkeepers. The crowd around the field was kept in a very good humour,and should another match be arranged for a similar object it would no doubt be again well patronised.</p>
        <p>The Parish Council of Great Sankey have had considerable difficulty—and they have not yet sur- mounted it—in getting possession of ths old parish chest, which contains papers relative to the rights of the township. This parish chest, which has not been opened for 50 years, is at present at the Old Chapel House,andithe occupants refuse to give it up. Some of the members showed a disposition to go to the length ot forcing them to resign possession of it, but milder propesals prevailed, and gentle per- suasion is to be tried. The papers in the chest will be of great interest to the curious: and it is to be hoped that there will be no squabble over getting possession of them. The Great Sankey Parish Coun- cil have now got thoroughly to work,and held meet- ings every month. This is in striking contrast with its lesser but more populous neighbour, Little San- key, at which only two Parish Council meetings have been held since the members were elected. The members, however, have no chance of showing what they can do, for they are under the shadow of approaching dissolution,and will probably come to anlend without a single administrative act,through the incorporation of the township with the borough.</p>
        <p>The dogs of Warrington will during the course of this week and for some time to come have to be securely muzzled or woe betide the unfortunate owner. This is due to the enforcement of the muzzling order, which commenced on Feb. 25th. There is always a large amount of discontent and ill-humour shown by dog-owners over this order, especially if their dogs are caught in the street without muzzles, hut as a preventitive against rabies, it has undoubtedly done great good, accord- ing to testimony from various sources. It certainly has the recommendation of carrying out the maxim that " prevention is better than cure." Perhaps Mr. Summers and Mr. li-nyon will shake hands over this, notwithstanding their divergent views with regard to Pasteurism.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>STOCKTON HEA.TII.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEA.TII.</p>
        <p>Tna WEEILLT LIiCTURIL—The fourth of the English Church History Lectures was delivered on Monday evening, the subject dealt with being the " Growth of Papal Despotism, a..n. 1090-1265." The lecturer briefly enumented the main paints established in preceding lectures principal among which were the absolute inde- pendence of the Church of England from Papal intbrfer- erica and control; and that whilst the Conqueror reigned the papal advocates made very little progress, but dur- ing the struggles of his children for the Crown, the cause of Rome advanced by leaps and bounds. The failure of Augustine's mission to permanently christia- niss any of the kingdoms of the Heptarchy—Kent alone being the only kingdom where any sign of peramence was seen, and this was on soil prepared by the influence of:the British Queen, Bertha—was also dealt with. In dealing with the subject for the evening the lecturer thought that the Pope was like themselves in that he , wanted his own way when he could get it, and he was assisted to this end by the weakness of the English Kings , —notably Rufus, Henry II and John, who from motives ' of a personal nature and by the sacrifice of former inde- pendence, gave him their allegiance, and the Pope, for a time, practically controlled the English kings and the English Church. The causes, which culminated in the wringing of Magna Charta from John by Langton arid the Barons, were brought out and the audience were particnlarly to notice that this" Chart of our Liberties," in referring to our Church spoke of it as the" Church of England."—Ecelesia Anglicana not Ecclesia Romana. Yet there was always a struggle and a resistance, some- times eveu from prelates who originally supported the Pope, which ultimately culminated in " The Refer:na- tion." A very instructive and interesting series of views of cathedrals and churches illustrating the Early Eng- lish and Decorated Styles of architecture were thrown on the screen, and the lecture was profusely illustrated throughout. At the con- clusion the declaration signed by the Ro- man Catho;ic Bishops in 1826 with regard to Church property was read by the lecturer :—" British ' (Roman) Catholics are charged with entertaining a pre- tended right to the property of the Established Church in England. We consider such a charge to be wholly without foundation. We declare that we entertain no pretensions to such a claim. We regard all the revenues and temporalities of the Churzh Establishment as the property of those on whom they ate settled by the laws of the land. We disclaim any right, title, or pretension with regard to the same." A correspondent sent the above, the lecturer said, to Mr. Gladstone, who replied (November Ist, 1891)—" I think you are unassailable on the point at issue. From my point of view the pro- position of the Roman Catholic Bishops, to which you fasten them is a sound one." The proceedings then terminated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>DARESBURY.</head>
        <p>DARESBURY.</p>
        <p>COUNTY COUNCIL ELECTION.—On Saturday afternoon, between three and four o'clock, Mr. H. Taylor, of Ches- ter, deputy returning officer for this division, sat at the schoolhouse to receive objections to the return of Mr. William Richard Earp, of the tannery, &amp;c., Preston Brook, as county councillor and to examine the list of nominations. No objection was made, and Mr. Earp, who was present during the sittinz, was declared re- turned unopposed for the third time.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>VIICKNIERS should acquire the "Pocket Diary and IL) Companion " issued by the well-known Sports Matm- factires, Ueorge G. Bussey t Co., Peckh trn, London. All the information Cricketers require is found therein, as well as the usual Diary, yet it will go in the waistcoat pocket Leather bound, gilt edges, 6d., postage id. It you are not satisfied G. G. B. A Co. will return ths pries. RUGBY:A WARRINGTON ST. MARY'S V. C.REST4k COLLEGE.—St. Mary's had as visitors on Saturday the Ahave team, on the Liverpool-road ground. There was* a good gather- ing of spectators. Both teams were well represented. The visitors won the toss, and started with the wind. A moment later the College were seen defending, and from a scrimmage O'Brien secured and passed to F. Hampson, who raced over in grand style. Roughley failed at goal. The Saints again pressed the College on their lines. A free kick to the visitors gave relief, until the home backs got possession,which resulted in O'Brien scoring,Thomas failing. The College were kept defend- leg, and from a neat forwarc. rush F.Elampson received a pass and scored his second try,the same player failing to enhance its value. From the drop out the College livened up considerably, play resting at half-way. Rutter accepted a pass from F. Hampson, and ran along the popular touch in grand style and scored, F. Hamp- son failing. The College appeared out-played,the Saints baring it all their own way, and Thomas scored from a scrimmage near the line, the kick again failing. A Moment later, Higginbottom was seen galloping over in tine style. The kick failed. The visitors played a de- fensive g,atne throughout, and never got farther than half way. At half time the score stood St. Mary's six tries, College nil. Resuming,the Saints had the wind in their favour. O'Brien secured and passed to Warbur- ton, who scored in the corner; Roughley landing the first goal. The College endeavoured to stem the pres- sure, but to no avail, and O'Brien scored his second try. Monks failed. The visitors' forwards broke away to centre, but F. Flampson secured,rounded his opdonents, and scored a try; the kick failing. The courage of the College men never tailed them, And they made one final effort, but it was frustrated by Hampson at the half way flag. He transferred to Davies, who sprinted over in fine style, Roughley landing a goal. After this the game proved of an uninteresting character,and Warbur- ton and Higginbottom added tries, the Saints estab- lishing a record, winning by three goals nine tries (42 points) to nil. WARRINGTON RANGERS V. CASTLE ROBBER WORKS.— This match took place on the ground of the former on Saturday. The visitors won the toss and Savage kicked off for the Rangers. It was soon seen that the home forwards had an advantage over their opponents, rush- ing the scrimmages in good style. The first scrimmage was formed in the visitors' 25, which saw Evans and Foden passing well, the latter giving Savage a quick pass, who romped over near the touch line, Siddall ing to convert. Jut before half time the visitors got a free kick, through Evans tackling Bennett off-side, the full back kicking a goal, which made the score level. Half-time was soon called with the score--Rangers one I try (three points) ; Rubber Works one penalty goall (three points). The visitors kicked MI in the second half, and made tracks for the home goal, hut Siddall effected a smart save. From a scrimmage Whitfield picked up and passed to Savage, who put in a good run being held on the line, but Bailey got over amidst loud cheers. The same player failed at goal. From the drop out the visi- tors' left came away splendidly, but passing the ball for- ward the chance wasspotled aod scrimmage was formed. From the following scrimmage Evans got hold, and passing to Fodee the latter kicked across to Bailey, and with no One to pass but the full back he got in after a splendid run, thus securing his second try, Charnock failing at goal. Soon after time was called with the score —Warrington Rangers three tries (nine points) ; Castle Rubber Works, one penalty goal (three points). WIDNES V. LANCASTER.--Played on the Lowerhouse ground, on Saturday, before 4,000 spectators. Barber started for Widnes. Widnes showed up to advantage by a good run by the forwards, but Clarke was robbed, and for a time the home citadel was the scene of opera- I tions. Good play by Rispan gave Barber an opportunity which he seized, but the relief was only temporary ,Lan- caster forcing the play. A rush by the Widnes forwards resulted in Grace passing to Nevitt, who secured a try amid loud cheers. The place kick proved abortive, Roused by this reverse the visitors worked the leather well up the field, and Alford failing to play the ball, it was rushed over, and matters were thus equalised. The home team returned the compliment, and Peters by a pass from Nevitt,put Whines ahead by an accurate drop kick. The score at half time was—Widnes, one goal, one try; Lancaster, one penalty goal. The initial stages of the second half were of a desultory nature,play being confined to midfield. Lancaster were the first to attack but were driven back. Barber, who was conspicuous in the midst of hostilities, passed to Brennan, a try fall- ing to the home lot. 'Fhis further reverse aroused Lan- caster, and their forwards rushed a try, which was not improved upon. They continued an attack for some time, until Brennan, who had gone full back, brought relief. At the other end Alford dropped a good goal. Reault—Widnes, three goals, one try ; Lancaster, one goal, one try.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>ASSOCIATION.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>PARNWORTH RESERVE V. WIDNES PARISH RESERVE.— These two teams met at Widnes for the first time this season on a slushy ground. Play was of a fast and ex- citing nature, both teams working hard. Widnes were the first to score, hut the visitors quickly returned the compliment. Result—Widnes Parish Reserve, two goals; Farnworth Reserve, two goals. WARRINGTON ASSOCIATION V. LIVERPOOL RAMBLERS.— Few spectators witnessed the above match at Bluudel- , sands on Saturday last. Connor started for Warrington and the home team getting possession made tracks for Warrington goal, but was pulled up by Phillips. The visitors then took the ball clown to the Ramblers' end, and Carroll forced a corner which came to nothing. Warrington pressed, but Wilson was not to be beaten, and he was in splendid form in goal. Pierce and Brown brought the ball down and forced two corners, which were cleared. The Warrington forwards passed very neatly, and Smithson from a pass by Connor missed a good chance. Both teams now pressed in turns and had corners, until Glover put iii a long shot which Evans, in trying to clear put beyond Webster's rea.ch,into the net. Ralf-time arrived with the score to nil in favour of the Ramblers. Early in the second half Warrington com- menced o press, and Cosnor taking the ball half the length of the field beat Wilson with a good shot. War- rington quir.V.c; dded another. The Ramblers tried hard to equalise but found the Warrington halves in good form. The whistle sounded with the score two— , one in favour of Warrington. BRICE-ST. MISSION V. Sr. MARY'S RESERVE.-011 the ground of the former. Meakin kicked off and play settled in the visitors' 25, until Appleton getting hold passed to Meakin, who scored. Half time, Mission 1 goal, St. Mary's 0. Final result, Mission 5, St. Mary's 2. Appleton (3), Meabin 2 were the scorers for the Mis- sion. flowracy WANDERERS V. PENICETH C.L. B.—At Penketh. Periketh won the toss and Howley kicked off and had hard lines in net scoring, until Bramhall rushed the ball down half lerwth of the field, and rushed at the goal- keeper, while Thornily kicked the ball through, which ended in a goal. Half time score—Wanderers, 1 goal; Penketli, nil. Second half—Ringer played a nice game centre forward and scored four goals. Final result— Pentoth, 4. Wanderers, 1 goal ;</p>
        <p>THE CROFT bTABBING CASE. At the Liverpool county Intermediate Quarter sessions, on Tuesday, John Knockton was cnarged with unlawfully wounding Michael Quinn,at Croft, on Feh.l7th. Mr.Swift,instructed by Messrs. Green- ell an 4 Buckton,prosecuted, and Mr. A. A. Tobin,in- structed by Messrs. Jenkins and Co., defended.— The prisoner, on the advice of counsel, pleaded guilty to the count of the indictment, charging him with occasioning actual bodily harm, and he was bound over in the sum of -CIO to keep the peace for twelve months, and to come Up for judg- ment when called upon.</p>
        <p>It is said that his Holiness,Leo Xlll.has just received. as a gift from President Kruger.an immense diamond of nine hundred and seventy-one carats, believed to be the largest diamond known. It was found by an African chief in the mines of Jagersfontein ; and is quite perfect in its bluish white sparkle, except for a tiny flaw not perceptible to the naked eye.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>IMPERIAL PARLIAMENT,</head>
        <p>IMPERIAL PARLIAMENT,</p>
        <p>MONDAY. WELSH DISESTABLISHMENT.</p>
        <p>In the House of Commons on Monday evening a new standing order was agreed to giving chambers of com- merce and chambers of agriculture the right to be heard against any bill which they considered would injuriouely affect the localities to which they applied. Mr. J. LOWTHER moved that it be an instruction to the Committee to leave out parts 5 and 6 of the Glasgow Corporation and Police Bill, on the ground that they in- terfered with public Acts. He said that under this bill it would be an offence for a gentleman to hold private conversation with a lady in the street or for a person to have in his possession a pack of cards. Sir G. TRE- VELYAN said he was sure the Committee would keep a sharp eye upon the provisions of the bill. Many of them, however, were very valuable. Several hon. members expressed the opinion that the bill might pro- perly be left to the Committee, and on a division the in- struction was rejected by 177 to 135. Mr. ASQUITH informed Mr. CARVELL WILLIAMS that he had recommended the remission of the remainder of the sentence passed upon a Nonconformist minister in Guernsey for taking part in an irregular marriage ceremony. Replying to a question,Mr.BßYCS said the French Gov- ernment had deckled that on the expiration next year ot the contract with the London, Chatham, and Dover' Railway Company for the conveyance of the French mails, the new contract should be given to the North of France Railway Company, on the express condition that the vessels employed should be built in France and be owned by a French colnpany. Mr. Ascsuira moved for leave to bring in a bill " to terminate the Estahlishment of the Church of England in Wales and Monmouthshire, and to make provision in respect of the temporalities thereof." The Government, he said, proposed that the bill should come into opera- tion on the Ist January,lB97. They had to dispose of a gross annual revenue of £279,000—£233,000 being parochial revenue and £46,000 being made up of capita- lar and episcopal estates .and revenue derived from Wales by the Ecclesiastical Commissioners. All private benefactions created since 1703 would remain the pro- perty of the disestablished and disendowed Church, as would also the churches and parsonages ; but burial grounds and glebes would be transferred to the parish, district, or town conncil, and tithe rent charges would be vested in the County Councils. The cathedrals would be vested in the Commissioners created, who would have to keep them in repair, sub- ject to the right of the disestablished Church to continue the use of them for the same purpose as at present. Vested interests of holders of ecclesiastical offices would be respected, and private patrons might receive compensation not exceeding one year's net emolument of a living. Of the central fund of £46,000 not less than two-thirds would be applied to higher education. The local and principal fund would be applied in accordance with the schemes—educational, charitable, Sec.—framed by the County Councils with the approval of the Commissioners. 'r he Government did not consider that it was any profanation of property which was dedicated to pious uses that it should be ap- plied in the relief of suffering, and even in bringing home to the common people at least the means of access to refining and humanising influences. Sir M. HICKS. BEACH said millions of English men and women believed that disestablishment was a wrong to the State and dis- endowment a plunder of the church. In all fairness a religious census for which the Opposition had re eatedly asked, ought to have been taken before the introduction of the present bill. The scheme was a bribe to the local ratepayer, but he was certain that if it was the mind of the House to disendow the Church in Wales it would insist that the fund should be dealt with as a whole and devoted to the benefit of the country at large The bill was not merely a blow aimed at Wales, but was intended to have an ulterior effect on England. Mr. F. EDWARDS, as a Churchman, supported the bill. Mr. J. LLOYD MORGAN said that, speaking generally,he thought it was an excellent measure. Sir G. 0. MORGAN said that, without committing himself to all its details, he believed the bill to be a just and statesmanlike measure. The debate was adjourned on the motion of Lord WOLMER. In the House of Lords the compulsory retirement of the Hon. C. BOURKE, the Chairman of the Irish Prisons Board, was again discussed. The Earl of ERNE asked the Government to take care that Mr. BOURKE did not suffer injustice in the matter of his pension by premature dismissal before attaining 65 years of age. Earl SPENCER, on behalf of the Government, said Mr. BOURKE had suffered no injustice by being called on to retire under the provisions of the Order inCouncil. His case was not an isolated one. In England there were innumerable auch cases.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>TUESDAY.</head>
        <p>TUESDAY.</p>
        <p>In the Hausa of Cens—m-o-n-s-o-n- Tuesday evening Sir W. Elsecrwar gave notice that on Thursday he would move a resolution for giving to the Government more time for their business. . . .</p>
        <p>Mr.EvEtrerr moved the following resolution :—" That this House regards with inreasing apprehension the constant fluctuations and the growing divergence in the relative value of gold and silver, and heartily concurs in the recent expressions of opinion on the part of the Government of France and the Government and Parlia- ment of Germany as to the serious evils resulting there- from. It therefore urges upon Her Majesty's Govern- ment the desirability of co-operatiug with other Powers- in an international conference for the purpose of con- sidering what measures can be taken to remove or miti- gate these evils." Mr. CHAPLIN seconded the motion. He said he was authorised by Mr. Batvona to say that had he been present he should have voted for the txt :ton. Sir W. Hancounr expressed himself gratified that there was nothing in this resolution about free silver coinage, or ratio, or about the question which raised the issues between bimetallists and monometal- lists. He himself was a monometallist. and Mr. CHAPLIN was a bimetallist, and he was sure that if they were to spend the rest of their lives in endeavouring to convince one another they would utterly fail. There was one mistake made at the Brussels Conference which ought not to be repeated. The delegates must be delegates who spoke in the name of the Government and with the responsibility of the Government; it was no use sending men of opposing and conflicting views to go Into an academic discussion in the presence of Europe. Passing to the specific question of bimetallism, Sir Wittiest quoted the adverse opinions of Mr. GLADSTONE, Mr. GOSCHEN, and others, and, referring to words used by Mr. BALFOUR when in office asked whether any man would say that "the United Kingdom and the City of London had thoroughly adopted and believed in bime- tallism." He himself had not very much confidence in the possibility of any international agreement. He was not willing to place the currency of this country, upon which its prosperity depended, at the mercy of any foreign nation. It the resolution included any commit- tal of this country to the bimetallic system he should resist it to the utmost. No responsible Government of this country. he was deeply convinced, would propose a chawge in the currency basis on which, since 1818 this country had reached a point of commercial and financial prosperity which no other nation bad ever attained, but as the motion was in terms on which they could find • common ground he should not oppose it. Colonel BRIDORMAN, Mr. FORWOOD, Mr. WHITELEY, Mr. COURT- NEY, and others took paet in the debate, at the close of which Mr. EVERETT'S motion was agreed to. WEDNESDAY.</p>
        <p>In the House of Commons,on Wednesday the Marquis of CARMARTHIN moved the second reading of the Pistols Bill, which he said was the same bill framed by the Stanaing Committee of the House of Lords last session. One of the alterations made, Lord Castimaraux ex- plained,was to relieve persons going abroad who desired to purchase a pistol at.the last mement ; another raised the price of a licence to sell pistols from is. to £1 ; and he should be ready, if it was desired, to abandon in committee the clause authorising a search for pistols. Mr. HOPWOOD moved that the bill be read a second time that day six months. It was not, he said, proposed to qheck the sale or the possession of guns on account of gun accidents, because that would interfere with the higher classes. Sir ALBHRT RoLur seconded the amend- ment, and Sir GEORGE RUSSELL announced that the Government were exceedingly well disposed towards the measure. The second reading was eventually carried' by a majority of 119. Mr. HOPWOOD opposed the Marquis of CARMARTHSN'S motion to refer the bill to the Standing Committee on Law, but the motion was carried by a majority of 114. Sir DONALD CURRIE moved the appointment of a Select Committee to consider the objections which have been raised to the adoption by order in council of rule 15 of the Revised International Regulations for Preventing Collisions at Sea, dealing with sound signals in fog, as suggested by the Washington Conference,and considered lsy the Maritime Powers. Mr. GIBSON BOWLES moved an amendment to enlarge the reference so as to include the fixing and screening of ships' side lights, and as he declined to withdraw his amendment, the debate was, therefore, adjourned till next Wednesday.</p>
      </div>
