      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>At the Runcorn Town Hall on Wednesday morning, Mr. Edmund Pearse Burd, Local Government Board In- spectpr, conducted a local inquiry relative to an appli- cation made by the now defunct Runcorn Improvement Commissioners, for the issue of a provi lonal order to partially repeal, alter or amend the RunKorn Commis- sioners' Act, 1893, and the Runcorn Improvement Act, 1852. There were present : Messrs. Lightburn, Taylor, Kirkham, Wilson, Sweet,man, Bishruwn, Grice, and 111 r. Lake (law clerk), Mr. Marshall (secretary), and Mr. Barker (surveyor). Mr. Lake said the rateable value of the town was £60,875, and the asses•iable va1ue,£49,879. The isonula,- tion in 1891 was 20,050, the number of houses was 3,861, and the total acreage 1,159. The application was for a provisional order to amend their acts for the redenaP° don of old loans. Under the Act of 1852 they were pledged to repay their loans within 33 years, and under this Act they had borrowed sundry amounts. There was at present outstanding against them on borrowed sums from 1861 to 1893, together with a sum of -21,800' borrowed prior to 1861, an account of £7,203. Tiler now asked the Board to consoli,late the loanS between 1860-93 and to allow them to 037,,. them back within 16 years from the 25C,u , March, 1893. £406 represented the a mona the)r would pay off each year. The Local Government Board estimated it at £386. The second part of the aPPlica' Urn' was to amend the Improvement. Act ,1 1852 IV striking out sections 57, 58, 59, 60 and 63 of the fown'4 Improvement Clauses-Act, 1847 which had referenCe t° the level and width of new streets. Recently wile: some new buildings were being erected they were with a difficulty, because the Local Government Boar' would not pass the by-laws for back passages,_" th,?, Authority were tied down by the clause in the Town' Improvement Act. They could not alter a statute LI by-laws, and they had thought it ad eisnhl blo.ket by-laws in accordance with the Street Improvernen.. Act which was more up-to-date and better than the AP ef 1847. They could not do this without striking the sections mentioned and that was the reason of the apnlication. There was no opposition to either of the applicationit and the proceedings only lasted fifteen minuteS. NOT IS</p>
        <p>ON ()FAMES AND ATHLETI FOOTBALL. . It was a great disappointment to the football publie of Warrington when it was discovered that notwide standing the thaw which set in last week, the Wi Ie pool enclosure was still unfit for play on Saturday's that the match with St.Helens had to be cancelled. Tto fixture probably comes next to Oldham in populanti! and it is hard lines on Warrington to have to postpone both of these popular games. Elswever, there ig almost absolute certainty of play being resumed to-da” and alt this will be the first appearance of the first teara before a Warrington " gate" since December 28th,thei ought to have a very cordial reception.</p>
        <p>The team which is chosen to do duty for Warringto9 against Rochdale St. Clements is as follows —llooe, lull back ; Barber, Fleesom, Bowen, and Burt in, three quarters ; Bate and Bennett, half backs ; Turner,Jelleit Dakin, laylor, Berry, Bailey, Barrett, and fluogO* forwards.</p>
        <p>thit It will be acticed that a strange name appears on list. I refer to Bowen, whose inclusion in the team no, called forth many expressions of surprise. It will he (), interest to state that the player in question is flee% Bowen, of the Cardiff first team who has come to tn. Vulcan W orks, Earlestown, and will be there for so 03; time. He will play regularly for Warrington during th„ present season, and will no doubt greatly strengthe; the back division. He has been a regular player in tbro Cardiff team this season,and was chosen to play for against Bradford on Tuesday. He should be an ac4u!ll4; Lion to Warrington,for he comes from one of the leachilr clubs in the kingdom. 4t The Warrington committee are developing a rne,,io the for the letter Bte an alarming extent. Just look at f list of players ; Boscow, Barber, Bowen, Burton. 154Atef,' Bennett, Berry, Bailey and Barratt--nine out of the teen which compose the team. In fact Heesom ie tat ' only back wnose name does not begin with B. Ile 111,70 feel rather lonely. However, it does not matter le"tr letter gains supremacy, so long as good players uee„ tained, and I hope that the above nine players belie the insect who "improves each shining hour.' " that they will become terrors to all opposing Wavle- -011 The league tournament was resumed on Saturday's be there were several severe struggles,but in most casee t„d favourites came off all right. Everton for the seco'ci. time this season accounted for Preston North although the struggle was a very keen one. Sunderie"o. also got the better of Sheffield United although they to play the greater part of the game with one man eil.°A. The United team appear to have played a very 0,7;4' game, to judge from Sunderland reports,and the 01,a;t00 was a very uninteresting and quarrelsome one. - Villa made a draw with Burnley, after being three' bellied twenty minutes from the finish. This that they are not short of pluck and determination.</p>
        <p>-- West Bromwich Albion were careful to show that their defeat of Small Heath in the cup tie competition WO/ fluke, for they again beat them in a league Match, have now a good chance of keeping out of the ' last three. The Wolves played their last league Lu-f at home, and only succeeded in making a drsvr 0, with Blackburn Rovers. They have yet four to P away from home, and unless they can get a point ori out of these, they run great risk of being comPelle engage in the test matches at the end of the season.</p>
        <p>There have been several league matches during present week, and one of them may have a very ihr tent bearing on the championship. In fact it IS certain that by it Ererton have lost almost their chance of gaining the League cup this year. " toffee" men journeyed to Sheffield to play the on Monday, and for the first quarter of an hour Of they made rings round their opponents, and Mit to obtain two goals. At this point, however, one ° 61 United players hurt Williams, the Everton goal Ig°,l/P„ln so seriously on the ankle, that he fell to the grouelt had to be carried off the field. Kelso and Milwara 1 cessively took Williams' place, but the Uniteciti every advantage of the weakened forces against and put on four goals, thus winning by four to tots is almost certain that but for the accident to Everten would have won easily, and it is therefor': tremely unfortunate that they should lose the 00°' 'ship not through poor play, but through accidentli• derland were playing the other Sheffield team the day, and won easily.</p>
        <p>-- Everton has one chance yet of getting level if passing Sunderland. They go to Sunderland 0° 30th, and should they win, they will draw inve,,ere that team. As they have never won there yet, tu te, but slight hope for them. Not a single visiting have won at Sunderland this season yet, and orilY nor have made draws. It must surely be gettirg ous to the Wearside public to see their team ttiv.i' win. A loss or tyre would add a little spice victories. It is now tolerably certain that the cup for the third time will go to the bank* 0' Wear.</p>
        <p>Returning to Rugby football, I notice that oa Yorkshire administered a severe defeat upon ! ot and it is very evident that the cheese eountV )5 not t champion county by a long way. Durham also got better of Northumberland by nine points t' eignto'op ' neithei of these matches have any bearing uv, clunty championship, they do not arouse ye; Spread interest.</p>
        <p>-- CYCLING. A t: The cycling season is fast approaching, am• to rington C.C. is already bestirring itself. A smoker the playersin touch with each other before the actually begins, was held at the Norton Arms on 't day eveaing, and before the present month ig 1050;t fixtures will be commenced in real earnest this year, is the genial Ted Pickavance, and a Mc?. thusiastic captain could not be got. There secretaries, Messrs. Booth, and G. Smith, h? airs wise enthusiastic cyclists. An excellent list been prepared, and a most successful season is a aced.</p>
        <p>SWIMMING. /101011 At the annual meeting of the Northern urh"A- -„y, Amateur Swimming Association, held on Satf the Preston Referm Club, the Warrington C.ltive-A; represented by Mr. J. Grace. Mr. J. Grace. ton, was elected a member of the Executive lit'oree and was also adopted official handicapper and re - Liverpool and district.</p>
        <p>I- - Lord Brassey is one of the most entousiastiC 'P!:1;0 of the time. lie says he cannot remember a tr rigg' he did not love scaling the ropes of real shipi. ° 00 little boats after a style of his sown at ho inc. been a barrister, a member of Parliamont. arldvrwrie• to Australia to takeover the Go vernorshiP °' f The new outbreak of iifluenza recalls a toot ()t Sir Andrew Clark, who complained, on thn firsing ance of the epidemic in this country, that forP9'i. seemed destined to become but another nerve., 80.0, well every complaint to which flesh is heir. trenti must be making a fortune," said Sir Andraw,italten inn the name of a pushing gentleman who haii, to influenza under his special care. " Pa„„ the Ili that he has discovered a new cure " Not at all," said Sir Andrew ; .0 h,</p>
