      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>PEOPLE."</head>
        <p>THE PEOPLE."</p>
        <p>BOLD-STREET CHURCH_ "SERVICES FOR</p>
        <p>FIRST ANNUAL TEA MEETING.</p>
        <p>On Thursday evening last week the first annual tea meeting under the aus- pices el the Bold-street Wesleyan Chu.ch Services for the People was held in the schoolroom and was very largely atrended.upwards of 600 persons being accommodated at the various tables. There were two sittings necessitated, and while the second was in pro- gress the Band if the People's Service played several selections. The ladies who presided over the 12 tables were—Mrs. T. S. Smith, Mrs. Charles '[oft, Mrs. J. Parkinson, Mrs. Pigot, Mrs. Davey, Mrs. S. B. Stringer, Mrs. Robinson; Mrs. Bailey, Mrs. Colin, Mrs. Brindle, Mrs. Bodle, Mrs. Watkins, Mrs. Mason, Mrs. F. Pendle- bury, Mrs. G. Weaver, Mrs. J. Toft, Mrs. Gandy, Miss Benson, Miss Weaver, Mrs. Allen, Mrs. Whitfield, Mrs. Dunn, Mrs. Bennett, Mrs. Kirtlan, Mrs. W. Hickman, Mrs. Wariug, Mrs. Osberne, Mrs. Collier, Mrs. Brookes, Mrs. Prince, Mrs. White, Mrs. W. Parkinson. Those who assisted the seniors were Miss Waring, Miss Collier, Miss Cohn, Miss Ada Colin, and Miss L. Jones. There were 40 gentlemen waiters, and the arrangements were carried out with every satisfaction, under the super- vision of Mr. Charles E. Tott, who had catered for the good things provided, and who had, as ab'e assistants, Mr. Waring, Mr. White and Mr. F. Pendlebury. At 8.15 the room was crowded for the public meeting. The Rev. J. Kirtlan presided, and the enjoyment of ie evening was greatly ephanced by the skilled and taste- ful playing of the Band of the People's Service under the conductorship of Mr. F. Pendlebury, who fully de- serves the compliments which were paid to him during the evening. For the last musical selection which was the overture "Le Diadetne," by A. Hermann, an encore was vociferously demanded and complied with. The Rev. J. KIRTLAN, commenced by detailing the origin of the services which they held every Sunday after- noon. For some time his colleague and himself felt that they had large premises, hut that they did not get as many of the masses of the people in the place as they should like,and they were anxious to provide some Ser- vice for young men and women who were walking about the streets,and others who were not in regular attendance at any place of worship. They also felt that many of those people were not opposed to such services. They thought if they could i..rovide a service which would attract even a small number they would be able to do a little good in that way. Then the question wae,where were the leaders ; they must have somebody who could find the music, and their thoughts turned to their friend Mr. F. Pendlebury who had succeeded so admirably as conductor and orginator of the Band of the Peop,e's Service. (Applause.) After some consideration he undertook the matter which had not been an easy one. It was a very laborious thing for anybody to have to get together such a band as that, and keep it up to the mark. (Hear, hear.) It required a great deal of time and much labour. The speaker was glad there had been no failure to chronicle in connection with the movement. They were rich in speakers,and had almost any number of men who could get up at five minutes' notice and gi ie a good Christian address. No running about after speakers was neces- sary, as they relied upon their own talents and had not made any mistake in that respect. Another question was the character of the services. It was felt that they did not want a P.S.A. because there was one and a very large and successful one, and they did not want to com- pete with the one at Wycliffe. Still, it was thought that there were thousands of people who were not able to go there. There were their sisters and wives for only occasionally were they allowed at the P.S. A., and,there- fore, it was thought that there was a great opening for ladies in a good afternoon service, and he was glad to say the ladies formed a large part in the congregations. Their services were a little freer than the P.S.A.,as they had no cards of membership—though that was an ex- cellent plan—but they decided they would have a ser- vice where people could g 0 in and out without any register. Their friends had appreciated its nature, and they did not want to alter it at present. It was their idea to make the services helpful and profitable, and they agreed that the addresses must be Gospel addresses. They did not want anything else but the old truths put in as many fresh words as possible by various persons, and it was desired to put some good in the hearts of the people. ,Some very good Christian addresses had been delivered from time to time at the services during the six months and it would be agreed that at present there was no sign of any falling off of interest. When they formed a com- mittee they found any number of brethren to throw themselves into the work and the success was due to this brotherly willingness. They "thanked God and took courage." Latterly it was thought desirable that they should have a general gathering of their friends and invited neighbours. A large committee was organised and that day they had had mere than 500 present. (Applause.) Such a tea as they had enjoyed could not often be got for 6d. (Hear, hear.) They had reason to thank God that night that everything proposed had been carried out to the letter, and they were very glad indeed for such a grand congregation. He trusted that they would all feel their brotherhood. (Hear, hear.) He felt that when the Methodist Church became rich and respectable and they got a church like Bold-street and a good minister they became somewhat satisfied. The masses were not forgotten but they were not looked after as they should be. Methodism was coming back to the masses. Mr. Mason sent his love to the meeting and was sorry that an accident to his knee prevented his attendance. The speaker concluded by exhorting the people to come as often as tney could, and added that the committee would do all they could to interest them. Mr. W. COLLIER thought the title of the services was a very happy one. It was not a service for men, women or children, but for all three to meet together. He was pleased to be there because it reminded him of the old- fashioned tea meetings whets they were held in the Public Hall. He was glad the Services had been such a success. Methodism had always been for the people and had gone to all parts of the world. It was a grand organisation and if they wanteo to succeed they must organise. The great thing they had to fight against was sin and to do that they must organise, work, pray, and endeavour by their influence and example to spread the name of Jesus Christ till it was a charm to all. He invited everybody to the services and said "come." (Applause.) Mr. W. HICKMAN was pleased to see such a large attendance, and that they had had such an excellent tea. He was also pleased with the movement and with the P.S.A. movement. (Applause.) He took a fatherly interest in the movement at Bold-st. as he had been able to give the inaugural address, arid if he had not been at all the meetings he had watched the growth and develop- ment of the movement and wished it God speed, and would be pleased and gratified to see Bold-st. Church filled every Sunday. He was glad they were not to have a stiff meeting. He liked a meeting where there was plenty of laughter. None of the meetings in connection with the People's Service should be dry, dreary and stiff,as he believed in all of them enjoying a hearty laugh at things proper and not improper. This was, he thought, one of the reasons that these movements had taken on so well throughout the country. Only a fort- night ago he was at a similar service at Manchester in one of the large Congregational churches, and every inook and cranny of the edifice was filled with men who had a set purpose in their countenances. He was de- lighted that in Lancashire alone there was in connection ' with similar movements nearly 20,000 members, and in the Birmingham district nearly 50,000 members who met every Sunday. In his inaueoral address he said they were to have a benevolent fund, and although they had not commenced so soon as was expected he was pleased there was now one which they would find to be very useful. He cited some instances where a benevo- lent fund had been made goad use of, and which had come under his own notice. In many cases it had helped to keep the wolf from the door. He knew of nothing in any shape to which men and their wives could go and spend a happier hour than they could at Bold-st. every Sunday afternoon. I Mr. JonN Torr proposed a vote of thanks to those who had in any way to do with the arrangements, to the Iladies, and those who had given trays, and hoped all I would be better for meeting together in that way.—The I vote was seconded and carried with applause, and Mr. S. B. Stringer responded. The meeting then terminated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>WHY THE RUSKIN MUSEUM WENT TO</head>
        <p>WHY THE RUSKIN MUSEUM WENT TO</p>
        <p>SHEFFIELD.</p>
        <p>Why the Ruskin Museum ever went to Sheffield at all has puzzled many people, especially those whose only acquaintance with the great cutlery capital is that gathered by s rush through the place in the darkness, when the view from the train is of rows of dull dingy houses and of huge factories lighted up by tha fitful glare of furnance fires. These persons (says Mr. Alfred Sprigg, in the course of a valuable article on "The Ruskin Museum and its Treasures " in the February number of the Windscr Magazine) regard Sheffield has about the last place for a museum rich in artistic trea- sures, many of them of priceless worth. Nor was the surprise lessened when they read that the St. George's Guild regarded a rlluVura not as a place of entertain- ment shnply, but a place of education, and that its founder desired it to be regarded as a place of noble instruction, were perions so minded could " devote a certain portion of secluded, laborious, and reverent life to the attainment of the Divine Wisdom, which the Greeks supposed to be the gift of Apollo, or the Suu,and which the Christian knows to be the gift of Christ." Such a museum it was said would be suitably housed at Derhain, or at Oxford, or at Worcester, or at Coniston. But why Sheffield, with its smoke and dirt, its rush and hustle, and its peculiar dialect, instea d of a quiet town with educational or artistic associations ? Mr. Ruskin, however, supplied the answer himself by saying that he acknowledges iron work as a necessary art ; that the cutlers' art at its best, as manifested in Sheffield, is "unsurpassable by that of any living nation, and that the Yorkshire temper is capable yet of the ideas of honesty and piety by which old England Lived."</p>
        <p>ONE CASE OF ARMY AND NAVY MEDICINES contains sufficient to cure Contagious Diseases of a certain class, (internal and external), Price 4s. 6d, Single Bottles of Mixtur 28. 9d. Only of 1101'X, Operative Chemist. (late dietlosen).332. Deansgate. 'Mewled r. Advice. free</p>
