      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>ST. JOIIN AMBULANCE BRIGADE.</head>
        <p>ST. JOIIN AMBULANCE BRIGADE.</p>
        <p>PRESENTATION TO DR. ADAMS.</p>
        <p>A coffee supper and smoking concert in connection with the Warrington Ambulance Brigade, took place in the Temperance Hall, Buttermarket-street, Warrington, on Thursday night, last week at !which a large number of members and friends sat down. This was the first meeting of the kind in connection with the brigade, wnich has only been formed for about 3 year. The brigade, which was started in July,-1894, consists of about 60 members has for its president, Mr. Robert Pierpoint, M.P., and there are seven hon. surgeons in climection with it. Most of the expenses during the year have been paid by the members themselves. The concert was got up in order , to present the certificates to the successful members and to present a silver mounted umbrella and meerschaum pipe to their instructor, Dr. Adams. Mr. Thomas Morris, one of the vice-presidents, presided at the concert. After the supper, Mr. Morris said he did not know of ' any institution:or course ofginstruction or lecture that did better work than are St. John Ambulance :Associa- tion did. (Hear, hear.) When they started that institution they knew it was one of those unselfish things in which they were in- structed to render aid to others in time of necessity. (Hear,hear.) The St. John Ambulance Brigade received instruction and could do good to others, This meeting was one of those things that did not take place often. He (Mr. Morris) Was never at such a one as this in his life before, and be was very pleased to be with thorn. In case of shipwreck or fire or anything that affected life and limb, Ambulance men were like angels to the in- jured. i.e hoped, however, they would never have a case to attena to as long as they lived. (Applause). Mr. Mounts then proceeded to award certificates to the successful members, after which he said that last, though not least,came the presentation to their instruc- tor. They must have had a good instructor, or they were good learners. (A Voice : " Both.") They had seven Hon. Surgehns, one Hon. Chaplain, a President, and two Vice-Presidents. Mr. Morris then presented a handsome silver-mounted silk umbrella and a meer- schaum pipe to Dr. Adams, who then rose amidst great applause and thanked the members for the honour con- ferred upon him, To him it was a very gratifying fact and measured the sense of esteem in which they held him. In reference to the brigade ha would say th,it Mr. Hardy had told him that at present there were about 300 men in Warrington who had passed examinations. They would like to open that room as the head centre in Warrington. The cost of joining the brigade was Is. and for that they got 48 practices' in a good room pro- vided for the purpose. Mr. HARDY proposed a vote of thanks to Mr. Morris for pesiding, to which Mr. Morris briefly replied. Anexcellent programme was provided, consisting of a cornet solo by Mr. N. Taylor, song by Mr. E. Jones, concertina solo by Mr. W. Whittaker and recitation by Mr. J. Massey. Messrs. Virerthington and Harg gave a very' good musical entertainment and Professor H. , Pritchard gave a ventriloquial performance with four figures,the figures being members of the brigade dressed for the occasion. Mr. W. Payton made an excellent accompanist. .After singing " God Save the Queen" the excellent entertain:nent was brought to a close.</p>
      </div>
