      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>i and,</p>
        <p>SHORTHAND PRIZE. The. prizes for shorthand writing, offered by Pro- fessor Fant-Abbott to the pupils of the Misses Rim- er, Wilson Patten-street, have been gained by- -Ist Miss Bessie Chantry, Bold-street, 2nd Miss lileevers, Padgate. MEAD MASTERSHIP OP ST. BARNABAS' SCHOOL. Mr. Charles Turner, of Fearnhead has been ap- pointed to the post of headmaster of the St. Barn a- bas' Schools, Warrington in succession to Mr. R. Y. Woollacott. Mr. Turner will commence his duties at the beginning of next month. ALL HOUSEHOLD LINEN, hemmed and marked, free of Ilharge.—J. and W. DUTTON, Sankepst.. Warrington.—Advt. DEATH OF MISS SUTTON. 14.4., C. Sutton (clerk to the Board of Guardians) bas sustained a very sad bereavement by the death of his only daughter, at his residence, Frodshatn, on Monday. Miss Sutton was very highly esteemed by all who had the pleasure of her acquaintance, was an accomplished and thoroughly domesticated wortitin; and from the active part she took in their borne life, her loss has been a severe blow to Mr. J. C. Suttim and Mrs. Sutton. Miss Suttod was about 32 years of age. PIPER ON "TRADES' UNIONISM." Ott Friday evening, at the usual meeting of the Baptist Mutual Improvement Society held in Go!- borne-street, Mr. Norman Balmford presided, and liir.Henry Walker read a paper on " Trades' Union- ism." The latter dwelt upon the necessity of the working classes combining against the capitalist, the middleman, and the sweater, ant urged that it Was the duty of every worker to belong to a trade Union. Trades' Unionism ,had been of great service in the past in helping the working classes to'attain to their present position, and that position Would be greatly enhanced if the non-unionists joined their ranks. The views of the essayist were criti- cized by Messrs. R. Miller, 3, A. Heywood, John S. green, S. Kickers, W. Patrick, and the Chairman, and Mr. Walker having replied, the meeting ter- lainated. ALL HOUSEHOLD LINEN hemmed and marked, free of Ilharge.—J. and W. DUTTON. Sankey.st., Warriugton,—Adv THIRD STARR-BOWICETT SOCIETY.—ANNUAL MEETING. The fifth annual meeting of the above society was beld at the offices, Cairo-street, on Tuesday even- ing, when there was a fair attendaace of members and directors. Mr. D. Fanshaw was voted to the chair. The report of the directors and balance sheet were adopted. The retiring directors, Messrs. E. Aukland, ft. H. Pearson, and W. -E. Stout, were re-elected, and Mr. B. Oddie, of Lymtn, was elected in the place of Mr. J. Hesketh (resigned). The auditors, Messrs. Edwin Bradshaw, C.A., and F. Timperley were also re-elected. At the conclusion Of the business, the meeting proceeded to conduct an appropriation, Mr. Fansliaw was elected to preside, and Messrs. G. .1. Brown and F. Tonge were appointed scrutineers. The ballot resulted in No. 8 being drawn, which entitled Mr. Kirby, 118, Bridge-street to an appropriation of) £4OO on the 'sisal terms. Votes of thanks to the chairman and aCrutineers closed the proceedings. MCAT HS.—GABLAND. Ironmonger, Warrington and Earle s-</p>
        <p>town, Tx CLERGY DAUGHTERS' SCHOOL.—FUNERAL oF Miss STAttoNs. The funeral took place on Saturday afternoon at the Cemetery of Rose Julie Symons, aged 14 years, who died suddenly on the previous Wednesday at the Clergy Daughters' School, where she was a pupil. The first part of the service was held in the private chapel, after which there was a procession to the Cemetery, consisting of the clergy of the Parish Church, the teachers and pupils of the school. The Rector conducted the service. Amongst those present were the parents of the deceased to whom much sympathy was exteneed. Szwrra MAcsnrus Hand £2 18s. 6d. Treadle £4 4s. with handsome walnut nover.-5. and W. DUTTON, 16 and 18 Sankey-it., Warrington. —Advt. TRA. ACCIDENT AT LATCHFORD.-11EAVY CLAIM FOR DAMAGES AT THE COUNTY COURT. At the Warrington County Court on Thursday, before his Honour Judge Wynne Ffoulkes John Tickle, Frederick-st., off Wash-lane, LatChforil, sued John Hamblett, Manchester-road, for .£5O. The plaintiff alleged that defendant or his servants neg- ligently or improperly. onalov. oth, allowed his horse and cart in the Ship -Canal yard at Thelwall, to remain unattended, in consequence of which, the horse bolted, and dashed against the plaintiff's cart which was proceeding on the proper side of the road, over the Ship Canal bridge, towards Grappen- hall, throwing the plaintiff out, thus cutting his head, face, and eye,and dislocating his right elbow, rendering him unconscious. The off shaft, axle,and off Wheel of plaintiff's cart were also broken. The plaintiff was alleged to be permanently injured, rendering him unfit for work. Mr. Collingwood Hope, barrister, Liverpool, instructed by Mr. Percy Davies, appeared for the plaintiff, and Mr. Arthur irowne d3fended. Upon the case being called, Mr. Hope said that he would like to make a personal application to have the case adjourned for a week, as be had received a telegram asking him to return to Liverpool to attend a Local Government Board inquiry. As the plaintiff had seven witnesses, and the detendant bad nine, it was impossible to con- clude the case that day.—Mr. Browne said, that personally, he had no objection, but his client wished the case to go on.—After some further dis- cussion, Mr. Hamblett consented to the case being adjourned, and it was accordingly adjourned until ten o'clock next Thursday morning. Asa. HAUSIC.IIOI.I3 LINEN, hemmed and marked free of Oharge.—J. and W. DUTTON. Sankey-st.. Warrington.—Adv 1. SATURDAY NIGHT POPULAR CONCERTS. At the usual penny concert held on Saturday in the Co-operative Hall, Sankev-street, there was a large audience. The Rev. W. H. Drummond, who presided, said he was sorry the accommodation was not sufficient to provide all with seats. An excel- lent programme had been provided by Mr. J. M. Muir, and under his leadership was executed in a manner which left nothing to be desired. A trio, "Minstrels three," was given by Messrs. Baker, White. and Knowles ; Mr. Watts "Pilgrim of Love." i A duet by Mrs. Muir and Mr. Whitehouse entitled Maying," was highly appreciated. Miss Allen was very successful with her song," The Carnival." The catch, "Would you know my Celia's charms ?" was given by Messrs. Muir, White, Knowles, and Jones, who were compelled to respond to an encore. Miss Pemberton sang" Star of Bethlehem" in a very sweet and effective manner. Messrs. White and Tree contributed a duet, "Larboard Watch." Miss Allen sang "Sunny Spain;" a duet, "Excelsior," was given by Messrs. Watts and Whitehouse, and a quartette "Ye banks and braes," arranged by Mr. J. M. Muir, Messrs. White and Whitehouse. Mr, Allen contributed two violin solos, which were highly appreciated and acknowledged. Mr. Jones sang a humorous song," The lost child," which pro- voked much laughter. Mr. Watts sang "Sally in our Alley," and a capital concert was brought to a close by a trio," Dame Durden," by Messrs. Jones, White and Knowles. Mr. S. B. Stringer proposed a hearty vote of thanks to the Chairman and artistes and this was carried with acclamation. Mr. Muir nd Mr. Praeger were the accompanists.</p>
        <p>COUNTY COUNCIL ELECTIONS. Tuesday was the last day for nominations to be nded in at the Town Hall for candidates for the position of county councillor for the borough. As only the three retiring gentlemen, Messrs. F. Monks, F. A. Roberts, and Alderman Francomb, were nominated, there will be no contest, and the old members have been returned unopposed. Mr. J. C. Sutton has also been re-elected unopposed for the Winwick division.</p>
        <p>_ SEWING MACHINES, Hand £2 183. 6d. Treadle £4 4s. with handsoms walnut cever.—J. and W. DUTTON, 16 and 18, Sankey-at., Warrington —Advt. P.S.A. AT THE Y.M.C.A. Sunday afternoon's al eetine of the above was of the Usual character, and Mr. 11. Jones officiated in the capacity of chairman. An interesting and prac- tical address was given by Mr. F. R. Roberts,M.A., L.L.8., on a subject which might be summed lap in "Plain truths for workingmen.' Mr. W. Woods contributed in an efficient manner the solo Come ye all that are weary," and Mr. Davies gave an oboe solo which was entitled "He shall feed His ishck " (Messiah), and the finished execution of which greatly delighted the audience. Mr. F. Hickman acted as accompanist. SEWING MACHINES, Hand £2 18s. 6d. Treadle 4s. witt• handsome walnut coyer.—J. and W. DUTTON, 16 and 13 Sankey-. Warrington.— 4 dvt. EXPLOSION AT THE INFIRMARY. On Tuesday night an accident occurred at the Infirmary which occasi9ned considerable alarm. About twelve o'clock a member of the nursing staff entered the kitchen,and not being accustomed to the gas apparatus turned on the wrong tap. A loud report followed the attempt to light one of the gas jets, two or three panes in the kitchen window being broken. Some of the kitchen utensils were disarranged, but nothing serious occurred. The nurse was not injured.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON AMATEUR PHOTOGRAPHIC SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>ADJOURNED ANNUAL MEETING. The adjourned annual meeting of the Warrington Amateur Photographic Society was held on Sunday evening at the committee-room of the Museum. Mr. H. N. Houghton occupied the chair, and there was only a small attendance. The business of the meeting was to elect a committee, and Messrs. Dalton and Hesketh were appointed tellers to count the votes. The following gentlemen were elected : Messrs. C. Aylward, H. Bond, P. Dalton, T. J. Down, J. Fairhurst, J. Harding (Eustace-street), J. Hard- ing (Liverpool-road.) T. Hesketh, IL N. Houghton, G. Kirby, H. Milling, H. Pritchard, D. Stone, T. Welsby, and J. Lyon Whittle. Regret was ex- pressed that members did not come to the meetings in larger numbers, as it was most discouraging that so few had attended the annual meeting. STOVES.—GARLAND, Heating Engineer, Warrington. ST. GEORGE'S MISSION TEA. PARTY.</p>
        <p>On Monday evening the annual tea party in con- nection with the above mission took place, when about 200 sat down to an excellent tea in the mis- sion-room, which had been tastefully decorated with banners, Japanese umbrellas, screens, palms and ferns. The arrangements for the tea were carried out by Mrs. Barratt, Mrs. Amson,Miss Jack- son, and Miss Renshaw, who were assisted by a large number of other willing workers. After tea, games, dancing, &amp;c., were indulged in. Songs, readings, &amp;c., were also given at intervals by Miss Willcocks, Miss White, Mr. Jowett, Mrs. Stanfield, Mr. Banner, Mr. Bibby, and Miss Ward. GOOD TEMPLARY AT LATCHFORD. A well attended gathering of Go id Templars and friends was held in the Temperance Hall, Latch- ford, on Monday evening. The chair was occupied by Bro. H. Richardson, chief templar of the lodge. After a very encouraging address by the Chairman, the ma was proceeded with, consisting of songs, recitals, etc., the following taking part : Bro. 11. Fletcher, reading ; Bro. B. Few, recitation, "The Parson and the Clerk," and receiving a hearty encore, gave "The Cuckoo Clock "; Bros. F. Blease, J. Ditchfield, W. Owen, and Sister L. Dough sung in pleasing style. Bro. J. J. Corbett caused much amusement by his able stump speech, and well de- served the encore he so heartily received. The Juvenile Templar Choir entertained at intervals. Totes of thanks were accorded to the chairman,and especially the choirmaster and organist, Bro. A. Ford, for the able manner he conducted the sing- ing. The meeting terminated with the doxology. TOLITNTEZR. SERGEANT;' DINNER. The staff-sergeants and sergeants of the lit V.B. (South Lancashire Regiment) held their annual dinner on Saturday evening at their mess room at the Drill Hall. There were present as guests Cap- tain Buckton and Lieutenants Ridgway and Fair- dough. Letters of apology for non-attendance were received from ether officers, and Lieutenant- Colonel Ridgway especially regretted that he was not able to attend owing to indisposition. About 30 sergeants were present, including Ssrgeant- Instructor Woodin and others from the Newton Company and a substantial dinner was provided by Mr. and Mrs. Headland. After dinner the chair was taken by the president, Colour-Sergeant Wil- cox,who was supported by Sergeant-Major Sullivan asd staff. A very Pleasant smoking concert ensued during which the loyal toasts were drunk, the, toast of the evening being that of "The new commander, Lieutenant-Colonel Ridgway," which was received with great enthusiasm. Capital songs were contributed by Sergeants Connor, Taylor, Davies, Moore, Tunstall, Lieutenant Ridgway. and the Sergeant-major, who surprised the guests with his vocal powers. Mr. Walter Bellian efficiently presided at the piano. ST. EARNADAS' CHURCH TEA PARTY. On Monday the annual congregational gathering in connection with the above church was held in the schoolroom, when about 120 sat down to an excellent tea. Trays were given by Mesdames Lyon, Fran- comb, Morris, Jones. Tiddy, Parr, Allton ; Messrs. P. Stubs, R. Davies, Willis, Tinsley, Grand Clothing Hall, Walker and Sons, R. I. IV. Jones, R. T. Smith, W. D. Jeans, T. Sutton, W. B, Houghton, W. Carson, and T. Evans. The tables were presided over by Mrs. Allton, Mrs. McGillivray, Mrs. Looker, Mrs. Roscoe, Mrs. Muir, Mrs. Smith, and the Misses Wilmshurst, Lonsdale, Short, Jones, E. J. and C. Steward. After tea a conversazione was held, at which about 300 persons were present. During an interval between the tea and the conversazione the infant children gave an excellent performance of the dumb-bell drill, for which they were warmly cheered. At the meeting which was subsequently held, the Vicar (Rev. G. Allton) presided, He said he was pleased to see so many present at their annual tea party, which was always a pleasant one. tie expressed his heartiest thanks to the kind friends who had so generously helped them again bit that occasion, both in and aroun4 the town. The Sunday schools were improving and the teachers were deserving of much praise, and especially those of the day schools, who had been so willing to help forward the religious instruction of the young. The choir still did their best to render the music in creditable manner, and the services were bright an hearty. The other institutions had progresset favourably under their variou: committees. In Mr. Bryson they had an excellent lay helper, who was a constant visitor and tried to do his best among the people. An enjoyable programme of vocal and instrumental music was sustained by various mem- bers of the congregation, and dancing was after- wards begun and kept up till a late hour to the %trains of Mr. Walter Bellian's band.</p>
        <p>program</p>
        <p>PENKETH. ILAND Ulf</p>
        <p>lt.ed 1</p>
      </div>
