      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>EXAM NER, SATURDAY, MARCH 2 1895</head>
        <p>THE EXAM NER, SATURDAY, MARCH 2 1895</p>
        <p>EARLESTOWN CRICKET CLUB. ANNUAL MEETING. The annual general meeting of the members of the above club was held in the Viaduct Institute on Tuesday evening. Mr. T. Ormand presided, and there was a good attendance of members. The minutes of the last annual meeting having been read, and confirmed, the Secretary, Mr. John Owen, read the following report :—"The report which I have pleasure in presenting to you on this occasion does not suffer in comparison with those of previous years. It is true we lost one or two matches more than in 1893, but the general play did not in any way deteriorate. The fact is that as each year comes round, we find ourselves pulled against better men. Consequently it can hardly be expected that we can maintain the high averages of the past few years, and so long as we can command a majority of wins over losses there will be no necessity to feel despondent. The number of first team matches played was the same as in 1893, viz. 21, but with a slightly different result, 9 were won, 7 lest, 4 drawn and 1 resulted in a tie. Turning our attention to the aver- age runs per wicket we find that our batsmen scored 2,543 runs for 161 wickets, averaging 15.83 per wicket. This is an advance of one run per wicket, while in the same games 2,204 runs were obtained against us for 189 wickets, equal to 11.66 par wicket; in the previous year it stood at 917 which clearly proves that our failures were in bowling. We had the mis- fortune to lose the services of one or two of our leading bowlers who did the most part of the attack the previous year, and bearing this in mind, we may con- gratulate ourselves upon coming out of the ordeal so satisfactorily. The second eleven played 16 matches,won 7, and lost 5 ; here also we note a slight falling away. The individual doings of the members have been made public, and are known by all in the room, it is,therefore, I unnecessary, to give them now in detail. One or two, however, are deserving of mention. Standing high up above all the rest,we find Miller with an average of 56•5 a really capital performance, and one that was equalled by very few batsmen lathe country, last year. Dennis I also did some very useful work, the 416 runs which he made, work out to 2117 per innings. The pick of the second team were T. Clare and W. Crowther, with 33•3 and 19-11 respectively. In bowling we find F. P. Clare and R. Bell did the brunt of the work,their wickets cost- ing a shade over 9 runs each. In the second team bowl- ing W. Harrison still defies all comers,and though several players have better results for the number of wickets taken, yet considering tue amount of work Harrison has accomplished, the Committee had no difficulty in award- ing him the bowling prize. Owing to the heavy demands made upon the club funds, we decided to discontinue giving prizes to the average batsmen, but at the end of the season we were agreeably surprised to find that the Institute Committee with their usual generosity, had placed the sum of £2 at our disposal, to be distributed as your Committee deemed best, aud after carefully go- ing into the naatter, it was decided to equally divide the sum between Messrs. Bell, Husband, Crowther and T. Clare,who have to-night to receive goods to the value of 103. each. T. Clare and Harrison each receive a pair of boots for topping the bowling averages, while Dennis carries off the gold medal offered by Mr. Hamer,in addi- tion to the bat given by Mr. Attfield for competitioa threughout the district. The number of members now on the books is 103, a considerable number being active members, 3, of whom took part in last year's matches. Owing to various causes we had less cash to deaf with than as usual. In the previous balance sheet we had over £l2 profit on the ball held in the works. Last year we had no extra income to help us beyond the comic cricket match, and our ordinary gate receipts fell away about £2, principally owing to the very wet summer. The actual amount spent was £55 4s. /1.d., of this amount no less a sum than £2l B.lod. has been expended on the players in railway fares, talent money, Are., this is £6 more than all the ordinary members' subscriptions. We have every rea- son to Isok hopefully to the coming season. The ground has received careful atttention, and a considerable por- tion relaid since we saw it last. Better accommodation will we hope be given for practiceess that portion of the grousid has by no means been overlooked, we shall have the double net at work,in addition to the one for juniors and by this means we trust all will have ample opportu- nity to make themselves fit for the work expected of them. A good list of fixtures has again been managed, the new clubs being Horwich, St. Helens Recreation, and Blackley. It remains with the players to make the sea- son a success, and this can only be accomplished by each assiduously doine all in his power to uphold the reputation of the club by a careful attention to the courtesies of the gentlemanly genie of cricket, at the same time having in view the desirable end " Victory." (Applause.) Tbe report together with the balance sheet was adopted.</p>
        <p>ELECTION OF OFFICERS. The election of officers was then proceeded with, and resulted as follows :—President, Mr. J. W. Emmett, J.P. ; vice-president, Mr. F. Harwood; treasurer, Mr. T. Ormand ; secretary, Mr. J. Owen; assistant secretary, Mr. John Barr, jun. ; captain of first eleven, Mr. C. W. Emmett; vice-captain, Mr. J. Saunders; captain of second eleven, Mr. J. Ogden; rice-captain, Mr. T. Clare. The question of the composition of the Committee, was then introduced by Mr. C. W. Emmett, and he proposed a resolution to the effect, that in future the Committee be composed of nine members, instead of seven, and that the secretary, assistant secretary, and two captains, sit on the Committee in place of the vice-captains. The motion was seconded, and adopted. The following were elected as the Committee :—Messrs. T. S. Liver- sage, H. Ball, W. Huddleston, G. Bunting, James Sed- don, G. W. Slinn, G. W. Dennis, G. Husband, and T. Mitchel. Auditcns : Messrs. J. Gooding and F. J. Emmett.</p>
        <p>PRESENTATIONS. The following presentations were afterwards made for the best averages in batting and howling—let team, bowling, F. P. Clare, pair of boots ; 2nd team, bowling, W. Harrison, pair of boots. Institute prizes : R. Bell, W. H. Crowther, and T. Clare, each a pair of boots. G. Husband, bag. G. W. Dennis was also presented witls Mr. Atttield's bat, which he had won for the highest average in the district, and also Mr. Hamer's gold medal for the highest average in the first team. Mr. W. fluddleston alluded to the bowlers going unrewarded, whilst the batsmen reaped a reward for their successes, and IQ offered a bat for next season, for the best aver- age second team bowler.—Mr. F. Horsley referring to the various prizes which he had seen given out that evening, said he did not notice that the Secretary re- ceived any reward, and he thought the time had come, considering the amount of work entailed in connection with the club, that the Secretary ought to receive some remuneration for his services. Several other mem- bers spoke, and the meeting generally, agreed with th • suggestion.—The Chairman said he was in full sym- pathy with the object, and thought that undoubtedly the secretary did the greatest amount of work in con- nection with the club, but the great difficulty as far as he could see, was where they were to get the money from ; but if the members among themselves could see their way to suggest anything, he would be prepared to fall in with it if it was feasible.—The Secretary (Mr. J. Owen) did not think the time had yet arrived when a paid secretary could be engaged. He had done the work tor about 13 years gratuitously, and was prepared, now he had been re-elected, to do the work for another year, and if at any time a way could be found by which a secretary could be paid, he shculd be prepared to stand down in favour of a paid secretary. (ijear, hear.) A vote of thanks having been accorded the Chairman, the meeting dispersed.</p>
        <p>_ NOTES BY THE WAY AT EARLESI, (By AN OCCASIONAL CONTRIBUTOR). The election of a County Councillor for the next three years has been decided, and there being only one nomination, Mr. C. B. F. Borron has been de- clared unanimously elected. Evidently the elec- tors are so well satisfied with the work Mr. Borron has done for them during the past three years, that they are quite content to let him have another innings, without interference. Mr. Borten issued an address previous to the election, in which he detailed the work he had done for the township, but the only portion of it that I shall allude to is that of technical education. He tells us what efforts he has put forward in support of this par- ticular subject, and by his remarks I gather he has a particular knowledge of the question. It is a pity Mr. Borron does not impart some of this know- ledge to some of his followers on the District Council ; they seem to be terribly anxious for in- formation, and I don't think Mr. Borron could do better than give them a little private information, , and so save the time of the Council. Now that we have been made aware of the qua- lity of the speeches delivered at the recent supper given to the Newton Ratepayers' Committee we cannot feel any surprise, more especially when it is noted from whom the remarks emanated. To say that they " chuckled " would be only to use a mild term, but it is a thousand pities, whilst indulging in an overflowing of joy, that they could not steer clear Of scurrility. The feeling between the two ends of the township has never been of the best,bue such expressions as were given vent to on the oc- casion alluded to, and by a non-resident, will cer- tainly not tend to a sweetening of the existing bitterness. • --- We shall soon be in the throes of another local struggle for the position of District Counillor for Town Hall ward. Politics, however,are to be an im- portant feature in the fight, which I very much re- gret. The election will take place on March 14th, and I hope the best man for all local purposes will top the poll. • . , HOT 'WATER, HEATING.—GIRLAND, Bridge-st., War- ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>COUNTY COUNCIL ELECTION. The last date for receiving nominations for County Councillors for the Newton-in-Makerfield district was Tuesday. The only nomination hanled in was that of Mr. C. B. F. Borron the present representative, and he was declared duly elected. ST. MARY'S R.C. SCHOOL. 1 On Tuesday evening the children who took part in the eperetta recently performed in connection with the above school were entertained to tea in the schoolroom. I After tea the evening was spent in games of various kinds, the children thoroughly enjoying_ themselves. Sr. MARY'S R.C. CHURCH CHOIR. On Monday evening the choir of St. Mary's Catholic Church, Newton,and the teachers of the day school held their annual social evening, in St. Mary's Hall. A num- ber of friends were also invited,and an excellent tea was partaken of. After tea the evening was pleasantly spent in a variety of games, the Rev. F. J. Lennon,who was present, heartily entering into the enjoyment of the juniors. _</p>
        <p>GOOD TEMPLARS. The weekly session of the Hope of Earlestewn Lodge of Good Templars was held in the Co-operative Hall, on Monday evening. Bro. T. Miller C.T., presided, and there was a large attendance of members. Your candi- dates were initiated and several others proposed. The report of the District Lodge was given by Bro. G. Clarke, showing an increase of members of 119 on the quarter, and 546 on the year. The programme of the evening was 'A night with the Poets," and a number of selections were given by members, a pleasant session being passed. EARLESTOWN WESLEYAN SUNDAY SCHOOL. In connection with the Young Men's Bible class at tached to the above school, a sacred musical service was given in the lecture hall, on Sunday afternoon. After opening with a hymn and prayer, the following items were very effectively rendered, and a really en- joyable afternoon was spent. " Lord remember David," Mr. C. Atkinson ; " Oh, rest in the Lord." Miss Hill ; " Ora pro nobis," Miss E. Howarth ; " Star of Bethlehem," Mr. F. Clare ; " He was despised," Miss Eckersley ; violin duet, " Meditation," Messrs. F. Wat- mough and E. Coates; violin solo, " Intermezzio," Mr. Whatrnough. Four rnernbees.o! the Earlestown Orches- tral Society gave a very pleasing interpretation of " Comfort ye." EaRLESTOWN PRIMITIVE METHODIST SALE OF WORK. The sale of es ork held in Use Primitive Methodist schoolroom last week, was brought to a close on Satur- day evening, the result being in every way satisfactory. The sale on Friday was opened by Councillor E.T. Evans, and on Saturday by the Rev. F. R. Davies, a good member of people being present on each occasion. On Friday night solos were sung by Mrs. Brown and Miss Dring, and on Saturday evening the Earlestewn Orches- tral Society gave several selections of music in excellent style. On each evening juvenile concerts were given in the small schoolroom, arranged and conducted by Mrs. Rawlings. At the close of the sale the stalls bore a bare appearance, the amount realised being £ll5 6s. sd. 1.0.G.T. (EBENEZER LODGE). The usual weekly session of the Earlestown EkTnezer Lodge, Na 752, was held in the Co-operative Hall on Tuesday evening. The lodge was called to order at 8 o'clock, and was presided over by Bro. John Taylor, C.T. Bro. J. W. Taylor, L.D., read a circular from the Direct Veto League and a committee was appointed to arrange for a speaker in connection with the same. Bro. A. Morris, P.C.T., gave his report on the coffee supper which was held on February 12th. It showed it to have been a financial success and was adopted. Bro. J. Taylor also gave his report of the District Lodge which was adopted. Several candidates were also proposed for membership: The programme of the evening was "Topical Talks on the Order of Good Templar' " which was very interesting and instructive. SONli OF TEMPBRANCE. The Samuel Morley Section of Cadets met on Wednes- day evening in the Co-operative Nall, Earleston, at 7 o'clock. Bro. W. Gibbs,W.A., presided. Three members were admitted to the order. The attendance was very good. Recitations were given by Miss E. Hughes, E. Edwards, E. Barrett, R. Pickett, J: Pickett, reading by Bros. J. Judson and 0. Pickett. At 8 o'clock the Britains Need Division met in the same room, Bro. S. Foden, W.P., in the chair. After opening. two members were initiated. The business of the division being gone through Bro. S. Foden was responsible for the evening's programme, each member to sing, recite or pay one peun7. An enjoyable meeting wag the result. Recitations were given by some but the majority paid the fine. The proceeds were given to the Cadet Fund. NEWTON AND DISTRICT GYMNASIUM.</p>
        <p>The annual competition for the championship in con- nection with the local gymnasium, which is conducted by Mr. Brunton, took place on Wednesday, and Thurs- day evenings in the Town Hall, Newton, before large audiences. In addition to the competitions, exhibitions in rope climbing, boxing, horizontal bars, parallel bars, &amp;c., were given by Messrs. Williams and Simpson of Liverpool who also acted as judges in the competitions. For the juniors a dumb-bell race was put down, and this was run off in six heats, there being 12 entries. The final was won by W. Haynes who beat J. Grinslade by about 4 yards. The other competitions for which there were about 11 entries consisted cf durabells, barbells, vaulting, horizontal and parallel bars, rope climbing, strength, jumping, &amp;c. The first prize (gold medal and championship) was w on by F. Wage with 1333/4- marks, 2nd (silver medal) R. J. Hill, 132 ; 3rd (silver medal) N. H. Percival, 124 ; the others being, P.Percival, 118 k ; H. E. Watkins, 100 ; J. Mather, 98 : J. Hoduct, 75; Gee, 501 ; J. Lipstrott, 444 ; W. Sankey, 34. A prize was also offered by Mr. Dorman for the highest scorer in the mass exercises, and this was won by W. J. Gee. The prizes were distributed at the close by Mr. R. Barton.</p>
        <p>LOCAL SCHOLASTIC SUCCESSES. Information has just come to hand that Mr. Thomas Bradley, assistant master at the Wesleyan Day School, Earlestown, and lately of Westminster Training College, Westminster. has obtained a second year certificate in the examination for masters held in December last year. Sixty-two sat, out of which 41 obtained let class certi- ficates, 20 second class, and one third class. Mr. Brad- ley occupied the eleventh position, which is certainly a position of merit, considering the status of the college. Last year out of 17 training colleges in England, the Westminster College stood at the top of the list, and they had the highest percentage of first classes, and this year although not quite at the tap they ran pretty close. In addition to Mr. Iradley'e first class certificate he has obtained a" Ist D " certificate. We are also in- formed that Mr. James Speakman,of Park-road,Newton- le-Vi Blows, has been placed second highest on the Chester College list. ; NEWTON CONGREGATIONAL SUNDAY SCHOOL. Under the auspices of the Wigan,and District Sunday : School Mission, a lecture was delivered in the Congre- gational Schoolroom, Newton, on Monday evening, by Mr. J. A. Ball, the subject being" Life in the East," illustrative of New Testament narratives. It has now become an annual event to give three lectures at Newton, : and they are looked forward to with much interest as they are ssmething out of the ordinary run of lectures. To illustrate his subject,the lecturer had engaged several members of the congregation, who were attired to repre- sent Biblical characters, such as " Peasant and his wife, ' " The Fellah of Egypt," " Fisherman of Galilee," " Woman of Niliareth," "Shopkeeper," " Ladies in )ut- door dress," " Rich man," " Servant," &amp;c., and He characters were represented by Messrs. Latchford, S. Birks, M.Battersby, J.Howard, D. McHardy, H.Penning- ton, Master A.Thompson, Misses Christy, Tait, P. Birka, Jones, and M. Mather. The lecture was also profusely illustrated by pictures of places in the Holy Land,repre- voting outdoor his, towns and cities and indoor life. It was most interesting and was intently listened to. WARGRATE CHURCH CHOIR. The concert annually given by the choir of Wargravo Genre') took place on Tuesday evening in the Wargrave schoolroom. There was a very fair audience, and a pleasingly varied programme was submitted, Mr. E. Leyland, organist and choirmaster, acting as conductor and accompanist. The programme was opened with the glee "Now by day's retiring laarip," which was well given, as also was the subsequent item " Jeck and Jill.' Mr. George Wright gave a capital interpretation of the song "Forward," and Miss Ld wards in "Always together," Miss Corlett in "Distant voices," Miss Carson in "The Golden Harvest," were warmly appreciated. Mr. C. H. Lea was honoured with a recall for the song " Bondolero," a similar compliment being recorded Mr. , Nichols for "The White Squall." Mr. W. Kay earned an encore for his clever rendering of the song " Star of the Desert," and in the duet" Excelsior," Messrs. Nichols and Kay received a recall. Miss Roberts created a fav- ourable impression by her careful singing of "Ora Pro Nolsis," and in response to an encore she sang " The better land," with equal success. Violin solos by Mr. T. Aleock and clarionette solos by Mr. T. Jukes, were finely executed, the plaudits of the audience being hearty. The male voices were heard to advantage in "The three chafers," and "Soldiers chorus," whilst a sketch entitled "A modern Cinderella" by Misses Corlett, Edwards, Guttridge, Jones, F. Laithwaite and Mayne. was well performed. The glee "Home sweet home," arranged by Birch, sang by the choir, was an appropriatetermination to the concert. At the close the Rev. J. Carson addressed the audience, and said the visitors were entitled to their heartiest thanks for their contribution to the programme, as were the choir and Mr. Leyland who had organised the concert.</p>
        <p>-- CHURCII OF ENGLAND TEMPERANCE SOCIETY. —On Mon- day evening one of the most successful concerts of the session was held in the infant's schoolroom. The room was packed, and the excellent programme was warmly applauded by the audience. The Rev. J. R. Jones occupied the chair. The programme was as follows : A pianoforte duet by the Misses Tunstall; song, " No Beautiful Palace Have I. Miss Woodward; song," They all love Jack," Mr. Norman; bong, "Have you seen my b3w-wow," Miss Emma Foster. Miss M. Leigh and Miss Tunstall gave excellent violin solos, the latter young lady receiving a hearty encore. Reciiations were given by the Misses Annie Owen and Lily Foster. A song and chorus by the members " There's a light in the window for thee was well rendered, Miss Daisy Owen taking the solo part. A most amusing sketch "Oh Dear," or "Sarah Ann and Mary Jane," the two old ladies in lodg- ings, was depicted on the screen, Mr. W. Owen mani- pulating the magic lantern. Mrs. Owen read the con- nective reading. Misses A. Gill and J. Pierpoint, and Mrs. Norman accompanied the artistes on the piano- forte. ACCIDENT AT TUE TANNERY.—On Monday last a youth named Joseph Lee, employed in the leather cutting de- partment at the tannery, was caught by the machinery and his hand very badly crushed. Lee was taken to the Infirmary, where he received surgical aid. He is now progressing favourably. An official communication from the Colonial Office states that the rumours put into circulation by several of our contemporaries on Friday and Saturday that Sir H. Loch had been recalled are without foundation. Her Majesty's Government have the highest„ appreciation of MOTITERS. —Mrs. Winslow's Soothing Syrnp has been Sir H. Loch's services, and no idea of his recall has ever used over fifty years by millions of mothers for their children been entertained. Elia tertupf service' at, the Cape will while teething with perfect success. It will relieve the poor fferer immediately. It Is pleasant to taste ; . e produces come to an end in the course of the present year, and he natural quiet sleep. by relieving the child from pain. and the proposes to return to England on leave before that period little cherub awakes "as bright PA a button." Of all has actually run out.. • Chemists, is. lid. per b ,ttle.—Ad VVI 4'lll 1:1 ra lii</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>HAY DOCK.</head>
        <p>HAY DOCK.</p>
        <p>- - - WESLEYAN CHAPEL.—The anniversary services in con- nection with the Horne Missionary Society were held in the Wesleyan Chapel, Hay dock, an Sunday. Special sermons were preached to large congregations by the Rev. Hugh Evans, and the choir rendered appropriate hymns. On Monday evening a "Methodist Social" was held in the schoolroom, when the choir rendered several anthems and choruses. Councillor J. Jackson presided, and several addresses were given.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>DISTRICT COUNCIL VACANCY IN LIBERAL CANDIDATE FOR THE THE TOWN HALL WARD, EA.RLESTOWN. NEWTON DIVISION.</head>
        <p>THE DISTRICT COUNCIL VACANCY IN LIBERAL CANDIDATE FOR THE THE TOWN HALL WARD, EA.RLESTOWN. NEWTON DIVISION.</p>
        <p>A TRIBUTE TO THE LATE MR. RATCLIFFE. On Tuesday evening a meeting of the Liberal electors of the Town Hall Ward, was held in the Town Hall, Earlestown, to discuss the vacancies caused in the ward on the District Council, and the Board of Guardians, and to receive nominations_ for those vacancies. Coun-</p>
        <p>cillor Evans presided, and he was supported by Coun- cillor Gilmour, Messrs. P. Parr, D. Forrester,J. Latham, and G. F. Clarke. There was a fair attendance of</p>
        <p>electors.</p>
        <p>The Chairman said they all knew the melancholy and deplorable event which had called them together that evening. He referred to the death of Mr. G. Ratcliffe. The town and district had suffered a most serious loss. . .</p>
        <p>...„ It was only about two months ago since Mr. Ratcliffe , stood on that platform beside his worthy colleague Mr. Gilmour and himself, and when they came to think that he was no more with them, it was a sad blow. It showed the high esteem in which Mr. Ratcliffe was held, when they remembered that he was returned at the head of the poll ; he was also returned by a large majority as Guardian, and they were all aware that he also acted as overseer for many years. Mr. Ratcliffe, he considered, was a well tried and trusted man, and enjoyed the en- tire confidence of the ratepayers, he having been several times re-elected to the various offices which he held with credit to himself, and advantage to the inhabitants at large. Before proceeding with the other business of the meeting, he would move that their secretary convey a vote of condolence from that meeting to Mrs. Ratcliffe and the family on the serious loss which they had sus- tained.</p>
        <p>Mr. Gilmour said they little thought two months ago that they should be in such an unfortunate position, as they were that evening. Before seconding the resolution which had been so ably and so nicely put before them, be should like to say a few words, seeing, that he had had a practical knowledge of their late friend for over 30 years. He was not going to preach that Mr. Ratcliffe was perfection because no matter where they went— and particularly so with public men—no matter what they tried to do, they would always find somebody ready to pull them to pieces, and say they have some motive in doing what they were trying to do. He had worked with Mr. Ratcliffe at the bench', and whilst he was fore- man, and when he was put up as guardian and overseer. He had attended meetings at Earlestown and Newton consecutively with a view of putting Mr. Ratcliffe in as overseer for the poor, simply because he was the only man he could see in the neighbourhood, who had time to do good to the poor. If Mr. Ratcliffe could not get the means officially to do good to the poor, he had he prac- tical means of doing it out of his own poejeet. He (Mr. Gilmour) said he could also endorse all that the Chairman had said with regard to Mr. Ratcliffe's kind disposition, and his efforts,to save the ratepayers' pockets. He was not an exeravagant man, he was a man they could trust. Most of them knew him, and knew his failings, and he could tell them hon- estly, that Mr. Ratcliffe had more trouble and anxiety over his own failings than they could imagine. The greatest tribute that it was possible to pay to any public man was shown at the funeral. All classes of the community in Earlestown,Newton,Warringtonellaydock and Ashton were represented, to show some tribute of respect to Mr. Ratcliffe. He seconded the vote of con- dolence. (Applause.) Mr. D. Forrester supported the motion. Mr.Ratcliffe, he said, was undoubtedly a valuable public servant to the ward. When they met there some two months ago and gave him their hearty support, which he did not lightly value, they little thought that they should be called together so soon to mourn his loss, a loss which certainly left the District Council, the Board of Guar- dians, and the ward considerably poorer by the removal from tkeir midst of a genial and kind-hearted man. (Applause.) Mr. John Clarke, a colleague of the deceased on the Board of Guardians, did not think there was anyone' in the room knew Mr. Ratcliffe as a Guardian better than he did. As a Guardian he had done many things which the ratepayers of Earlestown had never heard about. He was a man who was always for saving the ratepayers' pockets. He (Mr. Clarke did not say that the ratepayers should be saved at the expense of the poor people, and Mr. Ratcliffe did not attempt to do that,but when there was any attempt to do things in regard to alterations of the workhouse and at great expense, he set himself in antagonism to it and it was very often put off. It was ten years that month since Mr. Ratcliffe was returned as a Guardian' and when he first went to the Board there was a scheme before them for spending something like £l5O in putting bay windows into the workhouse. That they considered unnecessary, and Mr.Ratcliffe was one of the principal causes of its being warded off, and it was not done yet. There were many other things which their late friend had been instrumentalin preventing as a waste of the ratepayers' money. (Hear, hear.) • The motion was then adopted. The Chairman said the next business of the meeting would be to endeavour to select candidates to contest the Vacant seats in the representation of that ward on District Douncil, and on the Board of Guardians. rhey had plenty of very good material to select from, and be had no doubt that the voters of the Town Hall Ward, would make an excellent choice for both offices. If their selected candidates were to be opposed, as most likely they would be, from what they heard, he trusted that co effort would be spared to secure their election, and, judging by the way everyone worked on their side at the last election, he had no doubt their nominees would be elected by a handsome majority. (Hear, hear.) He considered their side was the side of progress and reform, and in contesting that election they were fight- I ing for those principles, and he hoped they would be thoroughly united, and stand together shoulder to I shoulder. If several candidates were nominated at that meeting for either of the posts vacant, he expected they would support the one who received the majority of votes that evenieg, whenever it might be. (Applause.) Mr. H. Forrester then proposed Mr. J. W. Prbfit as candidate for the District Council, explaining that when Mr. Profit contested the last election he was unable to give him his support on the ground of his being pledged to support the three candidates who had already been chosen. He was now, however, prepared to support him. There was a qualification about Mr. Profit which ought to be studied by working-men, aod that was his close study of the labour movement,and by sending him tothe Council they should gain the advantage of the con- sideration he had given to the subject ; and he had no doubt he would do his utmost in favcur of the working- man. (Applause.) Mr. W. Clare, jun., seconded the nomination. hie be- lieved Mr. Profit Was an intelligent man, and he did not work under any employer of labour in that district. Several men from the Viaduct Works had been ap- proached to stand, but it had always been thrown in their face that they were Emmett's men, but Mr. Profit did not work under Mr. Emmett, nor Mr. Borron, and as such could say things which they who had to work under great companies, would not like to say. (Hear, hear.) Taere being no other name put forward, Mr. Profit ascended the platform, and gave an address explaining his views on the several questions before the township. The motion was then put to the meeting, and unani- mously carried. Mr. Profit said he was deeply grateful for the confid- ence they had shown in him, and he was prepared to throw himself heart and soul into the struggle prepared , for t'nene,and out of which he hoped they would come triumphantly. (Hear, hear.) The Chairman said they had now to receive nomina- tions for the Board of Guardians.—Mr. P. Parr, pro- posed the name of Miss E. L. Grcenall, and said there was one principal reason why they ought to have ladies on the Board. Just about the present time the con- tracts were giving out for the year,and it was necessary that they should have all sides en the Board, so that their money was not spent to a disadvantage, and no one better than the ladies knew when a good piece of cloth or goed food was sent to them, and they knew also the prices better than the men. (Applause.)—Miss Watson seconded, saying the was sure a lady guardian would he a great help.—Mr. Latham spoke in support of the motion, and on being put to the meeting it was unanimously carried. A vote of thanks to the Chairman, moved Mr. W. Davies and seconded by Mr. G.F.Clarke, terminated the proceedings.</p>
        <p>MEETING AT EARLESTOWN. On Saturday afternoon, in the Co-operative Hall, Earlestown, a deputation of the Newton Division Liberals met Mr. James Moon, of Liverpool, for the pur- pose of requesting him to address the Liberal Three Hundred, with the view to becoming the Liberal candi- date for the division at the next election. Mr. Moon was accompanied by Mr. Edward Evans, jun., and the deputation, which was numerous and representative, included the Rev. F. E. Miller, chairman'of the Execu- tive Committee; Messrs. G. W. Rawlins, J.P., G. F. Clarke, hon. secretary of the Earlestown District; Rev. I. H. Ridette (Haydock), Messrs. E. T. Evans, J. Rodgers, S. Harvey, W. H. Dale, W. Davies (Earles- town), L. Murphy (Newton), T. Gilmour, R. Yates (Golborne), C. E. Parker, A. G. Ward, H. Wilkinson (Penketh), J. Tither (Rainhill), W. A. Gilbeys, J. Mit- chell, W. Hume (Ashton), T. Mehers, M. Gaffney (Burtonwood). Mr. Rawlins, in introducing the deputation to Mr. Moon, said that they represented the whole of the divi- sion excepting certain agricultural polling districts where Liberalism was extremely weak or almost non- existent. The gentleman present,however, represented the backbone of the division in the populous manufac- turing and coal centres, where Liberalism was more or less strong. The object of the deputation was to have a conference with Mr. Moon on political questions, and to ask him certain questions. The representatives pre- sent stood perfectly unpledged in any sort of way. The Liberals of the division had for a long time found the battle of Liberalism uphill work, having on every occa- sion when they had contested the constituency been defeated. Their very strong desire was to have a can- didate who would stand in their interests at the next election, and who would stick to it until the division was ultimately won. (Hear, hear.) Such a candidate was not very easy to get, and they would not ask any- one unless he were willing to do that, and unless they were each and all determined to fight their hardest, and to have the division organised, so as to secure the best chances of victory. Liberalism in the Newton Division was so active and thriving that if they had a fair chance they would win the battle,but to do so they must make an immense effort. On behalf of the deputation, he wished to say that they were very pleased indeed to see .Mr Edward Evans jun., among them,and to be able to congratulate him upon the great work he had been do- ing for tbe Liberal party—(hear, hear),—and the very responsible and honourable official position which he now occupied. (Applause.) To have him present on an occasion of that kind carried with it the conviction ' that their party leaders looked to them to do something at the next general election. (applause.) Mr. Moon, having suitably expressed his thanks, said that when that meeting was first suggested to him he had, for many reasons, some hesitation in consenting. Among others, there were business reasons, but the most important reason of all was the fear that he would be unable to do justice to the task of contesting that division. To give a condensed account of his political views, he could not better express them than by saying that, although they might get a better candidate, they , could not. possibly get a much better Liberal. (Hear, I hear.) It was impossible to read the history of the ' country for the last thirty years without seeing how much Liberalism had done for the country. (Applause.) They knew it was Liberalism which was responsible for many of the great and salutary changes which had taken place during the period he had mentioned, and that they were on the eve of still greater changes (hear, hear), and that the kind of men they wanted in Parlia- ment to-day was not that kind who would vote for this thing or that, but who were in entire sympathy with 'the important changes that were sought after by the Liberal party. (Hear, hear.) He believed he was in entire sympathy with that new spirit which seemed to be moving in politics, and also with the social movement which seemed to tend towards levelling things up rather than downward. Though he had not come prepared to deal with details, he wished to say that he was in entire accord with the policy of the present Government. (Hear, hear.) That, of course, included disestablishment of ale Church in Wales, be- cause he believed that, as a great religious institution, the church would be all the better for being placed on its own merits. (Applause.) With regard to temper- ance legislation, he wished to say he was strongly in favour of that now proposed, as there could be no doubt that the evils of the licensing laws as at present existing t were very great. As to the question of the House of ' Lords, though not opposed to a second chamber, he felt very strongly that the present House of Lords was an auomaly—(hear, hear)—and that it was impossible to read the history of our times without seeing that the Lords had opposed almost every legislative privilege ' that had been proposed to be extended te the democracy, and he questioned very much if history would not re- I peat itself in that respect in the future, unless the Liberals managed to alter it by fresh legislation, and by I taking away the power of veto now possessed by the House of Lords. (Hear, hear.) He believed, how- I ever, that these things, great as they might be, were inferior in importance to the question of one 1 man ORO vote, which occupied a prominent place in the 1 Liberal programme of the day. (Hear, hear and applause.) The working man, whose bread and butter depended upon the welfare of the country, ought cer- tainly to have the same voting power as his richer neighbours ; otherwise one might as well say that a man ought to have votes in proportion to the number of thousands of pounds he possessed. Were one man one vote secured to the Liberal party,he believed they would be within measurable distance of most of the reforms which had been advocated by Liberals in the past, and some of which had been rendered abortive by the action of the House of Lords. (Hear, hear and applause.) In congratulating the Liberals on the present position of the party, Mr. Moon said that of late they had not been hearing so much as formerly from their political oppon- ents about the waning influence of the Liberals. (Laughter and applause.) Since Parliament had reas- sembled it had been one long series of victories and votes of confidence. De sincerely hoped that these would be a good omen for their meetine, and for the results of the Earlestown Division, as well as of the general success of the party. Referring to the Budget of Sir William Har- court and its results, he said that a record like that ought to encourage the Liberals of that division to he in good heart as to the prospects of the election. If the Liberals were united and presented one solid front to th • enemy, he could not conceive why they should not return a Liberal either at that or at some future election. Mr. Edward Evans, jun., expressed the pleasure it gave him to be present and to hear that, notwithstand- ing the discouragine circumstances of the past, the Liberals of Newton Division were still determined on the first opportunity to take part in what promised to be I one of the most important political fights of the present century. (Elear,hear.) He was also particularly pleased to be present because it had given him notwithstanding long acquaintance with his friend dr. Moon, the first opportunity of delivering what he might call a political address. From conversation with him at the Reform Club in Liverpool, ha know what a thoroughly good and sound Radical Mr. Moon was, and they must all agree that for a first speech ha had shown how well he could acquit himself. There was a sincere rifle in what he said, and it was evident that his opinions had been formed by conviction, and that if he came to fight the battle of ' Liberalism in Newton Division it would not be for Ithe sake of fighting for a seat, but of giving expression to views that would benefit not only Newton, but the country at large. (Applause.) He knew that if they succeeded in inducing Mr. Moon to fight that battle, it would give a great deal of rejoicing at ketdquarters ha London, where the party were anxious that the whole of the constituencies in Eng- land, whether considered somewhat hopeless or not, should be provided at the earliest possible date with a good, sound candidate. Pe had always thought that I the duty of Liberals in every division, whether they could return a candidate or not, to try to keep Liberal- ism alive in their constituencies, because one never knew what was going to happen to benefit the party. As an 'instance in point, he said it had been his fate to be the chairman of a Parliamentary division similar to that of Newton—the Wirral Division of Cheshire. Although the Liberals were defeated at the last election by 2,00 G votes, their organisation had never been stronger than at the present day, not only in numbers, but in financial resources, which were subscribed by not a few people, but by over a thousand. They were, therefore, able to have a proper registration secretary, and to carry out the work ef registration thoroughly and efficiently. One important result was that in the recent County Council elections &amp;c., although in many instances the contests were noefought strictly on party lines, they had been able to secure within two of the number returned by the Conservatives. Regarding Newton Division, he desired to impart a little secret, which was that at headquarters in London, where the divisions were carefully classed as to their election prospects, Newton-le-Willows was cer- tainly looked upon as one of the hopeful ones, provided they had a good candidate, which meant that he ought , to be a good, sound, thorough going Radical—one with 1 no trimming, but who was sound on every principle of Liberalism, and alive to the circumstances of the pre- sent day. In order to: win the division, not only the leaders, but the rank and file, must individually do their utmost to secure victory. In accentuating what Mr. Moon had said regarding the position and prospects of the party in London, Mr. Evans remarked that it seemed to him to be gradually gaining ground (applause). This must be apparent when they considered that after the election in 1892 it was predicted that the Government would not last snore than six months,and that they had actually stood the racket of nearly three years, and that as the result of the numerous by-elections which had since taken place they had lost only two Seats, which seats he did not hesitate to say would have been saved had proper management been exercised, and then they would have been exactly where they were—a result quite unique in the history of any Government of the present century. (Hear, hear.) Mr. Moon then satisfactorily answered a number of questions on various political subjects. The Rev. F. E. Miller afterwards made a formal re- quest, on behalf of the deputation that Mr. Moon should agree te deliver his views in a more detailed way before the Three Hundred than he had been Ole to do cm that occasion. •</p>
        <p>•</p>
        <p>The Rev. J. H. Riddette, in seconding, while he acknowledged the member for the division to be an honourable opponent, spoke of the party whom they had t fight as a generally unscrupulous one, whose methods often practically amounted to intimida,- tion, and said they wanted someone capable of kelpin them to make an effectual fight against it. Sir Edward Russell had been exceedingly good to them in the past, but they could not expect him to be contiaually runn- ing down to Earlestown. He trosted that Mr. Evans would send them men to assist in fighting the battle,and infusing into the Liberals of the constituency a spirit of hopefulness, Mr. Moon, in response to the invitation,said he would carefully cor.s;der it,and communicate his answer to the chairman in a few days. On the motion of Mr. Rawlins, Mr. Moon and Mr. Edward Evans, jun. were cordially thanked towards the close of the proceedings.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-03-02_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>ST. JOHN'S CHURCH, EARLESTOWN.</head>
        <p>ST. JOHN'S CHURCH, EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>-- CONGREGATIONAL TEA PARTY. On Saturday the annual congregational gathering in connection with St. John's Church, took place in the Mission Hall. The gathering has now become quite an important event in, the year's doings of the church, and hearty responses are made to the requests for subscrip- tion, this year the tray givers numbering no less than 55 members of the congregation, several of the ladies doing the honours at the tables. There were 210 to tea, which besides being well served was of a substantia character, and was much enjoyed. After tea the tables were cleared, and the room re-arranged for a concert. The Rev. E. W. Whittenbury-Kaye presided in the absence of tho Vicar, the Rev. T. R. Matthews, who was confined to his room through indisposition. The pro- gramme consisted of a number of vocal selections, and was executed in a pleasing, and efficient manner, the items and artistes being as follows :—Quartette,"HaPPY be thy dreams," Misses Barcroft and Longfield, Messrs. Monks and Mayhew ; song, " Erleen Alannah." Mr. Healey ; song, "The Dmkey Cart," Miss A. Clarke ; song, " The clang of the forge," Mr. Corless ; duet, "Come to the Coral Caves," the Misses Clarke ; song, "night of Ages" Miss Conway; song,"After Sundown," Miss Hatch ; humorous song, " I really am so sleepy," Mr. Mayhew ; song" Sunshine and rain," Miss Lewis ; song " The Island of Dreams," ; Mr. A. Barton, song " Love's own sweet song," Miss Mee ; duet, " The lesson duet," Misses Conway and Mayhew. The en- tertainment was concluded by a juvenile operetta en- titled" Princess Tiny Tot's birthday." The part of Princess was taken by Miss E. Hughes, the Major-domo being Master H. Toft. The operetta was presented a few weeks ago, and at that time we gave an outline of the subject. It was again represented under the leadership of Mrs. Latimer, and again the children performed their parts in a perfectly satisfactory manner.</p>
        <p>. .„</p>
