        <head>LYiEM.</head>
        <p>LYiEM.</p>
        <p>DEATH OF THE Rev. JAMES BF.DELL.—The death of the Rev. James Bedell took place at his residence, F 00" Cray, Lymm, on Sunday, after a week's illness. The deceased gentleman was at one time minister at Oldham - road Chapel, Manchester, and afterwards at the Congre- gational Church,where he officiated for about five year.s. Since that tinge he has lived in retirement from MIDIS' terial duties. He was attended during his illness by Dr,. Douglas Moir, of Manchester, and Dr. Williamson,.ol Lymm. Deceased was in his 70th year. A memorial service was held in the Congregational Church at. twelve o'clock on Wednesday, and the remains were interred in the Bowdon Parish Churchyard the afterno4m of the same day.</p>
        <p>DEATH OP A WELL-KNOWN TRADE/MAN.—On Sunday, the death took- place at his residence,the Mi%tone Herel, of Mr. James Fairclough. His death may be classed as somewhat sudden as deceased was in Warrington on Thursday, and on returning home in the evening coin- plained Of feeling unwell. On Ffiday he was munil worse, and was conined to his bed. Dr. Brabazon waS called in, but despite this gentleman's skill, Mr. Fair- clough died on Sunday from intunrna,tion of the lungs. The funeral took place on Tuesday at Lymm Church, amid universal signs of respect, all the shops in the village being closed during the time of the passing of the cortege. The chief mourners were Mr. George Fair dough (brother), Mr. George Rowe Fairclough, Hugh Harrison, and Dr. Kent Fairclough (nephews), an" Mr. James Holt. A number of the tradespeople also walked In procession. The coffin was of polished oak with brass mountings. A large number of Aoral tribute were received, amongst those contributing them being Mr. George Rowe Fairclough,Dr. Fairclough, George and William Harrison, Mr. and Mrs. Harrison, Mr. Jareos Holt, the servants of the Millstone Hotel, Mr. and Mrs' Brazendale, the tradespeople of Lymm, Mr. Marsh, and others. The Rev. G. W. Guest was the officiating minister. Mr. Wm. Brazendale was the undertaker, and the carriages were supplied by Mr. Wilson, of the plough Hotel.</p>
